\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Asian Summary\b0\par\par\uc2 1,986 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2010\par 08:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0620 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mixed; roller coaster for Nikkei, spends all morning more than 1% lower before rebounding after JPY falls sharply vs USD; index briefly turned positive in afternoon session after JPY's fall, before falling back, ends off 1.0%; S&P/ASX 200 down 0.6%, Kospi +0.6%, HSI +0.3%, STI +0.2%, KLCI off 0.6%, SET +0.4%, NZX-50 down 0.5%, Taiex down 0.4%, PSEi +0.3%, Sensex +0.4%. China markets shut for holidays. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, JPY falls sharply vs USD, EUR, regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  at around 0415 GMT; some senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders at major \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  banks say drop in JPY looks like \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen directly. Kyodo, citing market sources, says BOJ appears to have sold JPY. But Nikkei report says some market players doubting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , noting big JPY selling orders by banks. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda declined to comment on whether government had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; "I have no comment," Noda says; USD/JPY last at 84.94; pair rose sharply around 0415 GMT from 84.52 to 85.40 high vs 84.34 in late NY trade. EUR/JPY last at 113.36, rose in tandem with USD/JPY to 113.61 from 112.77 vs 112.32 late NY. EUR/USD at 1.3341 vs 1.3317 late NY. Earlier Friday, Japan's finance minister Noda said government will take "decisive" measures in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if necessary; "Our fundamental view is that excessive fluctuation or disorderly movement in exchange rates have a negative impact on the stability of economic and financial conditions;" comments were at news conference after regular cabinet, came before yen's sharp fall. Traders in Seoul suspect BOK of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to curb KRW's rise, trading at 4-month highs vs USD; one says BOK may have bought USD at levels below KRW1,155; USD/KRW last at 1,155.2 vs 1,161.3 late Monday in Seoul. Aussie government renews commitment to strict fiscal discipline to restore budget to surplus by year starting July 1, 2012, after unveiling narrower-than-expected deficit for last financial year. Singapore August industrial production +8.1% on-year, below +14.6% tipped in Dow Jones poll, vs +9.9% in July. Output down 6.3% on-month seasonally adjusted vs 2.3% fall forecast, revised +1.5% in July. Spot gold at $1,292.90/oz, up 40 cents from NY close. November Nymex crude oil futures down 30 cents at $74.88/bbl on Globex. (matthew.allen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0619 GMT [Dow Jones] India three-day call rates up as banks borrow to cover cash positions on RBI's reporting Friday, says dealer with state-run bank; interbank call rates on RBI's negotiated dealing system at 5.90% vs 5.00% at Thursday's close, collateralized borrowing and lending obligations or CBLO flat at 4.00% since open. Banks must report their cash positions to RBI on Friday of every fortnight. "Call rates may ease a bit from here as most banks have more or less borrowed cash in the first half of day," says dealer. Tips call rates in 5.50%-6.00% range in session, CBLO likely to be in 4.00%-5.00% range in session. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0618 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan stocks end 0.4% lower at 8166.62, volume shrinks to NT$120.49 billion; daily volume exceeded NT$130 billion over past week. Mega Securities analyst Alex Huang says investors reluctant to build new positions at current valuations, but month-end window dressing of institutional investors, potential 12.5-bps rate hike by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Sept. 30 meeting may probably give another boost to Taiex; "If \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raises key interest rate, it will still be very low, but such increase will certainly improve the earnings of insurers and banks and boost financial stocks." Tips index to trade in 8100-8250 band next week. Bottom-fishing helps TSMC (2330.TW) recover from yesterday's 3% loss, stock ends +0.2% at NT$60.10, after sliding to NT$59.60 in early session. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0608 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds likely to draw strong demand at INR110 billion debt sale later today as market sentiment lifted by government's decision (late Thursday) to double foreign investment cap to $10 billion, traders say. Cut-off yield for benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond estimated at 7.87% (last 7.85%), while cut-off for 7.99% 2017 paper likely at 7.95%, according to Dow Jones Newswires poll of 5 traders. Cut-off yield for 8.30% 2040 bond likely at 8.37%. "It's been a while since the market saw a rally that played out yesterday and early today. Bond yields may rise again in the second half of the session as traders book profits," trader with primary dealership says. Trader with state-run bank says, positive cues like investment hike and cut in second-half fiscal borrowing by INR100 billion far outnumbers negatives such as expected rate hike at RBI's November 2 review. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0542 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.1% midday at 947.85 in moderate trade; fund inflows continue, keeping sentiment positive, but sharp gains unlikely amid lack of fresh catalysts, says BFIT Securities' strategist Aphisit Limsupanark. Adds, if market stays above 945 technical support, benchmark index likely to extend gains in near term. Resistance tipped at 955-957. Among top actives, \uc2 True Corporation\uc2  (TRUE.TH) +5.4% at THB4.32, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) +1.9% at THB131.50, \uc2 Siam Cement\uc2  (SCC.TH) +0.6% at THB336, \uc2 Krung Thai Bank\uc2  (KTB.TH) off 0.6% at THB15.60, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) off 0.5% at THB98.75. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0533 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD will likely hit 1.0000 in 4Q on weak USD story, strong domestic fundamentals, says \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  Strategist Roland Randall. "The real things to watch for the Aussie dollar are what the markets are thinking about QE in the U.S. and will we get anymore signals out of the U.S." Still, even if RBA hikes in October, reckons AUD has largely priced this in. AUD/USD last at 0.9499. For longer yields, expects 10-year yield to dip further. 2020 bond yield last at 5.0602%. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0531 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0448 GMT misstated the Nikkei level and yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  reports. The correct version follows:) Nikkei off 0.1% at 9558.82, up from 9446.71 midday close, after breaking into positive territory on sharp rise in USD/JPY (now 85.12) amid rumors that Japanese government has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. If the rumored \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is true, "the timing suggests the government wants to keep the dollar above 85 yen until end-September" when the fiscal first-half ends, says Mitsushige Akino, chief fund manager at Ichiyoshi Investment Management. But says yen action talk unlikely to boost Nikkei above this week's intraday high (9704.25) unless USD/JPY can recover to 87-88 level. Market focus now turns to quarterly BOJ Tankan survey due Wednesday and U.S. consumer confidence data next week. 16/33 Topix subindexes higher, led by large-cap exporters; \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) up 2.8% at Y3,125, \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) up 1.1% at Y2,617. \uc2 Asahi Breweries\uc2  (2502.TO) up 1.7% at Y1,629 on Nikkei report it plans to take nearly 10% stake in China's top food company \uc2 Ting Hsin International Group\uc2 . (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0530 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0448 GMT misstated the Nikkei level and yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  reports. The correct version follows:) Nikkei off 0.1% at 9558.82, up from 9446.71 midday close, after breaking into positive territory on sharp rise in USD/JPY (now 85.12) amid rumors that Japanese government has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. If the rumored \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is true, "the timing suggests the government wants to keep the dollar above 85 yen until end-September" when the fiscal first-half ends, says Mitsushige Akino, chief fund manager at Ichiyoshi Investment Management. But says yen action talk unlikely to boost Nikkei above this week's intraday high (9704.25) unless USD/JPY can recover to 87-88 level. Market focus now turns to quarterly BOJ Tankan survey due Wednesday and U.S. consumer confidence data next week. 16/33 Topix subindexes higher, led by large-cap exporters; \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) up 2.8% at Y3,125, \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) up 1.1% at Y2,617. \uc2 Asahi Breweries\uc2  (2502.TO) up 1.7% at Y1,629 on Nikkei report it plans to take nearly 10% stake in China's top food company \uc2 Ting Hsin International Group\uc2 . (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0529 GMT [Dow Jones] Most important from release of official advice to Australia's new Labor government is Treasury's advice on inflation needing a coherent response, says \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  Strategist Roland Randall. "It's code for both good fiscal and monetary policy," and implies a need for tightening on both sides, not just by RBA. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0523 GMT [Dow Jones] Jump in USD/JPY came as non-Japan names, Japanese banks bought pair, say senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers at European, Japanese banks in Tokyo. Some dealers say rise shows characteristics of Japanese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its suddenness, though no such move confirmed. "There's no doubt Japanese banks were buying a lot, though it's not clear whether this was related to any government action," says senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at major European bank in Tokyo. Dealers also note pre-weekend flows had been thin today, amplifying effects of any large-scale trades. Dealer at European bank says USD/JPY may hold in 84.50-85.50 band for rest of global day vs last 85.05, down from high marked after jump at 85.40. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com) [ 09-24-10 0220ET ]\par \par \uc2 0506 GMT [Dow Jones] A spike in food prices may boost overall inflation in Asia, but likely not by enough to seriously accelerate rise in region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Corn +45%, wheat +44% since late June. But in most of Asia, overall pressures remain low. At the high end, Philippine consumer prices were up 4% in August on year, while Indonesian inflation was 6.4%. This is low by local standards: Indonesia's consumer price index was +8.5% on average in 2000-2009, Philippines averaged 5.3%. Moreover, Indonesian authorities expect CPI rises to slow this month. Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says inflation only a minor concern -- it's still in the lower part of the bank's full-year 3.5%-5.5% target range. See: "=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Food Inflation Unlikely To Boost Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0504 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore August manufacturing growth weaker-than-expected due to sharp contraction in volatile pharmaceuticals sector. Industrial production +8.1% on-year, below +14.6% in Dow Jones Newswires poll and slower than +9.9% in July. Output dropped 6.3% on-month in seasonally adjusted terms vs 2.3% fall in poll and down on revised +1.5% in July. While pharmaceutical sector is main contributor to slower growth, base effect, which has been key reason for record growth this year, is also now fading. Data support government, analysts views of continued economic moderation in coming months. (samuel.holmes@dowjones.com) [ 09-24-10 0220ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100924e69o0003i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
