\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Solid Demand Likely For India Govt Bond Auction\b0\par\par\uc2 1,651 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2010\par 08:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0608 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds likely to draw strong demand at INR110 billion debt sale later today as market sentiment lifted by government's decision (late Thursday) to double foreign investment cap to $10 billion, traders say. Cut-off yield for benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond estimated at 7.87% (last 7.85%), while cut-off for 7.99% 2017 paper likely at 7.95%, according to Dow Jones Newswires poll of 5 traders. Cut-off yield for 8.30% 2040 bond likely at 8.37%. "It's been a while since the market saw a rally that played out yesterday and early today. Bond yields may rise again in the second half of the session as traders book profits," trader with primary dealership says. Trader with state-run bank says, positive cues like investment hike and cut in second-half fiscal borrowing by INR100 billion far outnumbers negatives such as expected rate hike at RBI's November 2 review. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0542 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.1% midday at 947.85 in moderate trade; fund inflows continue, keeping sentiment positive, but sharp gains unlikely amid lack of fresh catalysts, says BFIT Securities' strategist Aphisit Limsupanark. Adds, if market stays above 945 technical support, benchmark index likely to extend gains in near term. Resistance tipped at 955-957. Among top actives, \uc2 True Corporation\uc2  (TRUE.TH) +5.4% at THB4.32, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) +1.9% at THB131.50, \uc2 Siam Cement\uc2  (SCC.TH) +0.6% at THB336, \uc2 Krung Thai Bank\uc2  (KTB.TH) off 0.6% at THB15.60, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) off 0.5% at THB98.75. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0533 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD will likely hit 1.0000 in 4Q on weak USD story, strong domestic fundamentals, says \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  Strategist Roland Randall. "The real things to watch for the Aussie dollar are what the markets are thinking about QE in the U.S. and will we get anymore signals out of the U.S." Still, even if RBA hikes in October, reckons AUD has largely priced this in. AUD/USD last at 0.9499. For longer yields, expects 10-year yield to dip further. 2020 bond yield last at 5.0602%. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0531 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0448 GMT misstated the Nikkei level and yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  reports. The correct version follows:) Nikkei off 0.1% at 9558.82, up from 9446.71 midday close, after breaking into positive territory on sharp rise in USD/JPY (now 85.12) amid rumors that Japanese government has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. If the rumored \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is true, "the timing suggests the government wants to keep the dollar above 85 yen until end-September" when the fiscal first-half ends, says Mitsushige Akino, chief fund manager at Ichiyoshi Investment Management. But says yen action talk unlikely to boost Nikkei above this week's intraday high (9704.25) unless USD/JPY can recover to 87-88 level. Market focus now turns to quarterly BOJ Tankan survey due Wednesday and U.S. consumer confidence data next week. 16/33 Topix subindexes higher, led by large-cap exporters; \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) up 2.8% at Y3,125, \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) up 1.1% at Y2,617. \uc2 Asahi Breweries\uc2  (2502.TO) up 1.7% at Y1,629 on Nikkei report it plans to take nearly 10% stake in China's top food company \uc2 Ting Hsin International Group\uc2 . (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0530 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0448 GMT misstated the Nikkei level and yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  reports. The correct version follows:) Nikkei off 0.1% at 9558.82, up from 9446.71 midday close, after breaking into positive territory on sharp rise in USD/JPY (now 85.12) amid rumors that Japanese government has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. If the rumored \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is true, "the timing suggests the government wants to keep the dollar above 85 yen until end-September" when the fiscal first-half ends, says Mitsushige Akino, chief fund manager at Ichiyoshi Investment Management. But says yen action talk unlikely to boost Nikkei above this week's intraday high (9704.25) unless USD/JPY can recover to 87-88 level. Market focus now turns to quarterly BOJ Tankan survey due Wednesday and U.S. consumer confidence data next week. 16/33 Topix subindexes higher, led by large-cap exporters; \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) up 2.8% at Y3,125, \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) up 1.1% at Y2,617. \uc2 Asahi Breweries\uc2  (2502.TO) up 1.7% at Y1,629 on Nikkei report it plans to take nearly 10% stake in China's top food company \uc2 Ting Hsin International Group\uc2 . (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0529 GMT [Dow Jones] Most important from release of official advice to Australia's new Labor government is Treasury's advice on inflation needing a coherent response, says \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  Strategist Roland Randall. "It's code for both good fiscal and monetary policy," and implies a need for tightening on both sides, not just by RBA. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0523 GMT [Dow Jones] Jump in USD/JPY came as non-Japan names, Japanese banks bought pair, say senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers at European, Japanese banks in Tokyo. Some dealers say rise shows characteristics of Japanese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its suddenness, though no such move confirmed. "There's no doubt Japanese banks were buying a lot, though it's not clear whether this was related to any government action," says senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at major European bank in Tokyo. Dealers also note pre-weekend flows had been thin today, amplifying effects of any large-scale trades. Dealer at European bank says USD/JPY may hold in 84.50-85.50 band for rest of global day vs last 85.05, down from high marked after jump at 85.40. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0506 GMT [Dow Jones] A spike in food prices may boost overall inflation in Asia, but likely not by enough to seriously accelerate rise in region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Corn +45%, wheat +44% since late June. But in most of Asia, overall pressures remain low. At the high end, Philippine consumer prices were up 4% in August on year, while Indonesian inflation was 6.4%. This is low by local standards: Indonesia's consumer price index was +8.5% on average in 2000-2009, Philippines averaged 5.3%. Moreover, Indonesian authorities expect CPI rises to slow this month. Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says inflation only a minor concern -- it's still in the lower part of the bank's full-year 3.5%-5.5% target range. See: "=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Food Inflation Unlikely To Boost Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0504 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore August manufacturing growth weaker-than-expected due to sharp contraction in volatile pharmaceuticals sector. Industrial production +8.1% on-year, below +14.6% in Dow Jones Newswires poll and slower than +9.9% in July. Output dropped 6.3% on-month in seasonally adjusted terms vs 2.3% fall in poll and down on revised +1.5% in July. While pharmaceutical sector is main contributor to slower growth, base effect, which has been key reason for record growth this year, is also now fading. Data support government, analysts views of continued economic moderation in coming months. (samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0503 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD largely rangebound, last 0.7283 vs 0.7299 early; "The Kiwi is not moving a great deal today. It is underperforming the Aussie. I think the GDP report yesterday is still weighing on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says RBS \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Greg Gibbs. Tips support at 0.7260, resistance at 0.7300. Says some data overnight, like U.S. durable goods orders, may influence direction; "The Kiwi, broadly speaking, is following the direction of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." NZD/JPY meanwhile rises sharply amid unconfirmed reports of apparent MOF/BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in yen; last 62.045 vs 61.955. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0458 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY sharply higher as players see chances of another round of MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  increasing; if today's move results in NY close above 55-day moving average at 85.64 it may signal change in trend for pair, says Dow Jones CommentaryPlus analyst Mark Cranfield. Even during last week's heavy dollar buying USD/JPY failed to have daily close above closely watched 55-day average; which pair has been trading below since it dropped under 92.0 in April. If today's close is above 85.64 it could trigger new wave of bulls into USD/JPY in week ahead. (mark.cranfield@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0457 GMT [Dow Jones] JPY falls sharply vs USD, EUR, others, with USD/JPY jumping from around 84.50 to intraday high of 85.40, EUR/JPY going from around 112.95 to 113.75. Some senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders at major \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  banks say drop in yen looks like \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which Tokyo conducted last Wednesday for first time in more than six years; "the price action is very specific and strange" though no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been seen directly, says Minoru Shioiri, chief manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. Kyodo, citing market sources, says BOJ appears to have sold yen. But one trader at major Japanese bank says "I think this is just the market over-reacting to some large-scale buying in dollar/yen by non-Japanese players, and I think you'll see dollar/yen come back down." Nikkei also says some market players are doubting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , noting big yen selling orders by banks.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com) [ 09-24-10 0208ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100924e69o0003a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
