urns to quarterly BOJ Tankan survey due Wednesday and U.S. consumer confidence data next week. 16/33 Topix subindexes higher, led by large-cap exporters; \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO) up 2.8% at Y3,125, \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) up 1.1% at Y2,617. \uc2 Asahi Breweries\uc2  (2502.TO) up 1.7% at Y1,629 on Nikkei report it plans to take nearly 10% stake in China's top food company \uc2 Ting Hsin International Group\uc2 . (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0529 GMT [Dow Jones] Most important from release of official advice to Australia's new Labor government is Treasury's advice on inflation needing a coherent response, says \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  Strategist Roland Randall. "It's code for both good fiscal and monetary policy," and implies a need for tightening on both sides, not just by RBA. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0523 GMT [Dow Jones] Jump in USD/JPY came as non-Japan names, Japanese banks bought pair, say senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers at European, Japanese banks in Tokyo. Some dealers say rise shows characteristics of Japanese government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its suddenness, though no such move confirmed. "There's no doubt Japanese banks were buying a lot, though it's not clear whether this was related to any government action," says senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at major European bank in Tokyo. Dealers also note pre-weekend flows had been thin today, amplifying effects of any large-scale trades. Dealer at European bank says USD/JPY may hold in 84.50-85.50 band for rest of global day vs last 85.05, down from high marked after jump at 85.40. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0506 GMT [Dow Jones] A spike in food prices may boost overall inflation in Asia, but likely not by enough to seriously accelerate rise in region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Corn +45%, wheat +44% since late June. But in most of Asia, overall pressures remain low. At the high end, Philippine consumer prices were up 4% in August on year, while Indonesian inflation was 6.4%. This is low by local standards: Indonesia's consumer price index was +8.5% on average in 2000-2009, Philippines averaged 5.3%. Moreover, Indonesian authorities expect CPI rises to slow this month. Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says inflation only a minor concern -- it's still in the lower part of the bank's full-year 3.5%-5.5% target range. See: "=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Food Inflation Unlikely To Boost Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0504 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore August manufacturing growth weaker-than-expected due to sharp contraction in volatile pharmaceuticals sector. Industrial production +8.1% on-year, below +14.6% in Dow Jones Newswires poll and slower than +9.9% in July. Output dropped 6.3% on-month in seasonally adjusted terms vs 2.3% fall in poll and down on revised +1.5% in July. While pharmaceutical sector is main contributor to slower growth, base effect, which has been key reason for record growth this year, is also now fading. Data support government, analysts views of continued economic moderation in coming months. (samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0503 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD largely rangebound, last 0.7283 vs 0.7299 early; "The Kiwi is not moving a great deal today. It is underperforming the Aussie. I think the GDP report yesterday is still weighing on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says RBS \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Greg Gibbs. Tips support at 0.7260, resistance at 0.7300. Says some data overnight, like U.S. durable goods orders, may influence direction; "The Kiwi, broadly speaking, is following the direction of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." NZD/JPY meanwhile rises sharply amid unconfirmed reports of apparent MOF/BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in yen; last 62.045 vs 61.955. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0458 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY sharply higher as players see chances of another round of MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  increasing; if today's move results in NY close above 55-day moving average at 85.64 it may signal change in trend for pair, says Dow Jones CommentaryPlus analyst Mark Cranfield. Even during last week's heavy dollar buying USD/JPY failed to have daily close above closely watched 55-day average; which pair has been trading below since it dropped under 92.0 in April. If today's close is above 85.64 it could trigger new wave of bulls into USD/JPY in week ahead. (mark.cranfield@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0457 GMT [Dow Jones] JPY falls sharply vs USD, EUR, others, with USD/JPY jumping from around 84.50 to intraday high of 85.40, EUR/JPY going from around 112.95 to 113.75. Some senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders at major \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  banks say drop in yen looks like \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which Tokyo conducted last Wednesday for first time in more than six years; "the price action is very specific and strange" though no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been seen directly, says Minoru Shioiri, chief manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. Kyodo, citing market sources, says BOJ appears to have sold yen. But one trader at major Japanese bank says "I think this is just the market over-reacting to some large-scale buying in dollar/yen by non-Japanese players, and I think you'll see dollar/yen come back down." Nikkei also says some market players are doubting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , noting big yen selling orders by banks.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0456 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares +1.6% at 3388.777 midday in moderate volume, as foreign funds buying most bank, commodity-related shares, traders say; tip resistance at 3400. "Some fund managers are seen starting buying shares to shape up their portfolios by the end of September," says trader at Erdhika Securities. Tin miner Timah (TINS.JK) up 3.7% at IDR2,825, Bank Negara (BBNI.JK) up 2.9% at IDR3,575, while BCA (BBCA.JK) up 3.9% at IDR6,650. Foreigners net buyers of IDR288 billion worth of local shares. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com) [ 09-24-10 0143ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100924e69o00037\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
