senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at major European bank in Tokyo. Dealers also note pre-weekend flows had been thin today, amplifying effects of any large-scale trades. Dealer at European bank says USD/JPY may hold in 84.50-85.50 band for rest of global day vs last 85.05, down from high marked after jump at 85.40. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0506 GMT [Dow Jones] A spike in food prices may boost overall inflation in Asia, but likely not by enough to seriously accelerate rise in region's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Corn +45%, wheat +44% since late June. But in most of Asia, overall pressures remain low. At the high end, Philippine consumer prices were up 4% in August on year, while Indonesian inflation was 6.4%. This is low by local standards: Indonesia's consumer price index was +8.5% on average in 2000-2009, Philippines averaged 5.3%. Moreover, Indonesian authorities expect CPI rises to slow this month. Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  says inflation only a minor concern -- it's still in the lower part of the bank's full-year 3.5%-5.5% target range. See: "=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Food Inflation Unlikely To Boost Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0504 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore August manufacturing growth weaker-than-expected due to sharp contraction in volatile pharmaceuticals sector. Industrial production +8.1% on-year, below +14.6% in Dow Jones Newswires poll and slower than +9.9% in July. Output dropped 6.3% on-month in seasonally adjusted terms vs 2.3% fall in poll and down on revised +1.5% in July. While pharmaceutical sector is main contributor to slower growth, base effect, which has been key reason for record growth this year, is also now fading. Data support government, analysts views of continued economic moderation in coming months. (samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0503 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD largely rangebound, last 0.7283 vs 0.7299 early; "The Kiwi is not moving a great deal today. It is underperforming the Aussie. I think the GDP report yesterday is still weighing on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says RBS \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Greg Gibbs. Tips support at 0.7260, resistance at 0.7300. Says some data overnight, like U.S. durable goods orders, may influence direction; "The Kiwi, broadly speaking, is following the direction of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." NZD/JPY meanwhile rises sharply amid unconfirmed reports of apparent MOF/BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in yen; last 62.045 vs 61.955. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0458 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY sharply higher as players see chances of another round of MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  increasing; if today's move results in NY close above 55-day moving average at 85.64 it may signal change in trend for pair, says Dow Jones CommentaryPlus analyst Mark Cranfield. Even during last week's heavy dollar buying USD/JPY failed to have daily close above closely watched 55-day average; which pair has been trading below since it dropped under 92.0 in April. If today's close is above 85.64 it could trigger new wave of bulls into USD/JPY in week ahead. (mark.cranfield@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0457 GMT [Dow Jones] JPY falls sharply vs USD, EUR, others, with USD/JPY jumping from around 84.50 to intraday high of 85.40, EUR/JPY going from around 112.95 to 113.75. Some senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders at major \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  banks say drop in yen looks like \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which Tokyo conducted last Wednesday for first time in more than six years; "the price action is very specific and strange" though no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been seen directly, says Minoru Shioiri, chief manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. Kyodo, citing market sources, says BOJ appears to have sold yen. But one trader at major Japanese bank says "I think this is just the market over-reacting to some large-scale buying in dollar/yen by non-Japanese players, and I think you'll see dollar/yen come back down." Nikkei also says some market players are doubting \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , noting big yen selling orders by banks.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0456 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares +1.6% at 3388.777 midday in moderate volume, as foreign funds buying most bank, commodity-related shares, traders say; tip resistance at 3400. "Some fund managers are seen starting buying shares to shape up their portfolios by the end of September," says trader at Erdhika Securities. Tin miner Timah (TINS.JK) up 3.7% at IDR2,825, Bank Negara (BBNI.JK) up 2.9% at IDR3,575, while BCA (BBCA.JK) up 3.9% at IDR6,650. Foreigners net buyers of IDR288 billion worth of local shares. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0450 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW trims losses to 1,158.2 from 1,156.2 midday, vs 1,161.3 late Monday in Seoul on sharp spike in USD/JPY (local markets were closed for 3 days for public holiday); Last week, massive JYP-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities weighed on KRW as it heightened concerns it may prompt other Asian countries, including Korea, to act more aggressively to curb local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains. "But I don't see any (BOK) smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 (yet). And I don't expect the pair to move much for the rest of the session, with the Chinese market shut for holiday and offshore players not making big moves," says local bank trader. Tips USD/KRW in tight 1,155-1,160 range for rest of day. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0450 GMT [Dow Jones] Share price movements in Singapore generally limited as interest wanes after recent buying spree. STI +0.2% at 3089.37 midday vs down 0.5% at session low of 3069.15. Resistance expected at 52-week high of 3116, with 3043 August peak as support. "We are inclined to believe that the STI is going to hit a consolidation (phase)," says SIAS Research technical analyst Edmund Seow; "we caution all investors and traders against taking big long positions and wait for markets to rebound and form a base first before re-entering." Market breadth tad negative with overall volume modest at 1.27 billion shares worth S$972 million. Olam (O32.SG) remains best percentage gainer among STI components, +9.2% at S$3.22 on expectations of merger with Louis Dreyfus Commodities. Most active stocks in market include China New Town (D4N.SG), +10.0% at S$0.165, Genting Singapore (G13.SG), off 1.9% at S$2.03, JES International (EG0.SG), +5.8% at S$0.365. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com) [ 09-24-10 0131ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100924e69o00035\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
