\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/INR Down; Strong Support At 45.50 - Trader\b0\par\par\uc2 1,440 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2010\par 05:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0357 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR tad lower as custodial banks sell on expectations of increase in capital inflows after government hikes limit on foreign investment in bonds Thursday, says state-run bank trader. Pair at 45.59 after falling to 45.50 early session, 45.65 late Thursday. Trader says, pair facing strong support at 45.50 as state-run banks continue to buy while local stocks flat to mildly negative. Adds, gold importers have been buying in past few sessions and pair may rise once oil companies enter the fray. Tips pair in 45.40-45.70 band in session. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0349 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares down 0.1% at 450.51, with retailers selling amid lack of fresh leads; but traders say some institutions buying on hopes of strong 3Q corporate earnings after some companies posted solid August results earlier this week. Still, "many investors are staying on the sidelines as they wait for the government to announce new financial measures to help stabilize the market in 4Q," says trader. Notes key index lost 9% year to date. Volume thin with 27.5 million shares valued at VND695 billion traded. Among decliners, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) down 2.6% at VND33,300, Itaco (ITA.VH) down 1.7% at VND18,400, \uc2 PetroVietnam Finance\uc2  (PVF.VH) down 1.5% at VND20,400. Tips immediate support at 450. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0346 GMT [Dow Jones] India sovereign bonds sharply higher after government doubles foreign investment limit in government debt to $10 billion, says dealer with state-run bank; 7.80% 2020 benchmark bond at 99.70 vs 99.35 at Thursday's close. "Markets had jumped last evening as the news came in minutes before it closed for trading. It is extending gains today. Also, with the October-March government borrowing reduced by INR100 billion, it is double the cheer," says dealer. Dealer tips 2020 bond yields in 7.82%-7.85% range vs last 7.84%. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0338 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options edge up to 10.50%/11.20% from 10.40%/11.10% New York Thursday, as investors buy USD-call/JPY-put contracts. Options dealer at major Japanese bank says investors who had sold hedges (which protected against sharp USD/JPY rise) in days after Tokyo's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when USD/JPY fell, are now buying hedges again on growing view authorities will continue \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken JPY. Kyodo reports that U.S. President Obama, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Prime Minister Kan, didn't mention \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at their meeting, citing Japanese government official. Options dealer says no comment from Obama kept spot biased higher since it implies U.S. not actively opposing further yen selling/dollar buying by Japan. Says, however, that if spot stays at current levels, vols may decline by 10-20 bps later in global day. Elsewhere, single player buys 6-day USD-put/JPY-call contracts at strike price of 83.50 with implied vols of 10.95% for unknown amount, dealer says.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0329 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex likely to open slightly lower, tracking losses on Wall Street, most regional markets. "We expect the market to open slightly lower, and trade in a narrow range as foreign funds and local investors are going slow after recent run-up," says local trader; adds, bank shares may continue to underperform after recent rally. Sensex ended down 0.4% at 19,861.01 Thursday, tipped in 19,650-20,050 range today. (raghavendra.upadhyaya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0327 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds creep higher in active trade, taking cues from gains in U.S. Treasurys. Both foreign, local players spotted buying medium-, long-term tenors, but profit-taking likely to cap upside. Still, "investors think that Thai government bonds' yields are likely to fall further after the recent decline in U.S. Treasurys," says bond dealer; notes U.S. Treasurys have gained for 5 straight days as investors convinced Fed will launch new round of quantitative easing. Yields expected to stay rangebound. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.72%/2.68% vs 2.74%/2.72% late yesterday, November 2016 at 2.82%/2.75% vs 2.87%/2.80%, June 2019 at 3.03%/3.00% vs 3.06%/3.04%. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0322 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares last +0.6% at 952.42 in active trade on expected continued fund inflows. Telecom plays rebound but uncertainty remains after deferral of 3G licensing auction. "Fundamentally, nothing has changed much. So, investors should increasingly be cautious about the rise as the market is largely driven by capital inflows," says Asia Plus Securities SVP Therdsak Thaveethiratham. Resistance tipped at 960, support 944. Among top actives, \uc2 True Corp\uc2 . (TRUE.TH) +6.8% at THB4.38, \uc2 Krung Thai Bank\uc2  (KTB.TH) +1.3% at THB15.90, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) also +1.3% at THB100.50, PTT (PTT.TH) +0.3% at THB292, Banpu (BANPU.TH) +0.3% at THB698. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0315 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR likely lower on expectations of capital inflows after India's federal government Thursday doubled foreign investment cap in government bonds to $10 billion, and upped cap in corporate bonds to $20 billion from $15 previously, says foreign bank trader. Pair may open around 45.55 vs 45.65 late Thursday. "There is a possibility that the pair may slide to as low as 44.00 by December on capital inflows in not just debt but also equities as more share sales are expected, but India's current account deficit may limit some of the dollar's fall," trader says. Tips pair in 45.40-45.70 band in session. Foreign funds were net buyers of provisional INR5.34 billion worth of Indian stocks on Thursday, 1-month NDF quoting at 45.66/45.69. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0312 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan importers buying good amounts of USD/JPY from below 84.60 in past 5 minutes, lifting pair as high as 84.66, says Singapore-based dealer. USD/JPY last 84.64. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0309 GMT [Dow Jones] India bonds likely to rally on government's decision to double foreign investment in government debt, October-March borrowing cut by INR100 billion, says dealer with state-run bank; 7.80% 2020 benchmark bond could trade 7.80%-7.85% range in session vs 7.90% Thursday's close. "Increasing the cap on FII (foreign institutional investors) investments was long awaited, but we have our doubts as the decision allows them to only invest the additional amount in long term papers," says dealer. Adds, most foreign funds currently invested in short-term bonds of 1-, 2-year. India Thursday evening doubled investment cap of foreign institutions in government bonds to $10 billion, with incremental $5 billion to be invested in bonds of residual maturity of 5 years and above. It also cut by INR100 billion its fiscal 2H borrowing plan to INR1.63 trillion. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0257 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares +1.0% at 3370.769 in moderate volume, with banks, commodity-related stocks leading gains on bargain-buying, traders say; tip resistance at 3380. "Foreign funds are supporting the market which had fallen in the previous three sessions," says trader at Valbury Securities. Coal miner Bukit Asam (PTBA.JK) +2.6% at IDR19,900, Bank Negara (BBNI.JK) +2.2% at IDR3,550, while BCA (BBCA.JK) +3.9% at IDR6,650. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0237 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW sustains decline, last at 1,156.80 vs 1,161.30 late Monday in Seoul, on broadly weaker USD over 3-day local holiday, but further downside limited on corporate USD demand on dips after pair slips to 4-month low earlier, also players increasingly wary over possible USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders say. Still, "it looks difficult for the pair to recover the 1,160 level today...I expect the pair to continue seesawing in the 1,150s region," says foreign bank trader; adds offshore players not providing much momentum for KRW so far today. Local bank trader notes trading volume remains thin ahead of weekend. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com) [ 09-23-10 2357ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100924e69o00025\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
