\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: Yen Slides Briefly But Authorities Mum On Potential Role\b0\par\par\uc2 632 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2010\par 12:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds further comments on potential role of the government in market's movement.)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By William Sposato \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The yen staged a sudden and short-lived price tumble in Asian trading Friday, but traders were at a loss to say if Japanese authorities had staged a "sneak attack" on the market or if jittery \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders had jumped the gun.\par \par \uc2 Adding to the mystery was a refusal by Japanese officials from Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda on down to say one way or the other if they were responsible.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market has been on edge ever since Sept. 15, when Japan poured in yen-selling orders totalling an estimated Y1.8 trillion to try and break the cycle of a strengthening local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Officials have become increasingly worried that the strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would harm the big exporters vital to Japan's economy.\par \par \uc2 On Friday, amid a quiet Japan market in the early afternoon, the dollar jumped nearly a full yen from Y84.50 to Y85.40.\par \par \uc2 "The price action is very specific and strange," bearing the marks of government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Minoru Shioiri, chief manager of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities.\par \par \uc2 But Shioiri and other traders said they had not seen any direct signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "There's no doubt Japanese banks were buying a lot (of dollars against the yen), though it's not clear whether this was related to any government action," said a senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at a major European bank in Tokyo. "I'd say the odds were about fifty/fifty it was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The affect was short-lived. After the initial spike, the dollar trended lower again and was back where it started in late Asian trading at Y84.50.\par \par \uc2 If in fact there had been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it represents a sharp change in tactics by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 When Japan entered the market on Sept. 15, it was eager to make sure everyone knew. The trading was immediately visible through the EBS inter-bank trading network and the action was quickly confirmed by Finance Minister Noda in a hastily called news conference.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has said it would continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if necessary, and traders have been watching carefully to see if the authorities have a preferred range in mind, or whether they will seize on market opportunities to continue to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 In the end, part of their strategy is to keep the market guessing about their intentions since uncertainty will make traders reluctant to bet heavily on a stronger yen.\par \par \uc2 Some analysts suspect that Japan is mainly concerned with stopping the yen's rise, while others say that the authorities want to push it still lower. While the yen has pulled back from 15-year highs against the dollar, industrial leaders have said it is still too strong.\par \par \uc2 Suzuki Motor Corp\uc2 . Chairman and President \uc2 Osamu Suzuki\uc2  recently said that having the dollar around Y95 would be good for keeping Japanese manufacturers globally competitive.\par \par \uc2 Whether or not the Japanese government was behind the fall in the yen Friday, it remains clear that the administration of Prime Minister Naoto Kan remains deeply concerned over the damage the strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  exerts on the export-driven economy.\par \par \uc2 Speaking at a press conference Friday morning in Tokyo, before the price rise, Noda reiterated that Japan would take "decisive" steps to curb yen strength if necessary.\par \par \uc2 At a separate press conference, Economy Minister Banri Kaieda cited the strong yen--as well as Japan's recent disputes with China--as contributing to what he sees as risks to the economy.\par \par \uc2 -By William Sposato, Dow Jones Newswires; 81-3-6269-2785; william.sposato@dowjones.com [ 24-09-10 1031GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100924e69o000or\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
