\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Euro Up On Ifo; Yen Recovers Despite BOJ Fears\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2010\par 11:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--The euro got a sharp lift Friday from a slightly stronger-than-expected Ifo survey from Germany.\par \par \uc2 The Ifo helped to offset other recent negative data from the euro zone and gave the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  support at a time when the dollar is still suffering from speculation of increased quantitative easing in the U.S.\par \par \uc2 The euro's move came after a sharp spike in the dollar against the yen that raised fears that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had returned to the market with more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Last week, the bank sold about Y2 trillion in an effort to drive the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower.\par \par \uc2 However, Japanese officials declined to confirm Friday's action one way or another and the market was left assuming that the move was a false alarm.\par \par \uc2 Investors are particularly nervous about more Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as U.S. President Barak Obama failed to take an opportunity to criticize last week's exercise when he met with Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan in New York Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Reports suggest that \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  weren't on the agenda at their meeting.\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, a sharp rally in the dollar earlier Friday after it declined to about Y84.55 immediately triggered speculation that the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was back in the market. This was just above the Y84.26 low for the dollar after last week's major \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  exercise.\par \par \uc2 See how the dollar spiked against the yen on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4628"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4628}}}\par \par \uc2 Sentiment towards the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still being dictated largely by the suggestion from the U.S. Federal Reserve earlier this week that it may still need to increase the amount of bonds it buys to pump more liquidity into the system.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said this negative mood for the dollar is likely to prevail unless new U.S. data point to a stronger-than-expected upturn in the U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, U.S. relations with China are also contributing to dollar uncertainty as the market waits to see how the Peoples' Bank of China reacts to more pressure from Washington for China to let the yuan rise. U.S. President Barak Obama increased pressure for Beijing to stop manipulating its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in his talks with Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao also Thursday in New York.\par \par \uc2 However, with China still on holiday, the markets will have to wait until Monday to see if the PBOC in any way accelerates the yuan's recent slow rise against the dollar.\par \par \uc2 The euro made its gains after the Ifo survey showed that both business sentiment as well as economic expectations were a little stronger than the market had anticipated.\par \par \uc2 This helped to reverse some of the recent fears that the euro-zone economy, and Germany in particular, is slowing even more quickly than expected.\par \par \uc2 By midmorning, the euro had managed to rally all the way to $1.3387 from $1.3317 late Thursday in New York, according to EBS.\par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also up at Y113.25 from Y112.32, while the dollar rose to Y84.59 from Y84.34.\par \par \uc2 The dollar also fell to CHF0.9835 from CHF0.9856 while the pound was nearly flat at $1.5688 compared with $1.5683.\par \par \uc2 -By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9493; nick.hastings@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 24-09-10 0921GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1035 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100924e69o000m7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
