\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:NZ Shares Down 0.2%; Drifting On Dearth Of Drivers\b0\par\par\uc2 1,462 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 September 2010\par 01:47\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2347 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares down 0.2% to 3221.221, drifting in tepid trading; "There is not really too much that is newsworthy out there. It is a very, very quiet start to the day. The market is just a bit weaker following through from the weaker close in international markets overnight," says First NZ Capital senior broker James Snell. Says that given Australia's corporate diary looks fairly light for day, market may remain around these levels for most of session. Fletcher Building (FBU.NZ) down 0.6% at NZ$8.57 on profit taking after recent strong run. Kathmandu (KMD.NZ) +0.6% at NZ$1.78 after FY result in line with company guidance given last month. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2344 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi may consolidate due to lack of major cues amid expectations for reduced trading volume after long Chuseok holidays lasting from Tuesday to Thursday. "The foreign stock exchanges showed no major moves during the holidays, so we may see the index hover around Monday's close (of 1832.63)," says Taurus Investment & Securities analyst Oh Tae-dong. Tips 1825-1838 range for day, notes market may hold off on taking major moves ahead of global economic data next week as month of September comes to a close. \uc2 Hyundai Engineering\uc2  (000720.SE) may rise on M&A hopes after creditors officially launch sale of their 34.88% stake in company, while \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) may rise after opening $500 million manufacturing plant in Russia. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2343 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may again test post-\b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  low of 84.26 hit Thursday in NY trading in absence of any sign of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and if Nikkei drops after declines in U.S. shares overnight, says Naoto Taikoji, senior dealer at \uc2 Shinkin \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Adds \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be unwilling to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  just as PM Kan meeting with U.S. President Obama in NY. Pair last 84.53. EUR/JPY biased down on broad JPY strength, may trade in 112.20-113.00 band vs last 112.55. Adds any further falls may be limited as market has already priced in EUR weakness due to new doubts on health of European economy. Says EUR/USD may trade in 1.3300-1.3360 band vs last 1.3318. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2335 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW likely to fall, possibly targeting a breach of key 1,160 support vs 1,161.3 late Monday in Seoul, playing catch-up on broad USD weakness seen earlier this week during Chuseok holidays, traders say. Domestic markets reopen today after being shut for 3 days until yesterday. "Hints of possible quantitative easing measures from the Fed pushed the euro above the $1.3400 mark (earlier this week)," and offshore players likely to continue making bullish bets for Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says local bank trader. Adds, amount of corporate USD demand plus possible USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  at levels below 1,160 will likely be key for session. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2334 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 9 foreign brokerages place net sell orders for 800,000 Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 1st day of premarket selling in 9 sessions may be negative for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Sell orders total 13.9 million shares, with buy orders amounting to 13.1 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2333 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 6.1% at HK$3.632 billion Wednesday, as percentage of total market volume at 5.3% vs Tuesday's 5.5%, indicating bears remained deterred by HSI rally, with index up 9 of last 10 sessions. Cheung Kong (0001.HK) topped list with HK$226.77 million, followed by unit \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) with HK$194.11 million--duo have outperformed recently. Next came \uc2 China Unicom\uc2  (0762.HK) with HK$177.13 million, Tencent (0700.HK) with HK$141.57 million, A50 China Tracker (2823.HK) with HK$122.69 million. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2331 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex crude likely to extend its consolidation near-term, Dow Jones technical analysis shows, as daily continuation chart remains mixed: MACD flat, stochastics neutral; meanwhile, 5-day & 15-day moving averages meandering sideways. Support at $73.58 (yesterday's low); breach would expose downside to $72.75 (Sept. 17 low), then $72.63 (Sept. 7 low), $71.53 (Aug. 31 reaction low) and $70.76 (Aug. 25 reaction low). Resistance at $75.61 (yesterday's high); breach would tilt near-term outlook positive, targeting $76.00 (Wednesday's high), then $76.37 (55-day moving average), $77.43 (200-day moving average) and $78.04 (Sept. 13 reaction high). November crude last down 39 cents at $74.79/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2330 GMT [Dow Jones] India's decision to double foreign investment limit in sovereign bonds broadly positive, bonds set to rally in coming sessions, says Amandeep Chopra, head of fixed income for \uc2 UTI Mutual Fund\uc2 . "The bond market liquidity will ease by allowing the foreign funds to invest. Also with the October-March borrowing cut, markets will rally," he says. Notes, yields on 7.80% 2020 benchmark bond likely to fall to 7.75% in next few sessions, vs 7.90% last close. India doubled limit of foreign investment in government bonds to $10 billion;also cut by INR100 billion October-March borrowing plan to INR1.63 trillion. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2327 GMT [Dow Jones] India's 10-year benchmark bond could see yield easing to 7.50% (7.90% last) over next 4-5 months on government's decision to double foreign investment limit (to $10 billion) in sovereign bonds, says Dhawal Dalal, senior vice president and head of fixed income, DSP BlackRock Invest Managers. "The increase in cap by $5 billion will mean that liquidity of about INR220 billion will come into the market," says Dalal. Adding to cheer, government also cut by INR100 billion its October-March borrowing to INR1.63 trillion. "The two sets of news will mean that RBI's INR110 billion auction on Friday will be well bid," says Dalal. RBI will hold sale of 3 government securities including INR40 billion of benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2325 GMT [Dow Jones] India's move to hike foreign investment cap in government, corporate bonds should boost INR in coming sessions, says Ashish Vaidya, head of trading at \uc2 UBS\uc2 . "People will start positioning in anticipation of these debt flows over the next two to three months," Vaidya says. Expects USD/INR to trade in 45.40-45.60 band Friday (vs 45.65 late Thursday), but break below 45 over next 2-3 months as market also factors in expected inflows earmarked for government stake sales, other share sales. But says, a gaping current account deficit could help cap INR rise. (bijou.george@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2322 GMT [Dow Jones] LME base metals gained ground yesterday, in part reflecting supportive nature of decline in LME inventories for copper, aluminum, zinc, lead, says \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2 . Codelco, world's largest copper producer, expects 2010 production of 1.8 million metric tons with virtually no growth of production into 2011 which, against ongoing growth in copper demand, will increase reliance on smaller mines to supply tightening copper market through 2011, it says. "We continue to expect higher copper prices into next year," Bank says. LME 3-month copper rose $40 on day yesterday at $7,885/ton, lead leapt $52 at $2,275, aluminum added $57 at $2,292, zinc climbed $60 at $2,250, while tin jumped $395 to $23,695 and nickel gained $125 at $22,700. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2321 GMT [Dow Jones] Benchmark 10-year JGB yield may fall to 0.995% vs Wednesday's 1.015% as demand for safe-haven assets grows amid concerns over U.S. economic outlook, says Naoki Tsuchiyama, market analyst at Mizuho Securities. But adds, if 10-year JGB yield falls below 1.0%, that could prompt investors to sell cash bonds to lock in profits, limiting further declines in long-term yields. Lead December JGB futures may hover in 142.60-142.95 range vs Wednesday's 142.65. Tokyo traders say investors watching if Japanese authorities \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market again; if JPY weakens, Tokyo shares may rally, dampening demand for JGBs. (tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com) [ 09-23-10 1947ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100923e69n000d5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
