\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: LME Metals Aimless, Quiet As Dollar Stabilizes\b0\par\par\uc2 1,486 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2010\par 05:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0316 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals hardly trading in Asia with China, Japan, Hong Kong closed for holidays. Trader in Singapore says U.S. dollar has now stabilized after its Fed-inspired lurch lower, so no incentive to trade at the moment in such an illiquid market. Next cues to come from initial U.S. weekly jobless claims at 1230 GMT, U.S. existing home sales and leading indicators at 1400 GMT. LME 3-month copper at $7,838/ton, down $7 with 142 lots done. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0307 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR likely rangebound as USD steady vs most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in tepid trade as several markets in region closed for holidays; still, pair biased down as EUR strength, capital inflows support local unit, says dealer with foreign bank; pair likely to open in 45.58-45.60 range vs 45.59 late Wednesday trade. "Capital flows continue to drive the stock markets and the local unit. The trend is likely to continue," says dealer. Tips pair in 45.40-45.70 range in session. Foreign funds were net buyers of provisional INR9.15 billion worth of Indian stocks on Wednesday, according to data on National Stock Exchange. USD/INR 1-month NDF at 45.69/45.79. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0304 GMT [Dow Jones] India unlikely to tinker with fiscal 2H (October-March) scheduled borrowing (worth INR1.73 trillion) , says STCI Primary Dealership. Adds, borrowing can be attained via smaller auction sizes every week (around INR80 billion). Notes, another option would be avoiding auctions every week by skipping weeks in between, balancing it by having larger auctions worth INR100 billion-INR120 billion in remaining weeks . "A few weeks need to be given a break considering festival holidays and hence truncated working weeks." Expects bond yields to be only influenced by tenor of securities announced, bucket distribution, frequency of auctions and size of weekly auctions. Tips 10-year yield in range of 7.90%-8.05% for next one month. (7.94% last close). RBI, government to meet later today on fiscal 2H calendar. (subhadip.sircar@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0253 GMT [Dow Jones] India sovereign bonds likely biased up in range, tracking fourth successive session of gains for Treasurys, says dealer with state-run bank; yields on 7.80% 2020 benchmark bond likely in 7.92%-7.95% range vs 7.94% (99.07 in price terms) Wednesday close. "We await the outcome of the RBI and government meeting today on finalizing the October-March borrowing calendar," says dealer. Adds, market would prefer short-term securities instead of papers of 10-year and above maturity. Notes, government likely to stick to plan for INR1.7 trillion borrowing in fiscal 2H. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0248 GMT [Dow Jones] Chances of another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after last week's Y2 trillion move to weaken JPY, are keeping USD/JPY well above 84.00 mark, says UOB. Still, notes that, with \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and other North Asian markets closed today, actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't appear likely in Asian session, ahead of much-anticipated summit in Washington; "it looks like PM Naoto Kan might wait until his meeting with President Obama on Thursday before he acts." USD/JPY last at 84.55 vs 84.54 late in NY trade. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0244 GMT [Dow Jones] New production, return of shuttered projects will see a record nickel supply growth of 190,000 tons in 2011 to 1.6 million tons, vs 49,000-ton increase in 2009, 56,000-ton fall in 2008, says Macquarie. Drivers will be resumption of Vale's (VALE) Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  after year-long strike which took out 120,000-130,000 tons annual production, plus start-up of other Vale projects, \uc2 Anglo American\uc2  (AAL.LN) projects and others. As result, global nickel market to move from 2010's 85,000-ton deficit to 15,000-20,000-ton surplus, but house says this "not as catastrophic for the market as it first appears" as considerable uncertainty exists about which projects will ultimately come onstream. Notes, LME 3-month nickel well below 2007's record $51,800/ton price but well above $8,850/ton low in Oct. 2008; closed at $22,575/ton yesterday, yet to trade today. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0220 GMT [Dow Jones] China's iron ore import data show slowing imports from traditional suppliers, an attempt to diversify supply base through smaller exporters, local product, says Macquarie. While supply from "big four" -- Australia, India, Brazil, South Africa -- declined slightly on year during January-August to 347.6 million tons from 354.8 million tons, others increased share to 57.8 million tons from 50.2 million tons. Diversification drive has provided support to prices, but with exports from big four now ramping up "this support may be lacking in the near term." Domestic mines also increasing in importance; total imports barely increased on year though pig iron production +11.4%. Also notes that Australia only major iron ore supplier to China now achieving higher FOB prices than at mid-2008 market peak, with premium over Brazilian ore showing difficulty for ores to achieve parity given high freight costs. 62% Fe iron ore at North China delivery ports at $139.20/ton latest. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares down 0.1% at 453.26 as local investors sell on view that government will be reluctant to step in with efforts to lower interest rates and boost cash supply from 4Q because of rising inflation. State media Tuesday reported that prices in Ho Chi Minh City and Hanoi, stoking expectations for national data due later this month to show pickup in inflation in September. "Concerns about high interest rates are hurting investors' appetite to buy," says Hanoi-based brokerage trader. Adds correction likely mild, short-lived as market supported by hopes of solid 3Q earning. Tips key index in 451-455 range. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP higher early, lifted by suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders say. Pair last at 44.00 in moderate volume after settling at year-low 43.88 late Wednesday; resistance at 44.20, support 43.80. Traders say \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected to have bought at least $100 million between 43.88-43.95; "That prompted banks to cut short positions, pushing the dollar above figure (44)," says one local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0213 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares likely tad lower as weak U.S shares Wednesday may inspire investors to continue to take profit, traders say; tip support for benchmark index at 3320 after it closed down 0.6% at 3343.343 yesterday. But trader at Erdhika Securities expects "healthy buying" by foreign funds to offset losses of the main index. \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK), Bank Negara (BBNI.JK) may lead falls ahead of their plans to issue rights shares in 4Q. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold at $1,292.40/oz, up $1.20 since NY close after hitting intraday record of $1,296.25 overnight. "Gold bulls will be tempted to push prices towards $1300 on an intra-day basis. Gold prices remain underpinned by concerns over the global economic outlook," says Phillip Futures. Adds, data flow from U.S. pretty heavy today with initial jobless claims at 1230 GMT, U.S. existing home sales and leading indicators at 1400 GMT which likely to set the tone. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0209 GMT [Dow Jones] Harbor Intelligence says aluminum's 2.8% rally overnight came on back of weak USD, and newsflow from aluminum producers at a conference in Bahrain who have expressed confidence in the demand outlook. Harbor also noted forecast from industry consultant Werner Stelzer that aluminum consumption per car to rise from average of 157 kg per car today to 225 kg per car by 2020. "Our models continue to suggest aluminum prices to reach $2,400/ton at some point before the end of the year and $2,700 at some point in the first half of 2011. It is hard to advise not to be covered," said Harbor. However Triland noted that gains yesterday were made in relatively low volume. "A thin session then which makes us wonder if the rally will be sustained," it said in note. LME 3-month aluminum at $2,240/ton, up $5 and at highest level since early August when it reached recent peak of $2,249. Volume thin at 75 lots done so far reflecting holiday trading conditions with China, Hong Kong, Japan on holiday. (james.campbell@dowjones.com) [ 09-22-10 2316ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100923e69n0001n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
