\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Well Supported Above 84.00 - UOB\b0\par\par\uc2 1,496 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2010\par 04:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0248 GMT [Dow Jones] Chances of another round of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , after last week's Y2 trillion move to weaken JPY, are keeping USD/JPY well above 84.00 mark, says UOB. Still, notes that, with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and other North Asian markets closed today, actual \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  doesn't appear likely in Asian session, ahead of much-anticipated summit in Washington; "it looks like PM Naoto Kan might wait until his meeting with President Obama on Thursday before he acts." USD/JPY last at 84.55 vs 84.54 late in NY trade. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0244 GMT [Dow Jones] New production, return of shuttered projects will see a record nickel supply growth of 190,000 tons in 2011 to 1.6 million tons, vs 49,000-ton increase in 2009, 56,000-ton fall in 2008, says Macquarie. Drivers will be resumption of Vale's (VALE) Canadian \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  after year-long strike which took out 120,000-130,000 tons annual production, plus start-up of other Vale projects, \uc2 Anglo American\uc2  (AAL.LN) projects and others. As result, global nickel market to move from 2010's 85,000-ton deficit to 15,000-20,000-ton surplus, but house says this "not as catastrophic for the market as it first appears" as considerable uncertainty exists about which projects will ultimately come onstream. Notes, LME 3-month nickel well below 2007's record $51,800/ton price but well above $8,850/ton low in Oct. 2008; closed at $22,575/ton yesterday, yet to trade today. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0220 GMT [Dow Jones] China's iron ore import data show slowing imports from traditional suppliers, an attempt to diversify supply base through smaller exporters, local product, says Macquarie. While supply from "big four" -- Australia, India, Brazil, South Africa -- declined slightly on year during January-August to 347.6 million tons from 354.8 million tons, others increased share to 57.8 million tons from 50.2 million tons. Diversification drive has provided support to prices, but with exports from big four now ramping up "this support may be lacking in the near term." Domestic mines also increasing in importance; total imports barely increased on year though pig iron production +11.4%. Also notes that Australia only major iron ore supplier to China now achieving higher FOB prices than at mid-2008 market peak, with premium over Brazilian ore showing difficulty for ores to achieve parity given high freight costs. 62% Fe iron ore at North China delivery ports at $139.20/ton latest. (david.fickling@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0218 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares down 0.1% at 453.26 as local investors sell on view that government will be reluctant to step in with efforts to lower interest rates and boost cash supply from 4Q because of rising inflation. State media Tuesday reported that prices in Ho Chi Minh City and Hanoi, stoking expectations for national data due later this month to show pickup in inflation in September. "Concerns about high interest rates are hurting investors' appetite to buy," says Hanoi-based brokerage trader. Adds correction likely mild, short-lived as market supported by hopes of solid 3Q earning. Tips key index in 451-455 range. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP higher early, lifted by suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders say. Pair last at 44.00 in moderate volume after settling at year-low 43.88 late Wednesday; resistance at 44.20, support 43.80. Traders say \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  suspected to have bought at least $100 million between 43.88-43.95; "That prompted banks to cut short positions, pushing the dollar above figure (44)," says one local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0213 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares likely tad lower as weak U.S shares Wednesday may inspire investors to continue to take profit, traders say; tip support for benchmark index at 3320 after it closed down 0.6% at 3343.343 yesterday. But trader at Erdhika Securities expects "healthy buying" by foreign funds to offset losses of the main index. \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK), Bank Negara (BBNI.JK) may lead falls ahead of their plans to issue rights shares in 4Q. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold at $1,292.40/oz, up $1.20 since NY close after hitting intraday record of $1,296.25 overnight. "Gold bulls will be tempted to push prices towards $1300 on an intra-day basis. Gold prices remain underpinned by concerns over the global economic outlook," says Phillip Futures. Adds, data flow from U.S. pretty heavy today with initial jobless claims at 1230 GMT, U.S. existing home sales and leading indicators at 1400 GMT which likely to set the tone. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0209 GMT [Dow Jones] Harbor Intelligence says aluminum's 2.8% rally overnight came on back of weak USD, and newsflow from aluminum producers at a conference in Bahrain who have expressed confidence in the demand outlook. Harbor also noted forecast from industry consultant Werner Stelzer that aluminum consumption per car to rise from average of 157 kg per car today to 225 kg per car by 2020. "Our models continue to suggest aluminum prices to reach $2,400/ton at some point before the end of the year and $2,700 at some point in the first half of 2011. It is hard to advise not to be covered," said Harbor. However Triland noted that gains yesterday were made in relatively low volume. "A thin session then which makes us wonder if the rally will be sustained," it said in note. LME 3-month aluminum at $2,240/ton, up $5 and at highest level since early August when it reached recent peak of $2,249. Volume thin at 75 lots done so far reflecting holiday trading conditions with China, Hong Kong, Japan on holiday. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0204 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares down 0.4% at 8163.67, tracking weak DJIA overnight and on profit-taking, says Grand Cathay Securities analyst Mars Hsu. Notes many investors staying on sidelines as major Asian bourses closed for holidays today. "Taiex is likely to consolidate in tight range today," he says. Tips Taiex in 8130-8200 band for day. TSMC (2330.TW) down 2.4% at NT$60.30. \uc2 Chang Hwa Commercial Bank\uc2  (2801.TW) +2.8% at NT$20.55. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  expects \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2  to hike interest rates at least twice, beginning middle of next year as further subsidy cuts fuel inflation, stronger growth returns. Says due to rising risks to growth owing to deteriorating external environment, "we think there is less need for rate hikes (this year) but we think there's a possibility the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be back raising rates in 2011." Notes yesterday's August inflation data "marginally" stronger than expected, CPI +2.1% on year vs +2% expected, was +0.4% on month; inflation mainly fueled by 3.1% on-year rise in food, non-alcoholic beverages subindex. Says "we think further subsidy cuts will push inflation higher in the coming months" but expects inflation to stay below 2% for 2010. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD down sharply early at 31.5900 vs 31.7650 at close Tuesday. Analysts say foreign investors entering market, expectations USD to continue weakening trend, strong regional economic performance driving pair down. "In the next three days the pair could test as low as 31.2," says one local trader. Adds, USD/TWD to hover around 31.6 today.(paul.mozur@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0152 GMT [Dow Jones] USD may stay on downtrend, hit by worries on U.S. economic policy direction, now looking blurred by top profile resignations in White House economic team, confusing monetary policy as Fed leans towards extending quantitative easing while Washington drifts towards fiscal belt-tightening, DBS says. "Many also expect President Obama to face difficulty in pushing for more fiscal stimulus if the Democrat Party suffers huge losses at the U.S. mid-term elections on November 2. This goes some way to explain the Fed's reluctance to act and its preference to wait. After all, the QE must serve a purpose and throwing more money after bad (banks that have been reluctant to lend) doesn't make any sense whatsoever." Notes that, if debt monetization is driving the U.S. dollar lower, the "missing ingredient" is more fiscal stimulus to match the QE expectations. EUR/USD last 1.3396 vs 1.3394 late in NY trade yesterday, USD/JPY at 84.55 vs 84.54. (david.roman@dowjones.com) [ 09-22-10 2248ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100923e69n0001c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
