\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  MOF Should Plough On With  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  -RBS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,591 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2010\par 02:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones] Given healthy shape of BOJ's balance sheet compared to other major central banks, Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has plenty of ammunition and should plough ahead with massive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken JPY, especially given huge quantitative easing programs adopted by other central banks, says RBS \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Strategist Greg Gibbs. "The MOF...should not hold back from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in massive proportions to weaken the JPY. It could simply argue it is playing catch up to the QE policies of other major central banks." USD/JPY last 84.54. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0034 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's S&P/ASX 200 down 0.1% at 4621.6, with consumer discretionary, industrials, financials, utilities and property trusts down after S&P 500 fell 0.5%. But S&P/ASX 200 is proving well supported on dips again today, bouncing from early low of 4609.6 with materials, healthcare and consumer staples lending support. In financials, CBA (CBA.AU) down 0.5%, \uc2 QBE Insurance\uc2  (QBE.AU) down 1.7%, while in consumer discretionary sector, \uc2 News Corp\uc2 . (NWS.AU) (owner of this newswire) down 1.7%. Among miners, BHP (BHP.AU) up 0.5%, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) up 0.7%, while in healthcare, CSL (CSL.AU) up 1.6%. "It's very quiet today with most of Asia on holidays," says RBS head of domestic sales trading Justin Gallagher. He doesn't expect much impact from U.S. economic data before the weekend, with most of the attention on the potential for further quantitative easing, in the wake of dovish FOMC statement this week. "Obviously the Fed is getting close to pushing the button on further quantitative easing and that seems to be the focus of the market at the moment," says Gallagher. "The stock market seems happy enough that the Fed is going to do something about the economy. It was a good performance by our market yesterday to hang in there and the SPI is trying to rally here again today." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0027 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI may rise moderately, buoyed by continued gains in construction stocks in anticipation of major government projects to be unveiled in coming months. KLCI ended 0.1% lower at 1474.75 yesterday after swinging between positive, negative territories. "The KLCI could go either way today. But given that underlying sentiment remains positive, the bias is on the upside," dealer says; adds however, any gains likely modest as following rally to 32-month high last week, market has entered consolidation phase in recent sessions. KLCI tipped to stay in 1470-1480 range today. Builders such as Gamuda (5398.KU) and MMC (2194.KU) may be in focus on hopes government may be poised to roll-out key infrastructure projects soon. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0020 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR at 3.0900, unchanged from late yesterday; dealers say pair to maintain downward bias on broad USD weakness, positive outlook for Malaysian economy. "There is still strong momentum on the downside for the dollar/ringgit to test the next support at 3.0750. But how fast the pair gets to that level will depend on what the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  does," dealer says. Bank Negara suspected to have moved to slow MYR's rise as recently as yesterday, buying USD at 3.0900 level. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0018 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ 3Q GDP may be flat to negative, given soft 2Q GDP, drop off in activity in September due to earthquake in Canterbury region, says RBC Strategist Michael Turner. Says his expectations of patchy recovery were confirmed by weaker-than-expected 2Q GDP data "which are arguably suggestive of even less traction than we had assumed through 1H." Continues to expect RBNZ to remain sidelined into 1Q next year "before resuming a stilted policy normalization process through 2011." (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0014 GMT [Dow Jones] CBA becomes latest of Australia's big four banks forecasting RBA 25 bps hike in October, leaving ANZ as last one out; CBA Chief Economist Michael Blythe says inflation risks are growing rapidly in a fully employed economy. "Both these considerations pointed to a need for interest rates to move beyond average levels and into genuine restrictive territory." Doesn't expect back to back hikes though, that will depend on fresh developments. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0007 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares may pull back given Wall Street's overnight retreat, lack of leads from Asia with number of markets shut for holidays. Firm support for STI, which ends flat at 3096.10 yesterday, at August high of 3043. Any upside may be capped at yesterday's new year-to-date 3116 intraday peak. "It will be a lonely ride for Singapore with the key Asian markets closed today. I suspect the blue chips will be subdued while the lower liners will continue to draw interest," says local house trader. Among smaller-cap stocks, Technics Oil & Gas (5CQ.SG) may be in focus as equipment supplier to energy sector secures S$35 million contract to support oil field in offshore Vietnam; stock ends down 0.6% at S$0.895. Biosensors (B20.SG) may also be closely watched as heart-stent maker reveals findings endorsing efficiency of its stent system; stock ends +1.6% at S$0.985. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0006 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ economy lost momentum in 2Q, expanding only 0.2% "significantly below our and market expectations," says \uc2 ANZ Bank\uc2  head of Market Economists and Strategy Khoon Goh; "Based on today's GDP numbers, the RBNZ is well and truly on hold until next year." Says 3Q "will not be looking all that flash" due to negative impact of Canterbury earthquake earlier this month. Tips better prospects for 2011; "the more the economy undertakes the necessary adjustment now, the sooner it will set the stage for a more robust and sustainable recovery. The Rugby World Cup and reconstruction of Canterbury will assist growth next year as well." (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0005 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 last touch lower as market moves into slight holding pattern due to lack of local news, says \uc2 Craigs Investment Partners\uc2  broker Nigel Scott; "global markets have had a very strong run over the last couple of weeks and due to some of these levels that we've reached, people have probably gone into a bit of a holding pattern." Vector (VCT.NZ) trading lower on profit taking after rally this week. But Scott says news that it has refinanced its debt early "shows good management"; adds "they've been able to strengthen their balance sheet in the last year." Telecom NZ (TEL.NZ)+0.5% at NZ$2.04 after Commerce Commission revised criteria for determining where backhaul competition existed and benefited Telecom. NZX-50 last 0.1% at 3232.49. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0001 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian government bonds likely steady as market participants await announcement of new 3-year Islamic bond issue due later; market expectations are for issue size of MYR3.0 billion-MYR3.5 billion, dealers say. "Apart from a stronger ringgit, which is somewhat supportive for bonds, there aren't many leads at the moment. The CPI numbers were well within expectations," dealer says, referring to data released late yesterday showing August inflation of 2.1%. Yesterday, yields on benchmark MGS ended mixed, with May 2013 unchanged at 3.17%, August 2015 unchanged at 3.30%, September 2017 down 1 bps at 3.55%, November 2019 down 2 bps at 3.63%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2359 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals likely to have a relatively quiet trading day with Beijing, HK, Seoul, Tokyo all shut for holidays, says major market participant; adds base metals supported overnight by weaker USD, driven by fears Fed could start pumping billions of new dollars into U.S. economy to boost growth, which weighed heavily on USD; adds limited new supplies of mined copper combined with a gradual reduction in LME approved warehouse stocks continuing to provided support to base metals. LME 3-month copper up $170 Wednesday to settle at $7,845/ton, lead up $49.50 at $2,223.50, aluminum up $60.50 at $2,235, zinc climbs $49 to $2,191, while tin up $350 to $23,300, nickel gains $225 to $22,575. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2358 GMT [Dow Jones] BHP Billiton's (BHP) US$39 billion hostile play for \uc2 Potash Corp\uc2 . (POT) turning into quite the saga, with some big developments overnight as Potash lodged a complaint in the U.S. federal court saying that BHP's bid is built on false and misleading statement. Complaint says BHP violated U.S. securities laws and should therefore be prohibited from proceeding with its US$130/share tender offer for Potash shares. BHP says the lawsuit is without merit and that the company plans to "vigorously" contest it. BHP also reckons the suit won't interfere with its offer. Meanwhile, BHP CEO Marius Kloppers met with Canadian lawmakers to try to ease concerns about the bid. Developments unlikely to have a material impact on BHP shares today, but show any investors hoping for an easy deal are likely to be very disappointed. (lyndal.mcfarland@dowjones.com) [ 09-22-10 2044ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100923e69n0000e\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pic