\par\par \b \uc2 HEARD ON THE STREET: A Return To Beggar-Thy-Neighbor?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Richard Barley   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES COLUMN   \par \uc2 610 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2010\par 18:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Beggar-thy-neighbor \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  devaluations proved ruinous for the global economy in the 1930s. Is the world setting off down the same slippery slope again? \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's decision to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to drive down the value of the yen blew a hole in the developed world's united effort to persuade China and other Asian countries to stop artificially holding down their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Meanwhile speculation that the U.S. and U.K. could soon resume quantitative easing has hit the value of the dollar and sterling. The resulting tensions are bad news for the euro zone but a gift to goldbugs.\par \par \uc2 The good news is that so far \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been aimed more at containing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength than inducing weakness - and with limited success. The impact of the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's initial Y2 trillion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already fading: the dollar is back below Y85, although the risk of further sales of yen remains. Similarly, the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2 's efforts to contain the rise of the Swiss franc haven't stopped it gaining 13.4% against the euro this year. Some emerging markets, such as Brazil, while acknowledging the need for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, have also threatened to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent sharp moves.\par \par \uc2 The risk is that the lure of competitive devaluations may grow if the U.S. and U.K. embark on further quantitative easing. For central bankers, one of the happy by-products of printing money is that it helps drive down the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The Bank of England has welcomed the sharp decline in sterling since late 2008. U.S. Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke's 2002 speech on preventing deflation noted the impact of devaluation in the 1930s; the Fed's statement this week opening the door to more easing pushed the U.S. dollar index to a six-month low. As investors increasingly believe U.S. rates will stay low, the dollar may become ever more favored to fund carry trades.\par \par \uc2 As in the 1930s, competitive devaluations, whether by fair means or foul, are likely to increase international tensions and risk protectionist responses. Meanwhile they will do nothing to address the global imbalances that led to the crisis or tackle the chief problem facing the advanced economies today: lack of domestic demand in many countries.\par \par \uc2 In the short-term, the euro zone has most to lose from rising \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  tensions, given the relative hawkishness of the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The euro has already risen 2.3% against the dollar in the last week. But a rising euro could cause further problems for Europe's periphery as well as for the German export engine at the heart of the European recovery. Longer-term, the biggest gainer is likely to be gold, already pushed to nearly $1,300 an ounce this week, the traditional refuge of those unwilling to put their faith in politicians.\par \par \uc2 (Richard Barley is a writer for Heard on the Street. He has covered the European corporate credit market in one form or another since 1998. He can be reached at +44-20-7842-9406 or by email: richard.barley@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: 