\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Up Vs Yen, Euro Ahead Of Kan-Obama Meet\b0\par\par\uc2 By David Roman   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 715 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2010\par 07:20\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The U.S. dollar inched higher against the yen and the euro Thursday in Asian trade, in a correction of recent losses and ahead of a meeting between U.S. President Barack Obama and Japan Prime Minister Naoto Kan, during which \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be a key topic of discussion.\par \par \uc2 With \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's markets closed for Autumn Equinox Day, and mainland China, Hong Kong and Korean markets also on holidays, major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained in narrow ranges as traders focused on the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  launching another round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after it spent Y2 trillion to weaken the yen last week.\par \par \uc2 The dollar remained well above the Y84.25 mark it hit overnight, the lowest since the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Analysts from UOB said it should remain stable against the yen ahead of the summit expected in Washington later Thursday, even though no \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to come before.\par \par \uc2 "It looks like PM Naoto Kan might wait until his meeting with President Obama on Thursday before he acts," UOB added.\par \par \uc2 At 0501 GMT, the dollar traded at Y84.59 compared with Y84.54 late Wednesday in New York trade. Meanwhile, the euro traded at $1.3392 compared with $1.3394, and Y113.32 compared with Y113.22.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say both the euro and the yen may gain further ground in coming days, as investors remain concerned about U.S. economic prospects. The dollar Wednesday dropped to its lowest level against the euro since April.\par \par \uc2 Analysts from DBS said the greenback is also weighed by the perception that U.S. policy direction is being blurred by top profile resignations in the White House economic team, as well as confusion on monetary policy, as Washington drifts toward fiscal belt-tightening even as the Federal Reserve leans toward launching a fresh round of growth-stimulating asset purchases, known as quantitative easing.\par \par \uc2 "Many also expect President Obama to face difficulty in pushing for more fiscal stimulus if the Democrat Party suffers huge losses at the U.S. mid-term elections on Nov. 2. This goes some way to explain the Fed's reluctance to act and its preference to wait," DBS added. "After all, the QE must serve a purpose and throwing more money after bad (banks that have been reluctant to lend) doesn't make any sense whatsoever."\par \par \uc2 This U.S. dollar weakness, combined with Japan's continued concern on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength, may lead to more Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in coming days, said Greg Gibbs, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist with \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 ...should not hold back from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in massive proportions to weaken the yen. It could simply argue it is playing catch up to the quantitative easing policies of other major central banks," Gibbs said.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , last traded at 79.824, up from 79.812 overnight, but still close to six-month lows hit Wednesday.\par \par \uc2