\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Watch For MOF With USD/JPY Around 84.50 - BNP\b0\par\par\uc2 1,659 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2010\par 01:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2325 GMT [Dow Jones] Watch for possible MOF buying USD/JPY around 84.50 region, though with PM Kan in U.S., "we view aggressive JPY \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as highly unlikely from a political point of view," say \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  strategists. BNP expects pair to drift towards 82.00 by year end, trading range of 84.50-86.00 in near term. Says impact of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will be steam rolled by any renewed quantitative easing from FOMC. USD/JPY last at 84.51. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2321 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads mixed, with overnight SPI 200 futures down 0.3% at 4637.0 as Wall Street fell in quiet trading, although metal prices rose as the U.S. dollar fell after Tuesday's dovish FOMC statement. DJIA down 0.2% at 10739.31, S&P 500 down 0.5% at 1134.28, Nasdaq down 0.6% at 2334.55. U.S. 10-year bond yield fell 2 bps to 2.55%. U.S. July home prices fell 0.5% to 6-year low. \uc2 Adobe\uc2  fell 19% after missing sales estimates. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , BofA and \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  led declines, down 1.6%-2.2%. S&P 500 Financials down 1.6%, with KBW Bank index down 1.8% after \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  downgraded U.S. investment banks. S&P 500 Materials up 0.4%, with \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  up 4.8%, Vale up 1.4%, Freeport up 1.8%, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  down 3.3%, Barrick up 0.9%. BHP ADRs at A$38.98 (based on AUD/USD at 0.9559) vs local close at A$38.90. London listed BHP up 2.1%, Rio (RIO.AU) up 2.3%. S&P 500 Industrials down 0.5%. S&P 500 Energy down 0.5%. S&P 500 Consumer Discretionary down 0.7%. Spot gold US$1,289.73 (hit fresh record high of US$1,295.95) vs US$1,2860.70. Nymex crude oil down 0.4% at US$74.71 after hitting US$76.00. LME copper up 2.2%, zinc up 2.2%, aluminum up 2.9%, nickel up 1.0%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR/USD at 1.3401 vs 1.3255. AUD/USD at 0.9542 vs 0.9536. S&P/ASX 200 last 4625.2. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] Traders on guard for renewed \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen USD/JPY even with a holiday in Tokyo, and keeping a close eye on USD index for any break below 79.50, says NAB Chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist John Kyriakopolous. "A break below here would likely portend a decline in the U.S. Dollar index to its low for the year of 76.60, struck on January 13, which would be a 4% decline." USD/JPY last 84.52. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Singapore August CPI likely +3.3% vs +3.1% in July due to higher transportation, food prices, shows median forecast in Dow Jones poll of six economists. "Exceptionally high COE (certificate of entitlement for vehicles) prices, dearer food prices and rentals are the key factors pushing the inflation reading a notch higher," says Irvin Seah of DBS Bank, who expects +3.4% print. Note, government controls number of private vehicles on roads by issuing quota annually; car buyers bid for COE that grants them right to own vehicle. On month, seasonally adjusted, CPI likely +0.3% vs +0.5% in July, shows poll of three economists. Data due 0500 GMT. (venkat.pr@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2258 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD faces a ceiling at 0.9650 and will need a further improvement in AUS-US two-year swap yield to break higher, says NAB Chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist John Kyriakopolous. Pair last 0.9531, traded 26-month high of 0.9600 offshore. "We note that when AUD/USD was at its pre-GFC high of 0.9850, the JoC industrial metals price index was 11% higher than currently and our risk-appetite index above 70% versus below 60% right now." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2257 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ economy continued to expand in 2Q, but weaker than expected, will add to view RBNZ likely to keep official cash rate well and truly on hold until early 2011. NZD/USD may come under renewed pressure, despite broadly weaker USD. Stats NZ says 2Q GDP +0.2% on quarter versus +0.8% tipped by economists as decline in manufacturing activity offsets nearly all growth in quarter. "There were a lot of industries that were positive in the quarter, but the decline in manufacturing pretty much wiped that out," says Statistics New Zealand official Jason Attewell. On year 2Q GDP +1.9% on year versus expected 2.6%. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2250 GMT [Dow Jones] Despite rush this week amongst analysts to chop and change RBA rate forecasts and project an October move, rates market is still only priced for a 14 bps lift or 56% risk of a 25 bps hike, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  notes. November is different, however, with 23 bps priced in. Further out, dealers are betting on a cash rate of 5.00% in September 2011. Quite modest compared with some of the economists' forecasts in the market. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2248 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian wheat prices could face a steady day on commodity and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  leads. In U.S. overnight, a sale of 220,000 metric tons of U.S. wheat to Egypt, the world's largest importer, pushed prices higher, ending a 2-day losing streak. Soft red winter wheat for December delivery on CBOT added 1.75 cents, or 0.2%, to close at US$7.1975/bushel. Meanwhile, AUD remains strong at last 0.9559 but comfortably within yesterday's trading range, down from 0.9594 around midnight. Yesterday, ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in New South Wales fell A$4.00 to settle at A$275.00/metric ton; ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in Western Australia lost A$1.50 at A$305.50. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2247 GMT [Dow Jones] COMMODITY SUMMARY: Spot gold posted yet another record high yesterday at $1,296.25/oz, helped by a weaker USD, but then profit taking pushed metal down, according to ScotiaMocatta, and early in Asia today traded 70 cents lower from yesterday's close at $1,290.20. Gold futures on Comex in NY also continued march into record territory overnight on speculation U.S. Federal Reserve will flood the market with cash, enhancing gold's appeal as an alternative asset. "There appears to be little in the way of factors that could negatively impact the gold market in the coming months," said Anne-Laure Tremblay, analyst with \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . LME base metals rose, driven by weaker USD, which sent copper to a 5-month high as it makes USD-denominated metals cheaper for users of other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . LME 3-month copper hit an intraday high of US$7,868/metric ton, its highest level since April 16, before closing up $170 vs Tuesday close at $7,845. Among others, 3-month lead leapt $49 at $2,223, aluminum added $60.50 at $2,235, zinc climbed $48 at $2,190, while tin jumped $350 to $23,3000 and nickel gained $225 at $22,575. Of other precious metals, silver early in Asia unchanged at $21.13/oz, platinum eases $2 at $1,629, palladium unchanged at $544. Nymex October crude futures loses 26 cents overnight at $74.89/bbl. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2246 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIA OUTLOOK: Regional stock markets likely to drift lower, weighed by mild losses on Wall Street; financial, tech stocks could lead losers, like their U.S. peers but trade likely quiet with many markets in region shut. China, Hong Kong, Japan, South Korean markets closed for holiday. \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets likely in tight ranges with Tokyo off; traders likely to take cue from equities, still on watch for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken JPY from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 ministry of finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . EUR/USD at 1.3392 vs 1.3394 in late NY trade, EUR/JPY at 113.19 vs 113.22, USD/JPY at 84.50 vs 84.54. On data slate: Singapore CPI at 0500 GMT, BSP policy meeting highlights at 0800 GMT, Taiwan industrial output, jobless rate at 0800 GMT, Vietnam trade balance, CPI. Later there's euro zone flash PMI, U.S. jobless claims, Chicago Fed president Evans speaks, U.S. leading indicators, existing home sales, foreign U.S. debt holdings. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2240 GMT [Dow Jones] Technicals suggest EUR/USD could push towards 1.4000-1.5000 region in mid 4Q before sliding again, says ANZ Senior Strategist Tim Riddell. Dips should hold around 1.3350-1.3355. Last 1.3400. For AUD/USD, test of 0.9850 is likely, breach of 1.00 possible. But, says pair may have paused ascent for now, any slip below 0.9495 may see slide back to 0.9280. Last 0.9551. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2227 GMT [Dow Jones] USD index could revisit 2010 lows of 76.60 if it breaches key support of 79.60, says BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Mike Jones. Notes USD Index has already cleared 200-day moving average at 81.90 but further losses may be limited. "We suspect we'll have to see upcoming U.S. economic data continue to deteriorate to see speculation of further Fed easing continue to drag the USD lower. The strength of the U.S. housing and labor markets are key in this regard." (enda.curran@dowjones.com) [ 09-22-10 1925ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100922e69m000ec\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
