ed about Fed's Tuesday suggestion economy may need additional stimulative measures. \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  off 2.2% as software company launched bond issue worth at least $4.75 billion. Dow's financial components were also weak; \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  down 1.7%; \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  off 1.6%. Declines came as investors continued to digest Fed statement in which officials offered clearest signal yet that they are laying groundwork for new steps to bolster recovery if they don't see growth pick up soon or if inflation falls further. "The market really badly wants to push higher, however today, the information from the Fed is weighing heavily on it," says David Hefty, chief executive at Hefty Wealth Partners. \uc2 Adobe\uc2  tumbled 19% as software maker reported weakening sales of flagship Creative Suite product and issued 4Q sales guidance below estimates. \uc2 CarMax\uc2  +8.5% as its fiscal 2Q profit rose 4.8%, topping expectations. \uc2 Jefferies Group\uc2  fell 15.2% as fiscal 3Q profit fell significantly from fiscal 2Q. Dow dropped 0.2%, Nasdaq 0.6%,Philly Semicons down 1.4%. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2202 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD should trade 1.300-1.400 range, USD/JPY in 83.00-87.00 range ahead of November FOMC meeting but risks to USD are to downside, says \uc2 UBS\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Amelia Bourdeau. Says Fed's signal on possible further QE will hurt USD. "The Fed's move suggests it will add to liquidity even without economic growth deteriorating further, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets are likely to be wary of the dollar in the near term." EUR/USD last 1.3401, USD/JPY last 84.56. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2157 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 likely flat as market lacks cues, exporters may weigh as NZD/USD at 9-month high, says Hamilton Hindin Greene broker James Smalley; "We'll likely have a flat opening as there are not a lot of overseas cues." Says exporters Fisher & Paykel Healthcare (FPH.NZ), Rakon (RAK.NZ) may lose ground due to strength of NZD. Adds Fonterra to report today; "It's more about sentiment rather than direct impact on the capital markets." News Vector (VCT.NZ)has issued US$182 million worth of unsecured notes to repay NZ$250 million Credit Wrapped Medium Term Notes that mature April 2011 may support company. Says 2Q GDP data will be watched but has been overshadowed somewhat by 7.0-magnitude earthquake in Canterbury last month. NZX-50 ended flat yesterday at 3236.36. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2152 GMT [Dow Jones] Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  has set a target for triggering fresh USD/JPY \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which at this stage looks inevitable, says \uc2 UBS\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Amelia Bourdeau. Says comments by PM Kan on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  being unavoidable suggests the MOF has a level in mind to defend. "It appears that policymakers have set a clear floor for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  which will be habitually maintained if and when it is breached." USD/JPY last 84.535. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2148 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD continues to push higher but could be in for a quick reversal, says RBC \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Matthew Strauss. "Our chief technical analyst warns about the possibility of a short-term correction given overbought studies. The next key resistance level comes in at 0.9850, support at 0.9433." Last 0.9556, hit 0.9595 offshore. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2147 GMT [Dow Jones] \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MOF may delay any further plans for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen USD/JPY due to scheduled meeting between PM Kan and U.S. President Obama, says RBC \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Matthew Strauss. USD/JPY last 84.525, traded to 84.28 overnight, the lowest level since MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week at 83.00. "The depreciation of almost 1.5% during the last two trading days must sit uncomfortably with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , increasing the risk of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2121 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD likely to keep gaining in Asia; "It is currently the classic anti-dollar instrument. It's got momentum on its side. It is getting a bit over bought but it still has a lot of momentum behind it," says \uc2 Westpac\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Imre Speizer. Pair last 1.3400, tips resistance at 1.3440, support around 1.3330. Says with no reason for it to collapse, may well test resistance on day. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2120 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. SUMMARY: USD dipped to lowest level against EUR since April as investors ditched greenback on speculation Fed could soon act to kick-start slowing U.S. economy. USD weakened against JPY to lowest mark since last week's massive Y2 trillion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dipping below Y84.50 and putting investors on watch for another round of yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Fed's specific mention of weaker-than-targeted inflation has added a weight around USD's already stressed neck, says Sacha Tihanyi, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Scotia Capital in Toronto; "The overarching reason to dislike the dollar has broadened." EUR/USD at 1.3394 late Wednesday vs 1.3247 late Tuesday, USD/JPY at 84.54 vs 85.12, EUR/JPY at 113.22 vs 112.75, GBP/USD at 1.5669 vs 1.5627, USD/CHF at 0.9864 vs 0.9976. USD Index at 79.812 vs 80.442. Stocks slipped as investors worried about Fed's suggestion economy may need additional stimulative measures; \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  fell 2.2% as software company launched bond issue worth at least $4.75 billion. Dow fell 0.2%, Nasdaq fell 0.6%, Philly semicons down 1.4%. Long-dated treasurys rose as speculation continued that Fed will buy bonds at larger scale in coming months; 2-year yields flat at 0.428%, 10-years down 3 bps at 2.546%. December Comex gold rose $17.80 to $1,292.10/oz. November Nymex crude settled 26 cents lower at $74.71/bbl after unexpected increase in U.S. stockpiles of oil, fuel products. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com) [ 09-22-10 1847ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100922e69m000e0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
