\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Post-Fed Euro Is Winner, But Maybe Not For Long\b0\par\par\uc2 By Don Curren and Karen Johnson   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 671 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2010\par 21:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TORONTO (Dow Jones)--Investors are bailing out of the dollar as further monetary easing seems increasingly likely, with the euro the big winner in the immediate aftermath of Tuesday's shift toward easier policy at the Fed.\par \par \uc2 But it's not clear whether the euro will remain ahead of the pack in the new environment created by the Fed's indication that, if needed, it will open the monetary taps again.\par \par \uc2 The common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's gains could be trimmed in coming sessions, and other units, notably the Australian dollar and \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from emerging Asia, could be the bigger beneficiaries.\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the euro's popularity Wednesday is not due to any intrinsic attraction to the common \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  itself. It's more a reflection of its status as the main alternative to the dollar, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  now more seriously threatened by the potential corrosive effects of quantitative easing.\par \par \uc2 "The euro wins by default, to a large degree," said Shaun Osborne, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  in Toronto.\par \par \uc2 With Japanese authorities recently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  against strength in the yen and the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this year against strength in the Swiss franc, investors might be skittish in their pursuit of those \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and head into the euro instead, he said.\par \par \uc2 The potential for further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will deter appreciation by the yen, said Brian Dolan, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .com in Bedminster, N.J.\par \par \uc2 "It's going to have some tendency to strengthen. I think we can see [the dollar] back below Y84, but from there we're going to have reckon with some \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.\par \par \uc2 With the potential for further quantitative easing a key theme for the market, other non-U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , notably the U.K. pound, are more at risk of being undermined than the euro, Dolan said.\par \par \uc2 "It's the U.S. and the U.K. that are most likely to pursue quantitative easing, and Japan comes up in third place there," he said.\par \par \uc2 But, once again, the euro appears to be a winner by default rather than its own merits.\par \par \uc2 "It's simply because the [European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 ] has not indicated that it is going to ease policy any more than it already has," said Jessica Hoversen, fixed income and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 MF Global\uc2  in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 "Does that necessarily mean that the euro is going to continue on? No, it's got its own set of problems, and a reckoning is likely waiting in the wings."\par \par \uc2 Hoversen favors \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  within the commodity complex, namely the New Zealand and Australian dollars, for their tight links to Asian economic expansion.\par \par \uc2 Dolan, of \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 .com, adds the Norwegian krone to that list.\par \par \uc2 "The problem...there is that they're extremely popular and the market is already extremely long these \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said.\par \par \uc2 They agree the Canadian dollar is likely to lag its commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peers because of its close ties with the struggling U.S.\par \par \uc2 Emerging Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including those of South Korea, Thailand and Singapore, remain attractive in the current environment, said Dolan.\par \par \uc2 The biggest winner, however, in a world where central banks are increasingly pursuing policies that, directly or indirectly, devalue their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , isn't really a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  at all.\par \par \uc2 "The obvious winner would be gold," said TD's Osborne.\par \par \uc2 (Karen Johnson writes about Canadian fixed income and \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets for Dow Jones Newswires. She can be reached at 416-306-2022; or via email: karen.johnson@dowjones.com.)\par \par \uc2 (TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAmericas@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.) [ 09-22-10 1502ET ]\par \par \uc2 1141   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100922e69m000cc\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
