\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: EUR/JPY May Touch 114.00 In Europe Trade - Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,512 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2010\par 09:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0717 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY may rise to 114.00 in global day as European investors consider EUR relatively attractive as FOMC statement weakens USD while players increasingly cautious over possible Japanese authorities \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as USD/JPY falls below 85.00 in Asia time, says Kenichiro Ikezawa, senior dealer at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Daiwa SB Investments\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Says EUR/USD may rise to 1.3334, which is high marked early August; but further rises unlikely considering technical charts. EUR/JPY last 113.07 after hitting intraday high at 113.15. EUR/USD last 1.3317 after marking intraday high at 1.3331.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0716 GMT [Dow Jones] It's all going wrong for the GBP, says Nicholas Hastings in \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Focus. Bank lending is miserably low, house prices have turned down, public finances are proving even worse than expected, and chances are the Bank of England will now have to resort to more quantitative easing. Hopes that the UK would be the first of the major economies to see off the recession and start hiking rates early next year are quickly becoming a distant dream. And all this has happened even before the coalition government announces details of its harsh public sector cutting program next month. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0716 GMT [Dow Jones] Tuesday's EMU auctions and FOMC statement were positive factors for EUR/USD, notes \uc2 UniCredit\uc2 , with the spot easily overcoming its 200-day moving average at 1.3220. This move has considerably improved the near-term outlook, says the bank, and a break above the April high of 1.3340 is now a distinct possibility. However, \uc2 UniCredit\uc2  adds a note of caution, saying EMU worries are bound to come to the fore again and will offer welcome opportunities to take profit. EUR/USD now at 1.3314. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0708 GMT [Dow Jones] The dollar sank in late U.S. trade Tuesday after the Fed signalled a fresh batch of QE is more than likely although a double dip recession isn't. That mix enabled the risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rally hard, and Europe comes in to find EUR/USD near four month highs, AUD/USD at fresh two year highs, and USD/JPY fast approaching nine month lows. The dollar slippage was less severe against the JPY, which is not surprising as that pairing has the threat of BOJ \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  hanging over it. PM Kan said in a FT interview Wednesday that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, and also to put in place broad economic and monetary policies that will help weaken the yen. For Wednesday all eyes are likely to be on USD/JPY with traders trying to guess at just what level the BOJ will step in, especially as previously thought 'lines in the sand' at 85.50 and 85.20 have already been breached, and the next one at 84.80 is now under threat. (gary.stride@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0708 GMT [Dow Jones] The German bund yield curve flattens in early European trading Wednesday after Tuesday's FOMC statement suggested the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  was on the verge of another round of QE. "While the Fed did not say it is resuming QE, it said it is ready to act and its assessment on economy clearly points to a QE start before the end of the year," says the \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  strategy team. The 2s/5s bund yield spread trades 3bps lower at +64.5bps, 2s/10s flattens 5.5bps to +161.0bps and 2s/30s trades 5.5bps tighter at +220bps. Further out, the 5s/10s is down 2bps to +97bps and the 5s/30s falls 2bps to +156bps while at the long end, 10s/30s is unchanged at +59bps. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0705 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI steady at 1476.19; benchmark index staying within tight range with market breadth positive; 378 advancers vs 315 decliners. "The market is going through a healthy consolidation at the moment but we think the uptrend stay intact," says local dealer. Tips 1470-1480 range rest of day. Among key gainers are construction plays after government unveils plan for new high-speed rail, mass rapid transit system, among other projects, within 10 years; YTL (4677.KU) +2.8% at MYR7.80, Gamuda (5398.KU) +3.7% at MYR3.89, \uc2 MMC Corp\uc2  (5916.KU) +4.0% at MYR2.89. Decliners include exporters like latex glovemakers on concerns over strong MYR; Kossan (7153.KU) down 4.5% at MYR3.00, Latexx (7064.KU) down 3.0% at MYR2.62. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0701 GMT [Dow Jones] Portugal offers up to EUR1B of 4-year, and 10-year bonds Wednesday, and Nomura expects the auction to be absorbed following strong demand at Tuesday's Irish government bond sale and subsequent tightening of peripheral yield spreads. "Furthermore the Portuguese auction should also be encouraged by the government's commitment to slash the budget deficit below 3% of the GDP by 2012, a plan that is supported in principle by the opposition party," Nomura says. The 10-year Portuguese/German yield spread is 5.8 bps wider on the day at 389.3 bps, although that's also in part due to a sharp decline in bund yields. The 10-year bund yield is down 6.4 bps from Tuesday. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0659 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +0.8% at 22,184.45, supported by broad-based rally after FOMC's statement indicates low interest rates environment to sustain. UOB KayHian's Steven Leung expects index may test 22,500 near-term; "the latest rally is mainly driven by liquidity as the dollar's weakness is supporting the prevailing buying spree in the equity market." House keeps HSI end-2010 target at 26,500. Adds, recent improvement in volume suggests latest uptrend likely to persist, with today's amount brisk at HK$45.75 billion so far. HKEx (0388.HK) +1.4% at HK$142.60, supported by revival of AIA's HK listing, which could be world's 2nd-biggest IPO this year. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0654 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia government bonds gain as foreign flows continue to enter, dealers say. "Trading quite robust this morning, mostly foreign names," dealer says; adds, 10-year may see correction either this week or early next week, while longer-term notes yields likely steady. 5-year yield down at 7.34% vs 7.38% yesterday; 10-year yield at 7.80% vs 7.82%; 15-year yield tad higher at 8.32% vs 8.40%; 20-year yield at 8.67% vs 8.84%; and 28-year yield at 9.06% vs 9.14%. (andreasismar.sandiwan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0643 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 9.7% at HK$1.715 billion midday, 4.3% of total market volume vs 4.9% at Tuesday's half-day, suggesting bears mostly staying put amid HSI's 9th session of rise out of 10. \uc2 China Unicom\uc2  (0762.HK) leads with HK$121.55 million, followed by Tencent (0700.HK) with HK$115.60 million, Cheung Kong (0001.HK) with HK$104.76 million. Next come Hutchison (0013.HK) with HK$93.10 million, Agricultural Bank of China (1288.HK) with HK$74.36 million. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0643 GMT [Dow Jones] German government bonds rally across the board after the Fed overnight left the door ajar for further asset purchases, with bunds outperforming followed by bobls. Should the Fed end up buying more bonds, the speculation is it will not only restrict itself to the front end of the curve and buy more longer term Treasurys as well. The 2s/10s German yield curve flattens as a result, with the yield spread 5.4 bps tighter on the day at 155.7 bps. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0641 GMT [Dow Jones] Firmer U.S. stock futures augur well for Singapore shares for rest of session, paving way for STI to close above 3100 for first time in 27 months. STI +0.5% at 3111.25, with resistance at higher end of 3085-3146 breakdown gap formed in June 2008. Market breadth at just under 3 gainers for every decliner. "The buying momentum is strong, especially for the small and mid caps. Despite technical indicators showing overbought conditions, prices could still head higher in the near term," says local house dealer. GMG Global (5IM.SG) remains most active stock in market, +23.4% at S$0.29. In response to SGX queries over price action, natural rubber producer says it's in talks to acquire company in Southeast Asia engaged in rubber production and trading, and in process of taking on project in Africa; no other details disclosed. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com) [ 09-22-10 0317ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100922e69m00045\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
