vestors also cautious about yen movement while Japan markets close tomorrow for holiday. "Investors want to confirm (fiscal) first-half corporate earnings and the second-half outlook before bidding shares higher," says Masatoshi Sato, senior strategist at Mizuho Investors Securities. "It is difficult to draw a scenario for the Nikkei to top 10,000 with USD/JPY hovering at its current level (84-85)." Adds, worries over deterioration in China-Japan ties over shipping incident also weighing on market. 14/33 Topix subindexes lower; Komatsu (6301.TO) down 1.8% at Y1,888, \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) down 1.1% at Y2,608. Real estate sector top performer (up 1.2%) on signs decline in residential land prices for Japan's major cities may be bottoming out; \uc2 Sumitomo Realty & Development\uc2  (8830.TO) up 1.6% at Y1,720. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0434 GMT [Dow Jones] India sovereign bonds up on cues from overnight rally in U.S. Treasurys but tight liquidity may cap large gains, says dealer with state-run banks; 7.80% 2020 benchmark bond at 99.07 vs 98.92 at last close. "Any large movements ahead of RBI's INR110 billion auction is unlikely, though the borrowing calendar for October-March will be a major cue on how markets will position itself," says dealer. Government, RBI will meet to finalize borrowing calendar for October-March period on Thursday. RBI to hold its last auction, for fiscal 1H ending September, of three government securities including 10-year benchmark for INR40 billion on Friday. Dealer tips 2020 bond yields in 7.90%-7.97% range in session, last 7.94%. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0434 GMT [Dow Jones] Fed's dovish statement overnight bolstering bullish sentiment for precious metals; gold, silver, platinum all edging towards recent highs. BMO Capital Markets analyst Bart Melek says "considerable risks associated with the euro and the U.S. dollar over the long term (revaluation of the Chinese yuan and massive fiscal imbalances), growing interest in commodities as a bona fide asset class and a hedge, and producer de-hedging prompt us to see gold and the precious metals complex in a very positive light well into 2011." Spot gold last at $1,288.60/oz, +$1.80 since NY close, just off all-time record nominal high of $1,290.55; spot silver at $20.96, +1 cent, just off overnight 31-month high of $21.06. Spot platinum at $1,629/oz, +$10, highest level since May 19. "Rising cost of production, especially in South Africa after (power producer) Eskom's sharp price increases, should provide a bullish case for these metals (platinum and palladium), as will growing structural deficits," he says. Spot palladium last at $536/oz, +$3. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0432 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0343 GMT misstated the type of USD/JPY option a trader saw being bought. The correct version follows:) Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options unchanged at 10.50%/11.20% from New York Tuesday. Options dealer at major Japanese bank says though spot USD/JPY falls below 85.00, demand for downside protection remains low as investors expect \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities will again \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if there is significant further fall, following \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week at low of 82.87. Says vols may stay around current levels unless spot falls below 84.50 (currently 84.88). If USD/JPY does break that level, vols may rise to 11.00%, dealer says. Single player buys 1-week USD-call/JPY-put contracts at strike price of 85.00 with implied vols of 10.55% for about $100 million, same dealer says. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR finds strong support at 8,950; Bank Indonesia suspected to have bought around $200 million near 8,950-8,960 to stem IDR's rise, 3 dealers say. Pair last at 8,955 vs 8,965 late yesterday. "Bank Indonesia is supporting the dollar at around IDR8,950" but \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  probably won't lift pair higher because greenback is weak worldwide, dealer with foreign bank says. 8,950-8,970 range tipped for rest of day. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0419 GMT [Dow Jones] India sovereign bonds up on cues from overnight rally in U.S. Treasurys but tight liquidity may cap large gains, says dealer with state-run banks; 7.80% 2020 benchmark bond at 99.07 vs 98.92 at last close. "Any large movements ahead of RBI's INR110 billion auction is unlikely, though the borrowing calendar for October-March will be a major cue on how markets will position itself," says dealer. Government, RBI will meet to finalize borrowing calendar for October-March period on Thursday. RBI to hold its last auction, for fiscal 1H ending September, of three government securities including 10-year benchmark for INR40 billion on Friday. Dealer tips 2020 bond yields in 7.90%-7.97% range in session, last 7.94%. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0418 GMT [Dow Jones] Bangko Sentral Governor Amando Tetangco says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "mindful of the impact of the interest differentials being in favor of EMEs on global risk appetite and capital flows, as this could complicate policy going forward." Comments underscore view that Fed's decision to keep interest rates low makes it harder for emerging markets like Philippines to normalize rates after pulling them down to allow economies to cope with global financial crisis. Luz Lorenzo, economist at ATR-Kim Eng Securities, says \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have to keep overnight rates low for while longer than needed for fear of attracting more hot money, further boosting peso. "And this is the cause of all those housing problems (in the U.S.) -- they kept interest rates too low for too long." (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets says if history is a guide, Japan would likely be a top performer when U.S. job market improves. "In line with such gains, sales of expensive consumer durables, an area where Japan boasts competitiveness, tend to grow. Furthermore, as capital investments also begin to increase, shipments of capital goods, another field in which Japan's industrial strength lies, benefit significantly." Says assuming (typical) time lag of some 3 months following business cycle troughs, Japanese equity market will visibly improve its performance "possibly already within this year" and solidify a top performer position vs global counterparts. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com) [ 09-22-10 0039ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100922e69m0002o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
