\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY Vols Stable;  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Still Expected\b0\par\par\uc2 3,116 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2010\par 05:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0343 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options unchanged at 10.50%/11.20% from New York Tuesday. Options dealer at major Japanese bank says though spot USD/JPY falls below 85.00, demand for downside protection remains low as investors expect \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities will again \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if there is significant further fall, following \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last week at low of 82.87. Says vols may stay around current levels unless spot falls below 84.50 (currently 84.88). If USD/JPY does break that level, vols may rise to 11.00%, dealer says. Single player buys 1-week USD-call/JPY-put contracts at strike price of 85.00 with implied vols of 10.55% for about $100 million, same dealer says. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 Corrected September 22, 2010 00:35 ET (04:35 GMT)\par \par \uc2 0341 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares +0.2% at 454.21 on 3Q earnings hopes, but traders say sharp gains unlikely as government make think twice about taking measures to boost cash supply due to high CPI rises in Ho Chi Minh City, Hanoi this month. Index likely to stay in narrow range "until government announces key CPI and GDP figures later this month," says Hanoi-based brokerage trader. Volume thin with 23.5 million shares valued at VND640 trillion traded so far. Trader tips resistance at 457. Among advancers, \uc2 PetroVietnam\uc2  Fertilizet (DPM.VH) +1.7% at VND30,700, Itaco (ITA.VH) +2.2% at VND18,900, \uc2 Hoa Phat Group\uc2  (HPG.VH) +1.0% at VND38,500. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0329 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds mixed in thin trade. Earlier gains track U.S. Treasurys as expectations grow that Fed will eventually launch another large-scale bond buying program to help economy. But long-term notes fall after disappointing result in auction of MOF bonds due June 2019 worth THB15 billion, says local dealer; adds after sharp slide post auction, yields likely to hover near current levels. Auction result shows weighted average accepted yield of 3.0693% with 0.82X bid coverage ratio. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 quoted at 2.78%/2.75% vs 2.80%/2.75% late yesterday, November 2016 at 2.90%/2.83% vs 2.90%/2.88%, June 2019 at 3.15%/3.08% vs 3.05%/3.00%. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0328 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares +0.7% at 943.93 with across-the-board gains in moderate trade as foreign inflows return with gusto, while prospect U.S. Fed may look at further quantitative easing measures also bolsters equities. Index resistance at 945-950, support at 940. PTT (PTT.TH) +1.1% at THB290 after company announces withdrawal of bid for Thai assets of Carrefour (CA.FR). "I think that this is a positive factor because if PTT diversified their business to retail it doesn't make sense," says \uc2 Kim Eng Securities\uc2  strategist Mayuree Chowvikran. Another bidder, \uc2 Berli Jucker\uc2  (BJC.TH) +1.7% at THB18.30, also rising on that news. Among top actives, PTT Chemical (PTTCH.TH) +4.9% at THB129.50, Banpu (BANPU.TH) +1.5% at THB680, \uc2 Siam Commercial Bank\uc2  (SCB.TH) +1.6% at THB95.75. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0322 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex likely to open about 40-60 points higher helped by positive trade in some Asian markets, strong liquidity, upbeat mood after index closes above 20,000 mark yesterday for first time since January 2008, says local dealer. Sensex ends +0.5% at 20,001.55. Adds sustained foreign inflows, normal monsoon rains will further help; overseas funds net buy INR23.12 billion worth of shares yesterday vs INR18.80 billion Monday. Still, reckons Sensex to find strong resistance at 20,350 this week. Says high levels could witness profit-booking as valuations look stretched after over-20% rally since June 9. Adds lack of fresh domestic triggers this month, outperformance vs other emerging markets to also weigh, cap upside. Tips Sensex in 19,850-20,150 range. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0319 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares down 0.4% at 3350.821 in light volume, off earlier high of 3376.689, on further profit taking mostly by local funds amid absence of fresh leads, traders say; tip support at 3340. "Selling in most bank and consumer related stocks has driven the main index lower amid caution as to whether the correction will continue," says trader at Erdhika Securities. Among decliners: Bank Negara (BBNI.JK) down 4.1% at IDR3,475, \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) down 1.6% at IDR6,250 ahead of bank's plan to issue rights in 4Q, while car maker Astra (ASII.JK) down 0.9% at IDR54,700. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0319 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY may rise to 114.00 in global day, due to relative EUR attractiveness as FOMC statement overnight weakens USD, while investors remain cautious about Japan authorities actions to stem JPY strength, says Akira Hoshino, senior dealer at \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ. Tips resistance at 115.00 in near term. EUR/JPY hit 6-week high at 112.99 earlier vs last 112.92.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0313 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR likely lower as bearish USD index, stronger EUR may drive INR gains for 6th successive session, says dealer with private bank; pair likely to open in 45.50-45.55 band vs 45.67 in late trade yesterday. "The local unit will appreciate but gains could be limited on some month-end dollar buying and profit taking," says dealer. Tips pair may test 45.42-45.45, which if breached may pave way to 45.32. Notes equity markets may correct after Sensex touches 32-month high of 20,000 in last session. Foreign funds net buyers of provisional INR23.12 billion worth of stocks on yesterday, according to data on National Stock Exchange. USD/INR 1-month NDFs at 45.75/45.80. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0302 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered recommends selling USD/PHP via 3-month NDFs outright, with 44.20 as entry rate, spot reference at 43.90, spot target rate of 42.50; "short-term technicals for USD/PHP are modestly bearish as the 14-day RSI (relative strength index) and stochastics are rolling over still shy of oversold territory. The outlook in the weekly charts is also modestly bearish." Notes, next key support for USD/PHP at 43.70 ahead of 42.50. Spot last at 43.92.(cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0300 GMT [Dow Jones] India sovereign bonds likely to open higher, tracking overnight rally in U.S. Treasurys, says dealer with foreign bank; 7.80% 2020 benchmark bond likely to open around 99.10 vs 98.92 Tuesday's close. Still, sharp gains unlikely as liquidity concerns remain. "Also, the RBI's INR110 billion auction is slated on Friday so there may not be a large buying," says dealer. Tips 10-year paper in 98.95-99.25 range in session. RBI will auction three government bonds Friday including benchmark 2020 bond for INR40 billion. Dealer notes borrowing calendar for October-March period likely to be decided Thursday. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0255 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI extends gains, last +0.9% at 22,201.42, next target April 12 high of 22,388. Index rising for 9th session out of 10, for cumulative gains of 5.2%. Among blue chips, Cheung Kong (0001.HK), Wharf (0004.HK), \uc2 Li & Fung\uc2  (0494.HK) setting fresh 52-week high. Perhaps good time to revisit valuation given recent rally; Taifook Securities says valuation for local shares "is by no means stretched," noting blue chips trading at 14.1X historical earnings vs historical average of 15.0X. "The medium-term prospects for local equities thus remain promising, as undemanding valuation and high liquidity have effectively put a floor on major downside risks." Having said that, tips short-term consolidation likely given sizable gains. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0239 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up tad midday, +0.03% at 9605.12, as expectations for more \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and firmer EUR/JPY (now 112.91) help offset concerns over weaker USD/JPY (now 84.90). "The previous \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (carried out last week) was unexpected (in terms of timing), so it's possible the government will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again at the lower 84 yen level" to counter widespread expectations for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only when USD/JPY falls below 83, says Takashi Ushio, general manager at \uc2 Marusan Securities\uc2 . Puts Nikkei's afternoon range at 9550-9650, pegging support at 9500. 20/33 Topix subindexes higher; Panasonic (6752.TO) up 3.5% at Y1,150 on Nikkei report it expects to cancel equity financing plan. Sharp (6753.TO) up 1.9% at Y862 on deal to buy U.S. solar-power developer. China-linked stocks, including automakers, decline on concerns over escalating Japan-China tension over shipping incident; Nissan (7201.TO) off 1.4% at Y703, Komatsu (6301.TO) down 1.6% at Y1,893, Fanuc (6954.TO) down 0.7% at Y10,520. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com) [ 09-21-10 2343ET ]\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0432 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0343 GMT misstated the type of USD/JPY option a trader saw being bought. The correct version follows:) Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY 