ment securities including 10-year benchmark for INR40 billion on Friday. Dealer tips 2020 bond yields in 7.90%-7.97% range in session, last 7.94%. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0418 GMT [Dow Jones] Bangko Sentral Governor Amando Tetangco says \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "mindful of the impact of the interest differentials being in favor of EMEs on global risk appetite and capital flows, as this could complicate policy going forward." Comments underscore view that Fed's decision to keep interest rates low makes it harder for emerging markets like Philippines to normalize rates after pulling them down to allow economies to cope with global financial crisis. Luz Lorenzo, economist at ATR-Kim Eng Securities, says \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may have to keep overnight rates low for while longer than needed for fear of attracting more hot money, further boosting peso. "And this is the cause of all those housing problems (in the U.S.) -- they kept interest rates too low for too long." (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0409 GMT [Dow Jones] Daiwa Securities Capital Markets says if history is a guide, Japan would likely be a top performer when U.S. job market improves. "In line with such gains, sales of expensive consumer durables, an area where Japan boasts competitiveness, tend to grow. Furthermore, as capital investments also begin to increase, shipments of capital goods, another field in which Japan's industrial strength lies, benefit significantly." Says assuming (typical) time lag of some 3 months following business cycle troughs, Japanese equity market will visibly improve its performance "possibly already within this year" and solidify a top performer position vs global counterparts. (bradford.frischkorn@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0355 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex +0.4% at 20,075.22 on follow-through buying in select bluechips, with gains in most regional markets lifting sentiment. Market breadth positive with 24 of 30 index issues up. "The opening is on expected lines. Select auto shares and some index heayweights are seeing fair amount of buying interest," says dealer at domestic brokerage. Angel Securities expects next resistance at 20,107. Maruti (532500.BY) +2.4% at INR1,445, Wipro (507685.BY) +1.9% at INR443.30. Mahindra (500520.BY) +1.7% at INR693.25, HDFC (500010.BY) +1.2% at INR728.40. But, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hero Honda\f28 \uc2 \f2  (500182.BY) down 1.2% at INR1,785, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Infosys (500209.BY) \f28 \uc2 \f2 off 1.1% at INR3,025 on profit-booking. (gurdev.singh@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0355 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR off lows as month-end dollar demand from oil companies, profit- booking caps gains for local unit, says dealer with state-run bank; pair at 45.54 after falling to a fresh four-month low of 45.44 earlier, 45.67 late Tuesday trade. "Overall there is selling pressure for the dollar but oil companies have stepped and that has supported the pair," says dealer. Tips pair in broad 45.27-45.72 range for session. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0343 GMT [Dow Jones] Volatilities implied by 1-month ATM USD/JPY options unchanged at 10.50%/11.20% from New York Tuesday. Options dealer at major Japanese bank says though spot USD/JPY falls below 85.00, demand for downside protection remains low as investors expect \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  authorities will again \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  if there is significant further fall, following \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  last week at low of 82.87. Says vols may stay around current levels unless spot falls below 84.50 (currently 84.88). If USD/JPY does break that level, vols may rise to 11.00%, dealer says. Single player buys 1-week USD-put/JPY-call contracts at strike price of 85.00 with implied vols of 10.55%