\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: EUR/JPY May Rise; N/T Resistance Tipped at 115.00\b0\par\par\uc2 1,496 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2010\par 05:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0319 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY may rise to 114.00 in global day, due to relative EUR attractiveness as FOMC statement overnight weakens USD, while investors remain cautious about Japan authorities actions to stem JPY strength, says Akira Hoshino, senior dealer at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\f28 \uc2 \f2  UFJ. Tips resistance at 115.00 in near term. EUR/JPY hit 6-week high at 112.99 earlier vs last 112.92.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0313 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/INR likely lower as bearish USD index, stronger EUR may drive INR gains for 6th successive session, says dealer with private bank; pair likely to open in 45.50-45.55 band vs 45.67 in late trade yesterday. "The local unit will appreciate but gains could be limited on some month-end dollar buying and profit taking," says dealer. Tips pair may test 45.42-45.45, which if breached may pave way to 45.32. Notes equity markets may correct after Sensex touches 32-month high of 20,000 in last session. Foreign funds net buyers of provisional INR23.12 billion worth of stocks on yesterday, according to data on National Stock Exchange. USD/INR 1-month NDFs at 45.75/45.80. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0302 GMT [Dow Jones] Standard Chartered recommends selling USD/PHP via 3-month NDFs outright, with 44.20 as entry rate, spot reference at 43.90, spot target rate of 42.50; "short-term technicals for USD/PHP are modestly bearish as the 14-day RSI (relative strength index) and stochastics are rolling over still shy of oversold territory. The outlook in the weekly charts is also modestly bearish." Notes, next key support for USD/PHP at 43.70 ahead of 42.50. Spot last at 43.92.(cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0300 GMT [Dow Jones] India sovereign bonds likely to open higher, tracking overnight rally in U.S. Treasurys, says dealer with foreign bank; 7.80% 2020 benchmark bond likely to open around 99.10 vs 98.92 Tuesday's close. Still, sharp gains unlikely as liquidity concerns remain. "Also, the RBI's INR110 billion auction is slated on Friday so there may not be a large buying," says dealer. Tips 10-year paper in 98.95-99.25 range in session. RBI will auction three government bonds Friday including benchmark 2020 bond for INR40 billion. Dealer notes borrowing calendar for October-March period likely to be decided Thursday. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0255 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI extends gains, last +0.9% at 22,201.42, next target April 12 high of 22,388. Index rising for 9th session out of 10, for cumulative gains of 5.2%. Among blue chips, Cheung Kong (0001.HK), Wharf (0004.HK), \uc2 Li & Fung\uc2  (0494.HK) setting fresh 52-week high. Perhaps good time to revisit valuation given recent rally; Taifook Securities says valuation for local shares "is by no means stretched," noting blue chips trading at 14.1X historical earnings vs historical average of 15.0X. "The medium-term prospects for local equities thus remain promising, as undemanding valuation and high liquidity have effectively put a floor on major downside risks." Having said that, tips short-term consolidation likely given sizable gains. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0239 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up tad midday, +0.03% at 9605.12, as expectations for more \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and firmer EUR/JPY (now 112.91) help offset concerns over weaker USD/JPY (now 84.90). "The previous \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (carried out last week) was unexpected (in terms of timing), so it's possible the government will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again at the lower 84 yen level" to counter widespread expectations for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only when USD/JPY falls below 83, says Takashi Ushio, general manager at \uc2 Marusan Securities\uc2 . Puts Nikkei's afternoon range at 9550-9650, pegging support at 9500. 20/33 Topix subindexes higher; Panasonic (6752.TO) up 3.5% at Y1,150 on Nikkei report it expects to cancel equity financing plan. Sharp (6753.TO) up 1.9% at Y862 on deal to buy U.S. solar-power developer. China-linked stocks, including automakers, decline on concerns over escalating Japan-China tension over shipping incident; Nissan (7201.TO) off 1.4% at Y703, Komatsu (6301.TO) down 1.6% at Y1,893, Fanuc (6954.TO) down 0.7% at Y10,520. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0232 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP lower, weighed by gains in major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  vs USD following FOMC comments on readiness to support U.S. economy. Pair last at 43.90 in moderate volume after settling at 44.005 late yesterday, with support at 43.80. "We'll likely see limited gain for the peso. The market remains wary the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will provide substantial liquidity if the peso gains further," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0225 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +0.5% at 22,108.21, like yesterday led by HK developers after Fed statement signals ultra-low interest rates here to stay. \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) +1.9% at HK$125.30, Sino Properties (0083.HK) +1.8% at HK$15.68, Cheung Kong (0001.HK) +0.8% at HK$110.10. Overall, HSI rising for 9th session out of 10; despite already good gains, "we look for (Asian) equity markets to rally through to November," says Nomura, as "equities remain inexpensive versus government bonds in many markets." \uc2 China Resources Land\uc2  (1109.HK) again bucks trend, off 1.9% at HK$16.62 as latest asset injection priced less favourably than in the past. Market volume very strong at HK$10.24 billion, although in part inflated by HK$1.73 billion from Kingboard Laminates (1888.HK) after stake disposal by parent Kingboard Chemical (0148.HK). (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0217 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares starting to retreat, surrendering early gains to profit-takers; benchmark PSEi down 0.7% to 4060.23 in moderate volume after closing at new record high 4087.96 yesterday; support at 4000, resistance 4125. Jose Vistan, research head at AB Capital Securities, says investors are starting to consolidate recent gains, but market's retreat "should be cushioned by good fundamentals." Market breadth still positive, with gainers ahead of losers 49 to 40; leading decliners are property developer Megaworld (MEG.PH), down 2.4% at PHP2.40, Energy Development (EDC.PH), down 3.6% at PHP5.94, conglomerate SM Investments (SM.PH), down 1.0% at PHP520 -- all recent gainers. Cushioning market retreat are Philippine National Bank (PNB.PH), +5.8% at PHP60, SM Prime Holdings (SMPH.PH), +0.2% at PHP12.42. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0212 GMT [Dow Jones] As expected, volume very thin on LME in absence of Chinese participants due to 3-day national holiday. Some early dip buying visible after metals end lower yesterday - before FOMC statement. Statement likely to be perceived as supportive for metals as it left dollar weaker and opened door to further monetary stimulus in U.S. LME 3-month copper at $7,707/ton, up $32 with 131 lots done; LME 3-month aluminum $2,179/ton, up $4.50 with 78 lots done. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] Scale of any USD-buying, JPY-selling will be key to success of any further \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as Tokyo can no longer rely on element of surprise to amplify effects of action given that investors now expecting additional moves if USD/JPY keeps falling, says Yuichiro Harada, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 . "Since people are now on guard for the possibility of further Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the important thing will be the size of any move," in addition to level at which authorities act, Harada says. With USD/JPY again falling below 85.00, says expectations for MOF to move likely to increase. "The government jumped last week when the dollar fell into the 82-level, so the move back into the 80-85 range makes people think all the more that if MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again that wouldn't be strange at all." USD/JPY last 84.95.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0209 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD may rise to 1.3350 in global day if it breaks August 6 recent high of 1.3334, triggering buying stop-loss orders around that level, says senior dealer at Japan trust bank. Pair rose to intraday high at 1.3312 earlier in Asia morning as FOMC statement overnight raises expectation of further easing in U.S., prompting investors to sell USD, dealer says. Though pair falls slightly after marking intraday high, "Momentum is strong," dealer adds. Pair last 1.3294. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com) [ 09-21-10 2319ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100922e69m0001v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
