\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: India Govt Bonds Likely Higher On Gains In Tsys\b0\par\par\uc2 1,474 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2010\par 05:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0300 GMT [Dow Jones] India sovereign bonds likely to open higher, tracking overnight rally in U.S. Treasurys, says dealer with foreign bank; 7.80% 2020 benchmark bond likely to open around 99.10 vs 98.92 Tuesday's close. Still, sharp gains unlikely as liquidity concerns remain. "Also, the RBI's INR110 billion auction is slated on Friday so there may not be a large buying," says dealer. Tips 10-year paper in 98.95-99.25 range in session. RBI will auction three government bonds Friday including benchmark 2020 bond for INR40 billion. Dealer notes borrowing calendar for October-March period likely to be decided Thursday. (nupur.acharya@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0255 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI extends gains, last +0.9% at 22,201.42, next target April 12 high of 22,388. Index rising for 9th session out of 10, for cumulative gains of 5.2%. Among blue chips, Cheung Kong (0001.HK), Wharf (0004.HK), \uc2 Li & Fung\uc2  (0494.HK) setting fresh 52-week high. Perhaps good time to revisit valuation given recent rally; Taifook Securities says valuation for local shares "is by no means stretched," noting blue chips trading at 14.1X historical earnings vs historical average of 15.0X. "The medium-term prospects for local equities thus remain promising, as undemanding valuation and high liquidity have effectively put a floor on major downside risks." Having said that, tips short-term consolidation likely given sizable gains. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0239 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up tad midday, +0.03% at 9605.12, as expectations for more \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and firmer EUR/JPY (now 112.91) help offset concerns over weaker USD/JPY (now 84.90). "The previous \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (carried out last week) was unexpected (in terms of timing), so it's possible the government will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again at the lower 84 yen level" to counter widespread expectations for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only when USD/JPY falls below 83. Puts Nikkei's afternoon range at 9550-9650, pegging support at 9500. 20/33 Topix subindexes higher; Panasonic (6752.TO) up 3.5% at Y1,150 on Nikkei report it expects to cancel equity financing plan. Sharp (6753.TO) up 1.9% at Y862 on deal to buy U.S. solar-power developer. China-linked stocks, including automakers, decline on concerns over escalating Japan-China tension over shipping incident; Nissan (7201.TO) off 1.4% at Y703, Komatsu (6301.TO) down 1.6% at Y1,893, Fanuc (6954.TO) down 0.7% at Y10,520. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0232 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP lower, weighed by gains in major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  vs USD following FOMC comments on readiness to support U.S. economy. Pair last at 43.90 in moderate volume after settling at 44.005 late yesterday, with support at 43.80. "We'll likely see limited gain for the peso. The market remains wary the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will provide substantial liquidity if the peso gains further," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0225 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +0.5% at 22,108.21, like yesterday led by HK developers after Fed statement signals ultra-low interest rates here to stay. \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) +1.9% at HK$125.30, Sino Properties (0083.HK) +1.8% at HK$15.68, Cheung Kong (0001.HK) +0.8% at HK$110.10. Overall, HSI rising for 9th session out of 10; despite already good gains,