\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: HSI Extends Gains But Valuation Not Stretched -TF\b0\par\par\uc2 1,459 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2010\par 04:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0255 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI extends gains, last +0.9% at 22,201.42, next target April 12 high of 22,388. Index rising for 9th session out of 10, for cumulative gains of 5.2%. Among blue chips, Cheung Kong (0001.HK), Wharf (0004.HK), \f28 \uc2 \f2 Li & Fung\f28 \uc2 \f2  (0494.HK) setting fresh 52-week high. Perhaps good time to revisit valuation given recent rally; Taifook Securities says valuation for local shares "is by no means stretched," noting blue chips trading at 14.1X historical earnings vs historical average of 15.0X. "The medium-term prospects for local equities thus remain promising, as undemanding valuation and high liquidity have effectively put a floor on major downside risks." Having said that, tips short-term consolidation likely given sizable gains. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0239 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei up tad midday, +0.03% at 9605.12, as expectations for more \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market and firmer EUR/JPY (now 112.91) help offset concerns over weaker USD/JPY (now 84.90). "The previous \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  (carried out last week) was unexpected (in terms of timing), so it's possible the government will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again at the lower 84 yen level" to counter widespread expectations for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  only when USD/JPY falls below 83. Puts Nikkei's afternoon range at 9550-9650, pegging support at 9500. 20/33 Topix subindexes higher; Panasonic (6752.TO) up 3.5% at Y1,150 on Nikkei report it expects to cancel equity financing plan. Sharp (6753.TO) up 1.9% at Y862 on deal to buy U.S. solar-power developer. China-linked stocks, including automakers, decline on concerns over escalating Japan-China tension over shipping incident; Nissan (7201.TO) off 1.4% at Y703, Komatsu (6301.TO) down 1.6% at Y1,893, Fanuc (6954.TO) down 0.7% at Y10,520. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0232 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP lower, weighed by gains in major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  vs USD following FOMC comments on readiness to support U.S. economy. Pair last at 43.90 in moderate volume after settling at 44.005 late yesterday, with support at 43.80. "We'll likely see limited gain for the peso. The market remains wary the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will provide substantial liquidity if the peso gains further," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0225 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +0.5% at 22,108.21, like yesterday led by HK developers after Fed statement signals ultra-low interest rates here to stay. \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) +1.9% at HK$125.30, Sino Properties (0083.HK) +1.8% at HK$15.68, Cheung Kong (0001.HK) +0.8% at HK$110.10. Overall, HSI rising for 9th session out of 10; despite already good gains, "we look for (Asian) equity markets to rally through to November," says Nomura, as "equities remain inexpensive versus government bonds in many markets." \uc2 China Resources Land\uc2  (1109.HK) again bucks trend, off 1.9% at HK$16.62 as latest asset injection priced less favourably than in the past. Market volume very strong at HK$10.24 billion, although in part inflated by HK$1.73 billion from Kingboard Laminates (1888.HK) after stake disposal by parent Kingboard Chemical (0148.HK). (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0217 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares starting to retreat, surrendering early gains to profit-takers; benchmark PSEi down 0.7% to 4060.23 in moderate volume after closing at new record high 4087.96 yesterday; support at 4000, resistance 4125. Jose Vistan, research head at AB Capital Securities, says investors are starting to consolidate recent gains, but market's retreat "should be cushioned by good fundamentals." Market breadth still positive, with gainers ahead of losers 49 to 40; leading decliners are property developer Megaworld (MEG.PH), down 2.4% at PHP2.40, Energy Development (EDC.PH), down 3.6% at PHP5.94, conglomerate SM Investments (SM.PH), down 1.0% at PHP520 -- all recent gainers. Cushioning market retreat are Philippine National Bank (PNB.PH), +5.8% at PHP60, SM Prime Holdings (SMPH.PH), +0.2% at PHP12.42. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0212 GMT [Dow Jones] As expected, volume very thin on LME in absence of Chinese participants due to 3-day national holiday. Some early dip buying visible after metals end lower yesterday - before FOMC statement. Statement likely to be perceived as supportive for metals as it left dollar weaker and opened door to further monetary stimulus in U.S. LME 3-month copper at $7,707/ton, up $32 with 131 lots done; LME 3-month aluminum $2,179/ton, up $4.50 with 78 lots done. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0210 GMT [Dow Jones] Scale of any USD-buying, JPY-selling will be key to success of any further \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as Tokyo can no longer rely on element of surprise to amplify effects of action given that investors now expecting additional moves if USD/JPY keeps falling, says Yuichiro Harada, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 . "Since people are now on guard for the possibility of further Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the important thing will be the size of any move," in addition to level at which authorities act, Harada says. With USD/JPY again falling below 85.00, says expectations for MOF to move likely to increase. "The government jumped last week when the dollar fell into the 82-level, so the move back into the 80-85 range makes people think all the more that if MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again that wouldn't be strange at all." USD/JPY last 84.95.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0209 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/USD may rise to 1.3350 in global day if it breaks August 6 recent high of 1.3334, triggering buying stop-loss orders around that level, says senior dealer at Japan trust bank. Pair rose to intraday high at 1.3312 earlier in Asia morning as FOMC statement overnight raises expectation of further easing in U.S., prompting investors to sell USD, dealer says. Though pair falls slightly after marking intraday high, "Momentum is strong," dealer adds. Pair last 1.3294. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] Non-Japan names buying good amounts of USD/JPY at 84.80, triggering short squeeze to as high as 85.01 this morning, says Singapore-based dealer. Notes large stop-loss sell orders stand below 84.80, but these not yet triggered when USD/JPY hit 84.78 low this morning. USD/JPY last 84.93. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares +0.2% at 454.34 with locals coming back to buy after some firms, including \uc2 Vinamilk\uc2  (VNM.VH), FPT Corp (FPT.VH), \uc2 PetroVietnam\uc2  Fertilizer (DPM.VH), forecast solid results for 3Q. "Sentiment is slowly improving because many investors are also hoping that the government will soon take bold steps, including lowering interest rates and boosting cash supply, to help accelerate economic recovery in 4Q," says Hanoi-based brokerage analyst. Tips key index in 453-460 range today. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] Key question now with regard to Fed policy is what could trigger a second round of quantitative easing (QE2), ponder \uc2 Rabobank\uc2  analysts in note. Says double dip recession would be reason though threshold may be lower. Sluggish recovery with sticky unemployment (or rebound in joblessness) would make case for QE. So would risk of deflation; "In fact, that is what the FOMC underlined in its statement: core inflation is too low. Both employment and inflation are part of the Fed's dual mandate. Interestingly, the Fed used the word 'mandate' three times in statement. In previous statement in August, word did not appear at all. The FOMC may have come to the conclusion that its mandate offers possibility to start QE2 even in absence of a double dip. (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0156 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares likely slightly higher, helped by gains in several Asian markets, firmer IDR, traders say; tip resistance at 3380 after shares end down 0.2% yesterday at 3365.037. "Overall, I think the market may continue to consolidate amid an absence of fresh leads, with local retail investors likely taking profit on any gains," says fund manager at Lautandhana Securities. Car distributor Astra (ASII.JK), coal miner Bumi (BUMI.JK) may gain on hopes for higher 3Q earnings, but bellwether Telkom (TLKM.JK) may fall on further profit taking. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com) [ 09-21-10 2255ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100922e69m0001n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
