\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Thai Stocks Tipped Higher; 940 Resistance\b0\par\par\uc2 1,663 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2010\par 03:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0142 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares expect to continue push higher as foreign funds start to flood back into market after snapping up THB3.61 billion worth of stocks yesterday (THB17.37 billion month-to-date). Speculation U.S. Fed may look at more quantitative easing measures also provides some bullishness for equities, says KGI Securities strategist Rakpong Chaisuparakul. "We're still very positive on the market, and foreign funds inflows are expected to continue." Resistance at 940, then 945, support at 935. Index closed +1.5% at 937.21 yesterday. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0139 GMT [Dow Jones] Noted RBA watcher Terry McCrann writes in today's Daily Telegraph that absent a left-field shock, a rate hike next month is "now all but certain". Says potentially more painful for home owners will be a possible 10-15bp hike on top of that by commercial banks; that'd raise the ire of new minority Labor government, with Treasurer Swan already pre-emptively jawboning banks against any moves outside of official RBA hikes. Growing certainty of an RBA hike could cause commercial banks to hold off; but McCrann says if there's no official rise until February - as a shrinking number of commentators believe - banks will probably go independently in next few weeks. Meanwhile, \uc2 TD Securities\uc2  senior strategist Annette Beacher notes unexpectedly hawkish tone of RBA this week has finally prompted OIS market to price in a more realistic risk of RBA tightening by year end. (rachel.pannett@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0137 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI little changed at 1476.67, trapped in narrow 3-point range so far; dealers say key index likely rangebound rest of day, may even slip into negative territory later as absence of fresh catalysts, lack of cues from Wall Street overnight, could spur profit-taking. Any decline, however, would be temporary as long-term uptrend remains intact, analysts say. "Even a 50-point correction from the current level would not alter the bullish technical landscape," OSK Research says; pegs immediate resistance at 1500, support at 1457. Among gainers, Glomac (5020.KU) +2.1% at MYR1.49 after reporting above-view 1Q FY11 earnings, HSL (6238.KU) +2.9% at MYR1.75; among decliners, Genting Malaysia (4715.KU) down 1.1% at MYR3.60 on profit-taking after recent rally to 30-month high. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0131 GMT [Dow Jones] Investors continue to favor lowers liners in Singapore over blue chips. FTSE ST Small Cap Index +0.5%, FTSE ST Mid Cap Index +0.5%. STI off 0.3% at 3086.36, expected to hold above 3043 August peak. "The near-term upside is likely capped" for STI, says CIMB; "momentum is slowing in the last couple of days, as suggested by its indicators. The RSI is now overbought, which warns of a consolidation period in the near future." Market breadth at 1.5 gainers for every decliner. Key gainers among small caps include GMG Global (5IM.SG) +23.4% at S$0.29, UPP Holdings (U09.SG) +11.1% at S$0.20, Fuxing China (DC9.SG) +8.6% at S$0.19, UMS Holdings (558.SG) +5.8% at S$0.455. Among blue chips, decliners include Jardine C&C (C07.SG) off 2.3% at S$37.42, \uc2 Jardine Matheson\uc2  (J36.SG) off 1.2% at US$46.26. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0120 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI may drift in narrow range around 22,000 to consolidate recent gains (HSI up 8 of past 9 sessions for cumulative gains of 4.3%), as some investors may opt to take some money off table ahead of HK holiday tomorrow, China's 3-day holiday starting today. Futures trader says U.S. FOMC statement neutral for HK stocks, hints interest rates would stay low although HK developers already rallied yesterday ahead of meeting. Adds, Fed "leaving the door open" for more quantitative easing also expected. HK property stocks may take breather; market volume may taper off vs Tuesday's HK$70.38 million. HSI eked out small gains of 0.1% to 22,002.59, closing above 22,000 for first time since April 15. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0120 GMT [Dow Jones] Spot gold at $1,288.10/oz, up $1.30 since NY close after Fed hinted overnight it could take additional measures to stimulate economic growth; gold jumped from $1,270.60 to $1,288.50 after Fed statement released. Dealer in Sydney says there's definitely an uptick in buying interest this morning; "it does feel like Asia wants to try higher levels." But $1,290.55 record high hit earlier providing some resistance with a couple of failed attempts at $1,290 already. "It may take London to come in for us to decisively breach $1,290." Adds holidays in Japan, HK tomorrow could see some volatility in Asia with liquidity likely to be thin. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0119 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR lower at 8,955 vs 8,965 late yesterday on USD's general weakness, dealers say; add Bank Indonesia likely to buy pair if continues to fall below 8,950. "I expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to start buying the dollar around IDR8,945," a dealer says; Bank Indonesia's deputy governor Sarwono said recently that it would continue to smooth \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, but won't fight for certain level. 8,940-8,970 range tipped for today. (im-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0115 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB lower at 30.71-30.73 vs 30.74-30.75 late yesterday in Asia amid broad USD weakness against regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after FOMC says it's prepared to step in further to boost struggling U.S. economy. "It looks like there is the potential for another rally day for the THB," says Bangkok-based dealer, with pair possibly set to break below 30.70. Market also keeping eye on \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  for other possible measures despite Governor Tarisa yesterday saying there's no immediate plans to introduce additional measures to stem unit's rise. Pair tipped in 30.70-30.80 range, although push below 30.70 could see pair slip to 30.65. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0100 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ annual current account deficit narrows to smallest level in 1Q at NZ$4.5 billion, now NZ$5.6 billion and 3.0% of GDP, expected to widen, says ANZ economist Khoon Goh; "we do not expect a return to current account deficits of 7% plus (of GDP) - in the current environment that would be a recipe for a credit rating downgrade." Adds sustainable current account deficit in 4%-5% of GDP range. Says deficit likely to widen on continued profit improvement as economy continues its gradual recovery alongside expected pick-up in import demand. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0059 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped lower on broad weakness of USD after FOMC remarks it's prepared to move to bolster U.S. economy; pair may slide towards 43.80 support after settling at 44.005 late yesterday, with resistance at 44.20. "The dollar was down against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the Fed said it is prepared to provide additional accommodation if needed by the U.S. economy. The peso should follow" the lead of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  vs USD, says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.2% at 4625.7 in mixed trading after indecisive movements on Wall Street. Materials, telcos and property trusts underperforming, while energy, industrials, consumer staples, healthcare and utilities outperforming. Heavyweight gains include CSL (CSL.AU) up 2.8%, Newcrest (NCM.AU) up 0.6%, \uc2 Wesfarmers\uc2  (WES.AU) up 1.1%, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  (WBC.AU) up 0.8%, Origin Energy (ORG.AU) up 1.3%. Negatives include BHP (BHP.AU) down 0.3%, Rio (RIO.AU) down 0.7%, \uc2 Telstra\uc2  (TLS.AU) down 1.1%, \uc2 QBE Insurance\uc2  (QBE.AU) down 1.4%. \uc2 David Jones (DJS.AU) \uc2 down 1.4% after saying that to achieve the upper end of its 5%-10% profit growth guidance for FY11, "economic recovery needs to be in full swing". David Jones' FY10 profit rose 9.1% to A$170.8 million, meeting expectations. "The Australian market is holding slight gains after undeperforming the U.S. this week," says Chris MacDonald, investment adviser at RBS Morgans. "There's scope for profit taking in global equities after such a fantastic run this month. But you did have the Fed hinting that it's preparing for further quantitative easing, which would be bullish for equities. I think we are just looking for further catalysts but there's a lot of cash on the sidelines and the Australian market should be trading around 5000, based on forward PE ratios, so you have 10% risk built in on fear. I think greed will eventually take over, because earning 2.7% on a U.S. 10-year bond is not going to generate wealth." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0052 GMT [Dow Jones] Investors who piled up USD/JPY-long positions on expectation that pair could rise further if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again may reverse course, sell pair if Tokyo does not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon, says senior dealer at major European bank. Still no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite pair again breaching 85.00, which some players had expected could increase chances of Tokyo action. Says expectations for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be only reason to buy USD/JPY now, as U.S. Treasury yields extend falls after FOMC statement overnight. "The whole point is whether or not Japanese authorities will act or not." Pair last 84.85 after touching 84.81. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com) [ 09-21-10 2142ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100922e69m00013\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
