GDP, expected to widen, says ANZ economist Khoon Goh; "we do not expect a return to current account deficits of 7% plus (of GDP) - in the current environment that would be a recipe for a credit rating downgrade." Adds sustainable current account deficit in 4%-5% of GDP range. Says deficit likely to widen on continued profit improvement as economy continues its gradual recovery alongside expected pick-up in import demand. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0059 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped lower on broad weakness of USD after FOMC remarks it's prepared to move to bolster U.S. economy; pair may slide towards 43.80 support after settling at 44.005 late yesterday, with resistance at 44.20. "The dollar was down against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the Fed said it is prepared to provide additional accommodation if needed by the U.S. economy. The peso should follow" the lead of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  vs USD, says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.2% at 4625.7 in mixed trading after indecisive movements on Wall Street. Materials, telcos and property trusts underperforming, while energy, industrials, consumer staples, healthcare and utilities outperforming. Heavyweight gains include CSL (CSL.AU) up 2.8%, Newcrest (NCM.AU) up 0.6%, \uc2 Wesfarmers\uc2  (WES.AU) up 1.1%, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  (WBC.AU) up 0.8%, Origin Energy (ORG.AU) up 1.3%. Negatives include BHP (BHP.AU) down 0.3%, Rio (RIO.AU) down 0.7%, \uc2 Telstra\uc2  (TLS.AU) down 1.1%, \uc2 QBE Insurance\uc2  (QBE.AU) down 1.4%. \uc2 David Jones (DJS.AU) \uc2 down 1.4% after saying that to achieve the upper end of its 5%-10% profit growth guidance for FY11, "economic recovery needs to be in full swing". David Jones' FY10 profit rose 9.1% to A$170.8 million, meeting expectations. "The Australian market is holding slight gains after undeperforming the U.S. this week," says Chris MacDonald, investment adviser at RBS Morgans. "There's scope for profit taking in global equities after such a fantastic run this month. But you did have the Fed hinting that it's preparing for further quantitative easing, which would be bullish for equities. I think we are just looking for further catalysts but there's a lot of cash on the sidelines and the Australian market should be trading around 5000, based on forward PE ratios, so you have 10% risk built in on fear. I think greed will eventually take over, because earning 2.7% on a U.S. 10-year bond is not going to generate wealth." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0052 GMT [Dow Jones] Investors who piled up USD/JPY-long positions on expectation that pair could rise further if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again may reverse course, sell pair if Tokyo does not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon, says senior dealer at major European bank. Still no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite pair again breaching 85.00, which some players had expected could increase chances of Tokyo action. Says expectations for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be only reason to buy USD/JPY now, as U.S. Treasury yields extend falls after FOMC statement overnight. "The whole point is whether or not Japanese authorities will act or not." Pair last 84.85 after touching 84.81. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0052 GMT [Dow Jones] Expect Singapore government bond yield curve to flatten in Asian trade, tracking U.S. Treasury yield curve moves. Comes as Fed meeting raises speculation of more quantitative easing in future. Trader sees yields at longer end likely down 4-5 bps, while yields at short end down 1-2 bps. Yesterday, 2-year yield ends down 1 bp at 0.46%, 10-year yield ends down 3 bps at 2.12%. (samuel.holmes@dowjones.com) [ 09-21-10 2139ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100922e69m00012\par }\page {\