\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/IDR Down; Bank Indonesia May Step In At 8,945\b0\par\par\uc2 1,466 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2010\par 03:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0119 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/IDR lower at 8,955 vs 8,965 late yesterday on USD's general weakness, dealers say; add Bank Indonesia likely to buy pair if continues to fall below 8,950. "I expect the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to start buying the dollar around IDR8,945," a dealer says; Bank Indonesia's deputy governor Sarwono said recently that it would continue to smooth \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movement, but won't fight for certain level. 8,940-8,970 range tipped for today. (im-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0115 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB lower at 30.71-30.73 vs 30.74-30.75 late yesterday in Asia amid broad USD weakness against regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after FOMC says it's prepared to step in further to boost struggling U.S. economy. "It looks like there is the potential for another rally day for the THB," says Bangkok-based dealer, with pair possibly set to break below 30.70. Market also keeping eye on \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  for other possible measures despite Governor Tarisa yesterday saying there's no immediate plans to introduce additional measures to stem unit's rise. Pair tipped in 30.70-30.80 range, although push below 30.70 could see pair slip to 30.65. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0100 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ annual current account deficit narrows to smallest level in 1Q at NZ$4.5 billion, now NZ$5.6 billion and 3.0% of GDP, expected to widen, says ANZ economist Khoon Goh; "we do not expect a return to current account deficits of 7% plus (of GDP) - in the current environment that would be a recipe for a credit rating downgrade." Adds sustainable current account deficit in 4%-5% of GDP range. Says deficit likely to widen on continued profit improvement as economy continues its gradual recovery alongside expected pick-up in import demand. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0059 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP tipped lower on broad weakness of USD after FOMC remarks it's prepared to move to bolster U.S. economy; pair may slide towards 43.80 support after settling at 44.005 late yesterday, with resistance at 44.20. "The dollar was down against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the Fed said it is prepared to provide additional accommodation if needed by the U.S. economy. The peso should follow" the lead of major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  vs USD, says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0058 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.2% at 4625.7 in mixed trading after indecisive movements on Wall Street. Materials, telcos and property trusts underperforming, while energy, industrials, consumer staples, healthcare and utilities outperforming. Heavyweight gains include CSL (CSL.AU) up 2.8%, Newcrest (NCM.AU) up 0.6%, \uc2 Wesfarmers\uc2  (WES.AU) up 1.1%, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  (WBC.AU) up 0.8%, Origin Energy (ORG.AU) up 1.3%. Negatives include BHP (BHP.AU) down 0.3%, Rio (RIO.AU) down 0.7%, \uc2 Telstra\uc2  (TLS.AU) down 1.1%, \uc2 QBE Insurance\uc2  (QBE.AU) down 1.4%. \uc2 David Jones (DJS.AU) \uc2 down 1.4% after saying that to achieve the upper end of its 5%-10% profit growth guidance for FY11, "economic recovery needs to be in full swing". David Jones' FY10 profit rose 9.1% to A$170.8 million, meeting expectations. "The Australian market is holding slight gains after undeperforming the U.S. this week," says Chris MacDonald, investment adviser at RBS Morgans. "There's scope for profit taking in global equities after such a fantastic run this month. But you did have the Fed hinting that it's preparing for further quantitative easing, which would be bullish for equities. I think we are just looking for further catalysts but there's a lot of cash on the sidelines and the Australian market should be trading around 5000, based on forward PE ratios, so you have 10% risk built in on fear. I think greed will eventually take over, because earning 2.7% on a U.S. 10-year bond is not going to generate wealth." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0052 GMT [Dow Jones] Investors who piled up USD/JPY-long positions on expectation that pair could rise further if \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  again may reverse course, sell pair if Tokyo does not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  soon, says senior dealer at major European bank. Still no signs of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  despite pair again breaching 85.00, which some players had expected could increase chances of Tokyo action. Says expectations for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be only reason to buy USD/JPY now, as U.S. Treasury yields extend falls after FOMC statement overnight. "The whole point is whether or not Japanese authorities will act or not." Pair last 84.85 after touching 84.81. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0052 GMT [Dow Jones] Expect Singapore government bond yield curve to flatten in Asian trade, tracking U.S. Treasury yield curve moves. Comes as Fed meeting raises speculation of more quantitative easing in future. Trader sees yields at longer end likely down 4-5 bps, while yields at short end down 1-2 bps. Yesterday, 2-year yield ends down 1 bp at 0.46%, 10-year yield ends down 3 bps at 2.12%. (samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0048 GMT [Dow Jones] New floor for USD/SGD likely around 1.3260; pair falls to fresh low of 1.3272 on views U.S. Fed may take on more quantitative easing measures; trader notes 1.3260 about where "everybody expects the limits" of MAS (Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's) band lies, but says basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears "well-behaved" at present so no expected call for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  just yet. Resistance for USD/SGD expected around 1.3320; last 1.3281.(samuel.holmes@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0047 GMT [Dow Jones] Little in way of resistance for AUD/USD before July 2008 levels of 0.9850 but pair won't test parity just yet, that's more of a mid-2011 target, says RBS \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Greg Gibbs. Says good deal of RBA hikes priced in for now, but with rates likely to head near 6.00%, AUD can only push higher. "I would assume parity in the forecast for the middle of next year would be appropriate." AUD/USD last 0.9570, around fresh 26-month highs on heavy USD selling. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0043 GMT [Dow Jones] Benchmark 10-year JGB yield falls to 3-week low at 1.000%, may fall to 0.950% in coming sessions after dovish FOMC statement overnight fuels speculation that Fed likely to take further easing action ahead, forcing BOJ to follow suit, says Chotaro Morita, head of Japan fixed income strategy research at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Adds lead December futures may rise to 143.00; contract last up 0.32 at 142.70. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0038 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI may fall as investors take profits from recent gains in blue chips such as Genting (3182.KU) in absence of fresh cues; any downside, however, likely modest as stronger MYR (at fresh 13-year high), upbeat outlook for domestic economic growth to lend support, dealers say. KLCI ended +0.4% at 1475.99 yesterday. "Any pullback should be seen as a healthy correction. Most players are still looking at the 1500 psychological resistance to be tested at some point in the next few weeks," dealer says; tips KLCI to remain in 1470-1480 range today. Recent outperformers like Genting, Gamuda (5398.KU) may retreat on profit-taking. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0037 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei off 0.4% at 9562.85 following lower open as USD/JPY falls below 85 (now 84.87) after U.S. Fed signaled additional steps to prop up U.S. economy. Nikkei support seen at 9500 for today with investors still wary about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by Japanese authorities, says Masayoshi Yano, senior market analyst at Meiwa Securities. "I doubt the (prior executed) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be a one-off event, but it will be hard to do again immediately after the dollar fell below 85 yen," he adds, noting timing of next prospective \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be difficult. Says markets also closely eyeing talks between PM Kan and U.S. President Obama expected on Thursday. 25/33 Topix subindexes lower, with exporters such as Fanuc (6954.TO) down 0.8% at Y10,500, Canon (7751.TO) down 1.2% at Y3,860. Panasonic (6752.TO) up 3.5% at Y1,150 on Nikkei report it expects to cancel equity financing plan. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com) [ 09-21-10 2119ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100922e69m0000w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
