\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: 10-Year JGB Yield May Fall To 0.950% - BarCap\b0\par\par\uc2 1,529 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2010\par 02:43\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global FX & Fixed Income News\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 \u169\'00\'A9 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0043 GMT [Dow Jones] Benchmark 10-year JGB yield falls to 3-week low at 1.000%, may fall to 0.950% in coming sessions after dovish FOMC statement overnight fuels speculation that Fed likely to take further easing action ahead, forcing BOJ to follow suit, says Chotaro Morita, head of Japan fixed income strategy research at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barclays Capital\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Adds lead December futures may rise to 143.00; contract last up 0.32 at 142.70. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0038 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI may fall as investors take profits from recent gains in blue chips such as Genting (3182.KU) in absence of fresh cues; any downside, however, likely modest as stronger MYR (at fresh 13-year high), upbeat outlook for domestic economic growth to lend support, dealers say. KLCI ended +0.4% at 1475.99 yesterday. "Any pullback should be seen as a healthy correction. Most players are still looking at the 1500 psychological resistance to be tested at some point in the next few weeks," dealer says; tips KLCI to remain in 1470-1480 range today. Recent outperformers like Genting, Gamuda (5398.KU) may retreat on profit-taking. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0037 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei off 0.4% at 9562.85 following lower open as USD/JPY falls below 85 (now 84.87) after U.S. Fed signaled additional steps to prop up U.S. economy. Nikkei support seen at 9500 for today with investors still wary about possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  into \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets by Japanese authorities, says Masayoshi Yano, senior market analyst at Meiwa Securities. "I doubt the (prior executed) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be a one-off event, but it will be hard to do again immediately after the dollar fell below 85 yen," he adds, noting timing of next prospective \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be difficult. Says markets also closely eyeing talks between PM Kan and U.S. President Obama expected on Thursday. 25/33 Topix subindexes lower, with exporters such as Fanuc (6954.TO) down 0.8% at Y10,500, Canon (7751.TO) down 1.2% at Y3,860. Panasonic (6752.TO) up 3.5% at Y1,150 on Nikkei report it expects to cancel equity financing plan. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0035 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai stocks still remain attractive despite running up 30% since end of anti-government protests mid-May, says Ian Gisbourne, head of Thailand equity research at \uc2 UBS\uc2 ; he tells CNBC that Thailand's fundamentals remain strong with "manufacturing, tourism and agriculture all looking positive." House estimates Thai company earnings to rise 20% next year, but there's potential to revise that upwards. "There's certainly room for earnings to be raised from here and that means the market that, prima facie, looks expensive, isn't really that expensive." Energy, cyclical stocks still underperformers. Also says \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  clearly concerned about THB at 13-year highs vs USD, which should prompt it to leave policy rate unchanged at 1.75% for remainder of 2010 before modest increases in 2011. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0034 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ 2Q current account deficit has started to widen as expected, but impact of Canterbury earthquake likely to aid balance in coming quarters, says ASB economist Jane Turner; "We expect the overall impact (of the earthquake) will be to narrow the current account deficit temporarily, due to the one-off transfer coming from the insurance payouts." Says wealth impact of earthquake will manifest in international investment positions as state-owned insurance entity Earthquake Commission may sell some of its holdings of foreign assets, government may have to increase debt issuance to pay for public sector damage. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0019 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR falls to 3.0920 vs 3.1000 late yesterday, fresh 13-year low; pair lower in tandem with USD decline against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Dealers say breach of major 3.1000 support likely to accelerate USD/MYR's fall near-term. "We expect the next immediate support at 3.0750 to be tested soon," dealer says. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0011 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY breaks through overnight low marked after FOMC statement, hits fresh intraday low at 84.91, lowest in one week; investors sell pair amid broad USD weakness, in part to test MOF's resolve on any further USD-buying, JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Shuichi Kanehira, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 . Says key technical level at 84.40-84.50 now in sight. Meanwhile, says if MOF does again wade into markets, effectiveness of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would depend on size of USD-buying, JPY-selling. "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would have to show its resolve" for further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be effective amid broad-based USD weakness. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0010 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals face mixed signals today, says trader in Sydney after mulling statement from U.S. Federal Reserve policy committee, and its possible impact on markets. On one hand, market activity slowing in recent days, volumes low, and with China now on 3-day national holiday, this will further moderate activity, so maybe metals will "drift off a little bit." But statement suggests Fed will stimulate U.S. economy "which is good for metals," he says. Trader, who request anonymity notes that LME 3-month copper last $7,680/metric ton, near support at $7,650, which he doubts will be tested in Asia today but may well be in Europe tonight. Still, in all this, these smallish short-term indicators certainly adding to nervousness in market and under these circumstances "gravity takes over," he says. Of other base metals, tin slumped $450 yesterday to $23,000, nickel plunged $705 at $22,355, lead eased $20 at $2,175, aluminum lost $19.50 at $2,175, zinc lost $36 at $2,142. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0008 GMT [Dow Jones] Maintain neutral trading stance on front-month Nymex crude for now, but any rally toward $77/bbl area should be seen as short-term selling opportunity, says Jim Ritterbusch, president of oil trading advisory firm Ritterbusch & Associates. Oil market continuing to suffer from pangs of oversupply; increased regulatory concerns have facilitated removal of some perennial investment bank bulls from market. "Looking ahead through the balance of the week, we look for a battle between bearish oil balances and favorable macroeconomic guidance to prompt further pushing and pulling across the complex." Nymex crude for November delivery now down 18 cents at $74.79/bbl on Globex. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0005 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD ticking higher, dragged up by AUD, EUR after Fed leaves door open for more quantitative-easing measures, says \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's New Zealand Chief Manager Daniel Brdanovic; "QE down the track and off she goes." Says EUR/USD could test 1.3334 in short term, last 1.3284; "I think the market is ready to test that topside and it'll probably take Kiwi up as well." Tips NZD/USD resistance at 0.7395, pair last 0.7355. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0003 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares' recent advance may pause on flat performance on Wall Street overnight, close of key Asian markets China, South Korea for public holidays, giving investors excuse to digest gains. STI ends +0.5% at new year-to-date high of 3095.39 yesterday, +4.9% so far this month. Firm support expected at 3043 August peak, while any upside expected to meet resistance at higher end of 3085-3146 breakdown gap formed in June 2008. "There's not much in the Fed statement to excite the stock market in the near term. It's time for a pullback as prices have already gone up a fair bit in a short time," says dealer at local brokerage. Stocks likely in focus include \uc2 Mapletree Logistics Trust\uc2  (M44U.SG) given its S$305 million equity fund-raising exercise to finance acquisitions, \uc2 Soilbuild Group\uc2  (K6M.SG), which has announced delisting offer; Mapletree last ends +2.3% at S$0.88, Soilbuild resumes trade at 0100 GMT after halt since Monday; last +2.9% at S$0.705. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0001 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian government bonds may rise slightly, helped by rally in U.S. Treasurys overnight, stronger MYR; dealers say trading activity, however, may remain subdued ahead of August CPI data due 0900 GMT. CPI likely +2.0% on year in July, according to median forecast in Dow Jones poll of 11 economists. Market participants also awaiting \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announcement of 3-year Islamic bond auction expected by tomorrow. "Market expectations are for an issue size of around MYR3 billion to MYR3.5 billion," dealer says. Yesterday, yields on benchmark MGS ended mostly higher with May 2013 +3 bps at 3.17%, August 2015 down 1 bp at 3.30%, September 2017 +1 bp at 3.56%, November 2019 +1 bp at 3.65%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com) [ 09-21-10 2043ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100922e69m0000i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
