\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: USD/JPY At 1-Week Low; Market Testing MOF -Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,717 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2010\par 02:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0011 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY breaks through overnight low marked after FOMC statement, hits fresh intraday low at 84.91, lowest in one week; investors sell pair amid broad USD weakness, in part to test MOF's resolve on any further USD-buying, JPY-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , says Shuichi Kanehira, senior \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealer at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Says key technical level at 84.40-84.50 now in sight. Meanwhile, says if MOF does again wade into markets, effectiveness of further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would depend on size of USD-buying, JPY-selling. "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would have to show its resolve" for further \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to be effective amid broad-based USD weakness. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0010 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals face mixed signals today, says trader in Sydney after mulling statement from U.S. Federal Reserve policy committee, and its possible impact on markets. On one hand, market activity slowing in recent days, volumes low, and with China now on 3-day national holiday, this will further moderate activity, so maybe metals will "drift off a little bit." But statement suggests Fed will stimulate U.S. economy "which is good for metals," he says. Trader, who request anonymity notes that LME 3-month copper last $7,680/metric ton, near support at $7,650, which he doubts will be tested in Asia today but may well be in Europe tonight. Still, in all this, these smallish short-term indicators certainly adding to nervousness in market and under these circumstances "gravity takes over," he says. Of other base metals, tin slumped $450 yesterday to $23,000, nickel plunged $705 at $22,355, lead eased $20 at $2,175, aluminum lost $19.50 at $2,175, zinc lost $36 at $2,142. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0008 GMT [Dow Jones] Maintain neutral trading stance on front-month Nymex crude for now, but any rally toward $77/bbl area should be seen as short-term selling opportunity, says Jim Ritterbusch, president of oil trading advisory firm Ritterbusch & Associates. Oil market continuing to suffer from pangs of oversupply; increased regulatory concerns have facilitated removal of some perennial investment bank bulls from market. "Looking ahead through the balance of the week, we look for a battle between bearish oil balances and favorable macroeconomic guidance to prompt further pushing and pulling across the complex." Nymex crude for November delivery now down 18 cents at $74.79/bbl on Globex. (david.winning@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0005 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD ticking higher, dragged up by AUD, EUR after Fed leaves door open for more quantitative-easing measures, says \uc2 HSBC\uc2 's New Zealand Chief Manager Daniel Brdanovic; "QE down the track and off she goes." Says EUR/USD could test 1.3334 in short term, last 1.3284; "I think the market is ready to test that topside and it'll probably take Kiwi up as well." Tips NZD/USD resistance at 0.7395, pair last 0.7355. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0003 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore shares' recent advance may pause on flat performance on Wall Street overnight, close of key Asian markets China, South Korea for public holidays, giving investors excuse to digest gains. STI ends +0.5% at new year-to-date high of 3095.39 yesterday, +4.9% so far this month. Firm support expected at 3043 August peak, while any upside expected to meet resistance at higher end of 3085-3146 breakdown gap formed in June 2008. "There's not much in the Fed statement to excite the stock market in the near term. It's time for a pullback as prices have already gone up a fair bit in a short time," says dealer at local brokerage. Stocks likely in focus include \uc2 Mapletree Logistics Trust\uc2  (M44U.SG) given its S$305 million equity fund-raising exercise to finance acquisitions, \uc2 Soilbuild Group\uc2  (K6M.SG), which has announced delisting offer; Mapletree last ends +2.3% at S$0.88, Soilbuild resumes trade at 0100 GMT after halt since Monday; last +2.9% at S$0.705. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0001 GMT [Dow Jones] Malaysian government bonds may rise slightly, helped by rally in U.S. Treasurys overnight, stronger MYR; dealers say trading activity, however, may remain subdued ahead of August CPI data due 0900 GMT. CPI likely +2.0% on year in July, according to median forecast in Dow Jones poll of 11 economists. Market participants also awaiting \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  announcement of 3-year Islamic bond auction expected by tomorrow. "Market expectations are for an issue size of around MYR3 billion to MYR3.5 billion," dealer says. Yesterday, yields on benchmark MGS ended mostly higher with May 2013 +3 bps at 3.17%, August 2015 down 1 bp at 3.30%, September 2017 +1 bp at 3.56%, November 2019 +1 bp at 3.65%. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2358 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 2348 GMT misstated a technical range. The correct version is as follows:) S&P/ASX 200 likely quiet today with China closed for the rest of the week and Wall Street failing to see decisive moment. U.S. price action reflected a lot of indecision, although DJIA, S&P 500 stayed above former range highs near 10720 and 1130, respectively. With Wall Street sustaining the break of those resistance levels, albeit on minimal volume, the Australian market may continue consolidating in the 4603.6-4670.1 range unless bad news arises. But a downside break would be cause for concern because each time the S&P/ASX 200 has turned down from the 4600-4650 area since May, it has continued falling to the 4175-4314 area. Daily parabolic has been a reasonably accurate guide to the selloffs and it comes in near 4600 today, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. "Last ditch" support lies at the former neckline resistance of the inverse head & shoulders pattern, at 4580-4585. Index last at 4617.5. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2355 GMT [Dow Jones] NZX-50 down 0.1% at 3233.68 as market lacks major corporate news, says \uc2 Forsyth Barr\uc2  broker Suzanne Kinnaird; "I think we will be watching what Australia does for some leads and I think that might point us in the direction that we close." Adds \uc2 Guinness Peat Group\uc2  (GPG.NZ) +3.0% at NZ$0.68 as market reacts positively to news of appointment of 4 independent directors; "it has always been a question of who would be appointed, the calibre of them and exactly how independent they would be. The strength of the share price indicates there is some acceptance that they are good quality candidates." Adds ING Medical Properties (ING.NZ) down 2.4% of NZ$1.22 as market reacts to news it may acquire assets of Essential Healthcare Trust; "the market is waiting for further details." (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2349 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's economic prospects get most of credit in AUD/USD's recent run to a new 2-year high, but U.S. factors just as crucial and will support more gains today, says Melbourne-based \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer. Most notable was Fed's overnight downgrade to U.S. inflation outlook, which especially crucial given Australia's growing inflationary issue. "The AUD is expected to outperform the USD after last night's downgrade in the US inflation forecast." AUD/USD recently at 0.9555, above 0.95 for first time in 26 months. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2348 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 likely quiet today with China closed for the rest of the week and Wall Street failing to see decisive moment. U.S. price action reflected a lot of indecision, although DJIA, S&P 500 stayed above former range highs near 10720 and 1130, respectively. With Wall Street sustaining the break of those resistance levels, albeit on minimal volume, the Australian market may continue consolidating in the 4603.6-4670.1 range unless bad news arises. But a downside break would be cause for concern because each time the S&P/ASX 200 has turned down from the 4600-4650 area since May, it has continued falling to the 4175-4314 area. Daily parabolic has been a reasonably accurate guide to the selloff and it comes in near 4600 today, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. "Last ditch" support lies at the former neckline resistance of the inverse head & shoulders pattern, at 4580-4685. Index last at 4617.5. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2345 GMT [Dow Jones] Nymex front-month crude likely to range-trade between $72.75-$75.45 (Friday's low-Monday's high), Dow Jones technical analysis shows. Daily continuation chart mixed as MACD still in bullish mode, but stochastics neutral. Fall below $72.75 support would target $72.63 (Sept. 7 low), then $71.53 (Aug. 31 reaction low) and $70.76 (Aug. 25 reaction low). But rise above $75.45 resistance would tilt near-term outlook positive, targeting $75.59 (100-day moving average), then $76.36 (55-day moving average), $77.42 (200-day moving average) and $78.04 (Sept. 13 reaction high). November crude last down 15 cents at $74.82/bbl on Globex. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2345 GMT [Dow Jones] STOCK CALL: Auto parts maker Denso (6902.TO) may rise, outperform broader indexes despite weak USD/JPY concerns as \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  ups to Buy from Neutral and adds to its Conviction Buy list, traders say. Goldman says company increasing market share in U.S. market as it provides parts for GM, Ford, Chrysler, benefiting from demand in addition to \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  (7203.TO); notes 25% of North American sales come from "Big 3" U.S. makers. Adds Denso's profit growth in next few years likely larger than any other auto parts maker in Japan. Ups target price to Y3,300 vs Y3,000. Shares ask/bid at Y2,500/Y2,499 vs yesterday close of Y2,453.(ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com) [ 09-21-10 2011ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100922e69m0000b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
