\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Slip; DJIA Breaks Five-Session Winning Streak\b0\par\par\uc2 2,056 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 September 2010\par 00:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 China and Hong Kong markets are closed for the Mid-Autumn Festival Thursday, Japan markets are closed for Autumn Equinox Day, and South Korea markets are closed for the Chusok Holiday.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10739.31  -21.72  -0.20% \par Nasdaq     2334.55  -14.80  -0.63% \par S&P 500    1134.28   -5.50  -0.48% \par FTSE 100   5551.91  -24.28  -0.44% \par Xetra DAX  6208.33  -67.65  -1.08% \par  \par 2Y Tsy   99 29/32 flat  0.428% flat \par 5Y Tsy   99 23/32 -1/32 1.311% +1.0BP \par 10Y Tsy 100 22/32 +8/32 2.546% -3.0BP \par  \par USD/JPY       84.50-54     -0.61% \par Range         85.18-84.28 \par EUR/USD       1.3391-96    +0.97% \par Range         1.3440-1.3248 \par AUD/USD       0.9560-65    +0.13% \par Range         0.9599-0.9510 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol    10.60-10.90% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol    11.29-11.61% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol    12.50-13.05% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,292.10 +$17.80 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,287.50-$1,298.00 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $74.71   -$0.26 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks slipped Wednesday, with \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  and \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2  leading the decline as investors worried about the Federal Reserve's Tuesday suggestion that the economy may need additional stimulative measures.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average dropped 21.72, or 0.20%, to 10739.31, breaking a five-session winning streak and marking its biggest one-day drop since Sept. 7. The measure is up 7.2% for the month.\par \par \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  was the Dow's worst performer, off 54 cents, or 2.2%, to 24.61, as the software company launched a bond issue worth at least $4.75 billion. The Dow's financial components were also weak, with \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  down 23 cents, or 1.7%, to 13.42, and \uc2 J.P. Morgan\uc2  off 65 cents, or 1.6%, to 39.94.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite fell 14.80, or 0.63%, to 2334.55. The Standard & Poor's 500 index shed 5.50, or 0.48%, to 1134.28, led by its financial sector.\par \par \uc2 The declines came as investors continued to digest a Tuesday statement from the Fed in which its officials offered their clearest signal yet that they are laying the groundwork for new steps to bolster the recovery if they don't see growth pick up soon or if inflation falls further.\par \par \uc2 "The market really badly wants to push higher, however today, the information from the Fed is weighing heavily on it," said David Hefty, chief executive at Hefty Wealth Partners.\par \par \uc2 Hefty added that while it was encouraging to see the S&P 500 closed above 1130, which was a recent technical resistance level that now appears to be providing support, he is concerned the market may be getting overbought. The S&P 500 is up 8.1% this month, on track for one of its best historical performances for September.\par \par \uc2 Singapore shares ended flat Wednesday on profit-taking after recent gains, with analysts saying the market might enter a consolidation phase as participants look for fresh leads on how risk appetite will pan out.\par \par \uc2 "The near-term upside is likely capped" for the Straits Times Index, CIMB said in a note.\par \par \uc2 "It's time for a pullback as prices have already gone up a fair bit in a short time," a dealer at a local brokerage said.\par \par \uc2 Crude-oil futures settled lower in U.S. trading Wednesday after an unexpected increase in U.S. stockpiles of oil and fuel products.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures continued their march into record territory Wednesday, nearing the $1,300 mark as speculation that the Federal Reserve will flood the market with cash enhanced gold's appeal as an alternative asset.\par \par \uc2 Base metals closed higher on the London Metal Exchange Wednesday after a weak U.S. dollar drove gains across the commodity markets and sent copper to fresh five-month highs.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar dropped to its lowest level against the euro since April as investors broadly ditched the greenback Wednesday on speculation the Federal Reserve could soon act to kick-start a slowing U.S. economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Talk of a fresh round of growth-stimulating asset purchases, known as quantitative easing, weighed heavily on the greenback, which has suffered as people use cheap dollars to fund bets on higher-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar weakened even against the low-yielding yen to its lowest mark since last week's massive Y2 trillion \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , dipping below Y84.50 and putting investors on watch for another round of yen-weakening \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , slumped to its lowest level since this March, while the greenback fell to its lowest level since March 2008 against the Swiss franc.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fed's specific mention of weaker-than-targeted inflation has added a weight around the dollar's already stressed neck, said Sacha Tihanyi, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Scotia Capital in Toronto.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The overarching reason to dislike the dollar has broadened," he said. "Where recently it was a loss in growth momentum, it now becomes the fear that policy will ease further and not be normalized for some time, along with the recognition that the [Fed] is certainly worried about the prospects for deflation," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Wednesday in New York, the euro was at $1.3394, up from $1.3247 late Tuesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y84.54 down from Y85.12, while the euro was at Y113.22, up from Y112.75. The U.K. pound was at $1.5669, up from $1.5627. The dollar was at CHF0.9864, down from CHF0.9976.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index dropped nearly 1%, sinking to 79.812 from 80.442 late Tuesday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The fear of more quantitative easing by the Fed has been enough to move the needle on the dollar, even if investors are ultimately wrong on their assumption of a fresh round of stimulus, said Geoffrey Yu, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 UBS\f28 \uc2 \f2  in London. The verbal posturing by the Fed has done its job, he said. "The Fed is saying something negative on the dollar right now, in an effort to lower it," Yu said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro took advantage of the dollar's woes, with the common \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  gaining nearly 1% as it also benefited from a successful auction of Portuguese government debt.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro/dollar is likely to keep gaining in Asia, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Strategist Imre Speizer said. "It is currently the classic anti-dollar instrument. It's got momentum on its side. It is getting a bit over bought but it still has a lot of momentum behind it."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With the dollar also dipping below the Y85 mark, coming off last week's post-\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  highs, the threat of a new round of Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  loomed.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There were no signs of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the market Wednesday, even as investors pushed the dollar lower, said Hidetoshi Yanagihara, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some yen watchers said the Japanese government might wait to act until a meeting takes place Thursday between President Barack Obama and Japan's Prime Minister Naoto Kan. "Anything less would surely be a finger in the eye of the president," said David Gilmore, a partner at \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics in Essex, Conn.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Long-dated Treasurys rose for a fourth straight session Wednesday as speculation continued to mount that the Federal Reserve will buy bonds at a larger scale in coming months to support the economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors bet that such maturities are the main targets for the Fed to push down long-term borrowing costs for U.S. households, businesses and the government. The 10-year note's yield, a benchmark for consumer and corporate borrowings, has dropped more than 20 basis points over the past four trading days.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shorter-dated Treasurys also underperformed as new supply looms. The Treasury Department will release the sizes for next week's auctions for two-year, five-year and seven-year notes auctions Thursday. Some market participants forecast $100 billion in total, down from $102 billion in August.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 1922 GMT, the benchmark 10-year Treasury note was up 8/32 in price and its yield fell to 2.546%. The 30-year bond was 26/32 higher to yield 3.740%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The two-year note was little changed at 0.428% after earlier touching a record low of 0.403%. The five-year note was down 1/32 to yield 1.311%. Bond prices move inversely to their yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bets on large-scale purchases in Treasurys from the Fed, known as quantitative easing, have increased since Friday's data showing a key gauge of inflation stayed below 1% for a fifth consecutive month. The reading was well below the implicit target of 1.5% to 2% from the Fed, fueling concern about deflation, or a persistent fall in consumer prices.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Such wagers got an extra boost after Fed policymakers, in its latest rate policy meeting Tuesday afternoon, flagged deflation risks and hinted that they are prepared to provide more monetary stimulus.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The threat of [more quantitative easing] will keep interest rates low across the curve," said James Barnes, senior portfolio manager at National Penn Investors Trust Company in Wyomissing, Pa., who manages $1 billion in fixed-income assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Charles Comiskey, head of Treasury trading at Scotia Capital in New York, said the timing for large-scale purchases could come in the November rate policy meeting should the economy remain subpar and unemployment rate remain high, which may push the 10-year note's yield to a lower range of 2%-2.25%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among the data due in Thursday's holiday quietened session, Singapore's consumer price index likely rose 3.3% in August, compared with a 3.1% rise in July, due to higher transportation and food prices, the median forecast in a Dow Jones poll of six economists shows. [ 22-09-10 2203GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On the month, seasonally adjusted CPI is likely to have risen 0.3% compared with July's 0.5% rise, a poll of three economists shows.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US House Prices Fall 0.5% In July \par China's Wen Wants To Improve China-US Relations \par US Must Sell GM At $133.78/Shr To Break Even \par Crisis Damage Repair A Long Way Off - Geithner \par Volcker Says Finreg Misses Three Issues \par Lehman Increases Its Creditor-Recovery Estimate \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Lufthansa\f28 \uc2 \f2  Names CEO; Orders EUR3.5B Of Aircraft \par Dingell Letter Urges China Change Tech Demand \par US Seeks To Halt 'Hedgeloan' Scheme \par =Gastar Enters JV With Korean Firm To Develop Marcellus Assets \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2245  New Zealand  2Q  GDP \par 2245  New Zealand  Aug International Visitor Arrivals \par 0500  Singapore    Aug CPI \par 0630  India        Weekly WPI (for fuel products and primary \par                    articles) \par 0645  France       Sep Business survey (goods-producing industries) \par 0700  France       Sep Flash PMI \par 0730  Germany      Sep Flash PMI \par 0800  Philippines  Bangko Sentral ng Pilipinas - Philippine Monetary \par                    Policy meeting highlights \par 0800  Taiwan       Aug Employment / Unemployment \par 0800  Taiwan       Aug Industrial output \par 0800  EU           Sep Euro-zone Flash PMI \par 0830  UK           Aug Major British Banking Groups stats \par 0830  UK           Aug Automotive Production \par 1230  US           Sep 18 Unemployment Insurance Claims Report - Initial Claims \par 1340  US           Chicago Fed Pres Evans gives welcoming remarks at Chicago \par                    Fed/IMF banking conference in Chicago \par 1400  US           Aug Mass Layoffs \par 1400  US           Aug Leading Indicators \par 1400  US           Aug Existing Home Sales \par 1400  US           Sep 11 DJ-BTMU U.S. Business Barometer \par 1430  US           Sep 17 EIA Natural Gas Storage Report \par 1700  US           Chicago Fed Pres Evans introduces Paul Volcker at Chicago \par                    Fed/IMF banking conference in Chicago \par 2030  US           Money Stock Measures \par 2030  US           Sep 22 Foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Holdings \par 2030  US           Sep 22 Federal Discount Window Borrowings \par N/A   Japan   