\par\par \b \uc2 INDIAN MORNING BRIEFING: Sensex Likely To Be Rangebound\b0\par\par\uc2 1,643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2010\par 05:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 China markets and Taiwan markets are closed Wednesday for Mid-Autumn Festival, while South Korean markets are closed for Chusok Holiday.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par DJIA                10761.03  +7.41    +0.07% \par Nasdaq              2349.35   -6.48    -0.28% \par S&P 500             1139.78   -2.93    -0.26% \par FTSE 100            5576.19   -26.35   -0.47% \par Nikkei Stock        9605.12   +3.01    +0.03% \par Hang Seng           22108.02  +105.43  +0.48% \par Kospi               n/a       n/a      n/a \par SGX Nifty           6030.00   +15.00   +0.24% \par Sept. contract \par  \par USD/JPY             85.02-03     -0.81% \par Range               85.77-84.99 \par EUR/USD             1.3264-67    +1.55% \par Range               1.3281-1.3059 \par  \par CBOT Wheat Dec.     $7.180 per bushel \par Comex Gold (electronic trade) $1,287.70 +$6.90 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $73.52   -$1.34 \par  \par  \par CHARTING THE SENSEX \f28 \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bombay Stock Exchange\uc2 's 30-share Sensitive Index is likely to trade in a 19,850-20,100 range, according to charts.\par \par \uc2 The completion of a "high wave" candlestick pattern Tuesday is a sign of indecision. A breakout above minor resistance at 20,100 could trigger a rise to major resistance at 20,220.\par \par \uc2 But a fall below 19,850 would be a sign of weakness, and may push the Sensex to major support at 19,650.\par \par \uc2 The National Stock Exchange's 50-share Nifty could be confined to a 5,970-6,025 range.\par \par \uc2 A shift out of this range would set the tone for the next major move. Above 6,025, the Nifty could target major resistance at 6,080, while a fall below 5,970 may result in a test of major support at 5,940.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Blue-chip stocks finished slightly higher for a fifth-straight session Tuesday, led by Caterpillar, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hewlett-Packard\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Intel, after the Federal Reserve hinted it could take additional measures to stimulate economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Caterpillar powered the Dow's gains, rising 2.2%. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hewlett-Packard\f28 \uc2 \f2  gained 1.4% and Intel rose 1.1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fed concluded its one-day policy meeting without announcing additional steps to boost the recovery. But \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials signaled they are uncomfortable with the recent low levels of inflation and said they're prepared to take additional action to boost the economic recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It looks like the Fed is keeping its foot on the economic gas pedal but hasn't yet pushed the turbo button," said Burt White, chief investment officer at LPL Financial. "They definitely showed a lot of restraint and frankly confidence that the economy is sort of plowing through a soft spot and not headed toward a double dip."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financials were the S&P 500's biggest-declining sector Tuesday, falling 1.03%. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Legg Mason\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell 2.9% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  declined 2.9%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stock markets were mixed Wednesday, with Japanese exporters falling on the yen's strength against the dollar after the Federal Reserve said Tuesday it was ready to do more to recharge the U.S. economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Nikkei Share Average and Australia's S&P/ASX 200 were both tad up, while New Zealand's NZX-50 was down. South Korean markets were closed for a public holiday. Dow Jones Industrial Average futures were off one point in screen trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese exporters were weakened as the yen strengthened to its highest levels against the U.S. dollar in a week. All eye were now on Tokyo authorities to see if they would step into the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to weaken the yen again.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among exporters, Fanuc was down 0.8% and Canon lost 1.2%. But Panasonic rose 3.6% on a report in the Nikkei that the company was likely to drop its plan to issue new shares to finance tender offers to turn \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sanyo Electric\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Panasonic Electric Works\f28 \uc2 \f2  into fully-owned subsidiaries.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Sydney market was characterized by mixed trade after Wall Street's indecisive performance. Materials, telcos and property trusts were underperforming, while stocks related to energy, industrials, consumer staples, healthcare and utilities were outperforming.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CSL was up 2.8%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Newcrest Mining\f28 \uc2 \f2  was 0.6% higher, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wesfarmers\f28 \uc2 \f2  rose 1.1% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2  added 0.8%. Losers included BHP Billiton, down 0.3%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rio Tinto\f28 \uc2 \f2  0.7% lower and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Telstra\f28 \uc2 \f2  shed 1.1%.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was buying Y84.97, from Y82.12 in late New York trade Tuesday while the euro was at Y112.85 from Y112.75. The single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was fetching $1.3285 against the dollar, from $1.3247.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold futures set a new record high in electronic activity after the settlement Tuesday on the view that the Federal Reserve would initiate another round of government asset purchases to stimulate the economy, boosting gold's value as an alternative asset. The contract had settled lower Tuesday. Spot gold was at 1,287.90 per troy ounce, up $1.10 from its New York close.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Base metals closed lower Tuesday as cautious investors await the outcome of a meeting of the Federal Reserve's rate-setting committee.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OIL SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 November Nymex crude oil futures were down 15 cents at $74.82 per barrel on Globex.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CALENDAR OF EVENTS Sept. 22 \par 0900 IST New Delhi:   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Power Finance Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . chairman, BHEL director, Tata \par                       \f28 \uc2 \f2 BP Solar\f28 \uc2 \f2  sales and marketing head, Indo Solar \par                       chief technology officer at renewable energy \par                       India forum \par 1030 IST New Delhi:   GAIL annual shareholders' meet \par 1200 IST Gurgaon:     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bharti Telecom\f28 \uc2 \f2  annual shareholders' meeting \par 1100 IST New Delhi:   Precision Electronics chairman, TCS Innovation Lab \par                       head at seminar on battlefield management system \par 1200 IST New Delhi:   Junior power minister at LED products manufacturers \par                       association event \par 1200 IST New Delhi:   DLF Pramerica managing director and chief executive \par                       press meet \par 1300 IST New Delhi:   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Union Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  chairman and managing director, \par                       executive director at event \par 1500 IST New Delhi:   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Indian Oil\f28 \uc2 \f2  chairman at press meet \par 1500 IST New Delhi:   Bell Ceramics managing director, executive director \par                       at press meet \par 1530 IST New Delhi:   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nestle India\f28 \uc2 \f2  chairman and managing director at press \par                       meet \par 0830 GMT UK:          September Bank of England monetary policy committee \par                       meeting minutes \par 0830 GMT UK:          September Agents' summary of business conditions \par 0900 GMT EU:          July industrial new orders \par 1000 GMT EU:          European Parliament votes on new EU financial \par                       supervisory framework \par 1100 GMT US:          Sept. 17 MBA mortgage applications survey \par 1400 GMT EU:          September FCCI Flash consumer confidence indicator \par 1800 GMT US:          Treasury secretary testifies before house financial \par                       services committee on state of international financial \par                       system, including international regulatory issues \par                       relevant to implementation of Dodd-Frank Act \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES - Dow Jones/WSJ \par =WSJ UPDATE: SINGAPORE PM: CHINA 'NEED NOT GO SLOW' ON YUAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore Prime Minister Lee Hsien Loong said a faster appreciation of China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  would be "helpful" for the region, but cautioned that further pressure from foreign governments might make it harder for Beijing to let the yuan rise. (published 2202 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ SHIRAKAWA: \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX INTERVENTION\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  DECISION WAS APPROPRIATE - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa said that the government's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets was the right course of action given the country's economic conditions, according to an interview published Wednesday in the Yomiuri Shimbun. (published 0141 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par HKMA KEEPS BASE RATE AT 0.50% FOLLOWING FED STANDING PAT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Hong Kong \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  kept its base rate at 0.50% Wednesday following the U.S. Federal Open Market Committee's decision overnight to keep its policy rate steady. (published 0013 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CBI RAISES 2010 UK GROWTH FORECAST, LOWERS 2011 VIEW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Confederation of British Industry said Wednesday it had raised its forecast for U.K. economic growth this year, but lowered its prediction for 2011 due to the government's efforts to cut spending and shrink the budget deficit. (published 2301 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =WSJ: OBAMA TAX CREDIT LOOKS ENDANGERED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 President Barack Obama's signature tax policy, known as the Making Work Pay credit, might not continue past this year, just like the Bush-era tax cuts. (published 2345 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PANASONIC: STILL MULLING WAYS TO FINANCE SANYO, OTHER UNIT BUY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Panasonic Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Wednesday said it is still considering methods of financing the purchase of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sanyo Electric Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 . and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Panasonic Electric Works\f28 \uc2 \f2  Co. . (published 0047 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par HEALTHSCOPE SHAREHOLDERS APPROVE TAKEOVER BY TPG, CARLYLE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Healthscope Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . shareholders on Wednesday approved a A$2.7 billion takeover of the private hospital operator by private equity firms \f28 \uc2 \f2 TPG Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 . and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Carlyle Group LP\f28 \uc2 \f2 . (published 0127 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UPDATE: DOJ TRIES TO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  IN `OF-LABEL' CASE AGAINST PFIZER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The U.S. Justice Department is trying to take the lead in a lawsuit accusing \f28 \uc2 \f2 Pfizer Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wyeth\f28 \uc2 \f2  unit of improperly promoting an organ-transplant drug for unauthorized uses and paying illegal kickbacks to doctors. (published 0112 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =2ND UPDATE: \f28 \uc2 \f2 ADOBE SYSTEMS\f28 \uc2 \f2  3Q PROFIT UP 69% ON RECORD REVENUE; 4Q VIEW WEAK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Adobe Systems Inc\f28 \uc2 \f2 .'s fiscal third-quarter profit jumped 69% on record revenue as sales of its flagship Creative Suite 5 remained strong. (published 2312 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par =MICROSOFT RAISES QUARTERLY DIVIDEND 23% TO 16 CENTS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 22-09-10 0301GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Microsoft Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . raised its quarterly dividend three pennies to 16 cents, a 23% boost. (published 2329 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES - Indian Media \par India, US Likely To Sign $3.5 Bln Defense Deal - Economic Times \par Embraer Plans To Sell 55 Planes In India Over 10 Years - Hindustan Times \par Oswal Chemicals To Invest INR50 Bln On Fertilizer Plant In Andhra Pradesh - PTI \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 ArcelorMittal\f28 \uc2 \f2  Signs MoU With Villagers For Land In Bokaro - Economic Times \par JSW Bengal May Merge With Parent After Completion Of Steel Project - Economic Times \par India's \f28 \uc2 \f2 Karur Vysya Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  Plans INR4.5 Bln Rights Issue - PTI \par India's Ranbaxy Get 6-Month Exclusivity On Generic Alzheimer's Drug - Business Standard \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Tech Mahindra\f28 \uc2 \f2 , Satyam Merger Could Be Completed In 6-8 Months - Financial Express \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 22-09-10 0301GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 62500   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100922e69m0005b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
