\par\par \b \uc2 INDIAN MORNING BRIEFING: Sensex Likely To Be Rangebound\b0\par\par\uc2 1,643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2010\par 05:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Energy Service\par \uc2 NRG\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 China markets and Taiwan markets are closed Wednesday for Mid-Autumn Festival, while South Korean markets are closed for Chusok Holiday.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GLOBAL MARKETS \par DJIA                10761.03  +7.41    +0.07% \par Nasdaq              2349.35   -6.48    -0.28% \par S&P 500             1139.78   -2.93    -0.26% \par FTSE 100            5576.19   -26.35   -0.47% \par Nikkei Stock        9605.12   +3.01    +0.03% \par Hang Seng           22108.02  +105.43  +0.48% \par Kospi               n/a       n/a      n/a \par SGX Nifty           6030.00   +15.00   +0.24% \par Sept. contract \par  \par USD/JPY             85.02-03     -0.81% \par Range               85.77-84.99 \par EUR/USD             1.3264-67    +1.55% \par Range               1.3281-1.3059 \par  \par CBOT Wheat Dec.     $7.180 per bushel \par Comex Gold (electronic trade) $1,287.70 +$6.90 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $73.52   -$1.34 \par  \par  \par CHARTING THE SENSEX \f28 \par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Bombay Stock Exchange\uc2 's 30-share Sensitive Index is likely to trade in a 19,850-20,100 range, according to charts.\par \par \uc2 The completion of a "high wave" candlestick pattern Tuesday is a sign of indecision. A breakout above minor resistance at 20,100 could trigger a rise to major resistance at 20,220.\par \par \uc2 But a fall below 19,850 would be a sign of weakness, and may push the Sensex to major support at 19,650.\par \par \uc2 The National Stock Exchange's 50-share Nifty could be confined to a 5,970-6,025 range.\par \par \uc2 A shift out of this range would set the tone for the next major move. Above 6,025, the Nifty could target major resistance at 6,080, while a fall below 5,970 may result in a test of major support at 5,940.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par US STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Blue-chip stocks finished slightly higher for a fifth-straight session Tuesday, led by Caterpillar, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hewlett-Packard\f28 \uc2 \f2  and Intel, after the Federal Reserve hinted it could take additional measures to stimulate economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Caterpillar powered the Dow's gains, rising 2.2%. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Hewlett-Packard\f28 \uc2 \f2  gained 1.4% and Intel rose 1.1%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Fed concluded its one-day policy meeting without announcing additional steps to boost the recovery. But \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials signaled they are uncomfortable with the recent low levels of inflation and said they're prepared to take additional action to boost the economic recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It looks like the Fed is keeping its foot on the economic gas pedal but hasn't yet pushed the turbo button," said Burt White, chief investment officer at LPL Financial. "They definitely showed a lot of restraint and frankly confidence that the economy is sort of plowing through a soft spot and not headed toward a double dip."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Financials were the S&P 500's biggest-declining sector Tuesday, falling 1.03%. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Legg Mason\f28 \uc2 \f2  fell 2.9% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  declined 2.9%.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par ASIAN STOCKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stock markets were mixed Wednesday, with Japanese exporters falling on the yen's strength against the dollar after the Federal Reserve said Tuesday it was ready to do more to recharge the U.S. economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's Nikkei Share Average and Australia's S&P/ASX 200 were both tad up, while New Zealand's NZX-50 was down. South Korean markets were closed for a public holiday. Dow Jones Industrial Average futures were off one point in screen trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese exporters were weakened as the yen strengthened to its highest levels against the U.S. dollar in a week. All eye were now on Tokyo authorities to see if they would step into the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets to weaken the yen again.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among exporters, Fanuc was down 0.8% and Canon lost 1.2%. But Panasonic rose 3.6% on a report in the Nikkei that the company was likely to drop its plan to issue new shares to finance tender offers to turn \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sanyo Electric\f28 \uc2 \f2  and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Panasonic Electric Works\f28 \uc2 \f2  into fully-owned subsidiaries.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Sydney market was characterized by mixed trade after Wall Street's indecisive performance. Materials, telcos and property trusts were underperforming, while stocks related to energy, industrials, consumer staples, healthcare and utilities were outperforming.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 CSL was up 2.8%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Newcrest Mining\f28 \uc2 \f2  was 0.6% higher, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wesfarmers\f28 \uc2 \f2  rose 1.1% and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Westpac\f28 \uc2 \f2  added 0.8%. Losers included BHP Billiton, down 0.3%, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Rio Tinto\f28 \uc2 \f2  0.7% lower and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Telstra\f28 \uc2 \f2  shed 1.1%.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar was buying Y84.97, from Y82.12 in late New York trade Tuesday while the euro was at Y112.85 from Y112.75. The single \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was fetching $1.3285 against the dollar, from $1.3247.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par METALS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold futures set a new record high in electronic activity after the settlement Tuesday on the view that the Federal Reserve would initiate another round of government asset purchases to stimulate the economy, boosting gold's value as an alternative asset. The contract had settled lower Tuesday. Spot gold was at 1,287.90 per troy ounce, up $1.10 from its New York close.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Base metals closed lower Tuesday as cautious investors await the outcome of a meeting of the Federal Reserve's rate-setting committee.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par OIL SUMMARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 November Nymex crude oil futures were down 15 cents at $74.82 per barrel on Globex.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par CALENDAR OF EVENTS Sept. 22 \par 0900 IST New Delhi:   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Power Finance Corp\f28 \uc2 \f2 . chairman, BHEL director, Tata \par                       \f28 \uc2 \f2 BP Solar\f28 \uc2 \f2  sales and marketing head, Indo Solar \par                       chief technology officer at renewable energy \par                       India forum \par 1030 IST New Delhi:   GAIL annual shareholders' meet \par 1200 IST Gurgaon:     \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bharti Telecom\f28 \uc2 \f2  annual shareholders' meeting \par 1100 IST New Delhi:   Precision Electronics chairman, TCS Innovation Lab \par                       head at seminar on battlefield management system \par 1200 IST New Delhi:   Junior power minister at LED products manufacturers \par                       association event \par 1200 IST New Delhi:   DLF Pramerica managing director and chief executive \par                       press meet \par 1300 IST New Delhi:   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Union Bank of India\f28 \uc2 \f2  chairman and managing director, \par                       executive director at event \par 1500 IST New Delhi:   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Indian Oil\f28 \uc2 \f2  chairman at press meet \par 1500 IST New Delhi:   Bell Ceramics managing director, executive director \par                       at press meet \par 1530 IST New Delhi:   \f28 \uc2 \f2 Nestle India\f28 \uc2 \f2  chairman and managing director at press \par                       meet \par 0830 GMT UK:          September Bank of England monetary policy committee \par                       meeting minutes \par 0830 GMT UK:          September Agents' summary of business conditions \par 09