\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 2-Japan PM warns of more action vs yen, BOJ eyes easing\b0\par\par\uc2 786 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2010\par 06:26\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "unavoidable" if drastic \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves - PM Kan\par \par \uc2  * BOJ Governor signals won't drain funds if \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  * BOJ eyes easing next month but no consensus on next step\par \par \uc2  * BOJ Miyao says boosting bond buying one of policy options  (Adds BOJ board member Miyao's comments)\par \par \uc2  By Leika Kihara\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Sept 22 (Reuters) - Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan said Tokyo was ready to act again if the yen moved sharply, keeping traders on guard against further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as expectations of U.S. monetary easing weighed on the dollar.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last week by selling yen for the first time in more than six years as its surge to a 15-year high against the dollar threatened to derail Japan's slowing economy and worsen deflation.\par \par \uc2  In an interview with the Financial Times published on Wednesday, Kan said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be unavoidable if there were a drastic change in the yen exchange rate.\par \par \uc2  The comments coincided with the dollar's drop below 85 yen to its weakest level since last week's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , after the Federal Reserve signalled it was ready to stimulate the U.S. economy more.\par \par \uc2  Traders said the yen was still below levels that would trigger another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but more yen selling could not be ruled out.\par \par \uc2  "I don't think markets are bracing for imminent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with the dollar still above 84.00 yen. But if the dollar falls further to test 82 yen, markets will focus on whether authorities will step in again to defend that level," said Ayako Sera, market strategist at \uc2 Sumitomo Trust & Banking\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  "Japanese authorities will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the event of sharp market moves, regardless of whether Kan will be away from Japan or not."\par \par \uc2  Kan, who is travelling to New York this week for a U.N. General Assembly meeting, said there was an agreement among G20 nations that overly rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  movements were undesirable, and that he would seek to defend Japan's action.\par \par \uc2  Bank of Japan Governor Masaaki Shirakawa declared further support for the country's economy, saying in a newspaper interview the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would provide ample funds to the market.\par \par \uc2  That would include liquidity supplied through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Shirakawa said, suggesting that the BOJ would continue to refrain from draining funds released into the market when authorities sell the yen.\par \par \uc2  The BOJ also stands ready to ease policy further at its next rate review on Oct. 4-5, although there is a debate within the bank on what exactly it should do next with its policy options limited, sources familiar with the bank's thinking said.\par \par \uc2  BOJ'S OPTIONS\par \par \uc2  Those options include increasing government bond purchases, buying private sector assets or expanding a cheap fund-supply scheme targeting growth industries, sources say.\par \par \uc2  Shirakawa told the Yomiuri newspaper that greater uncertainty about the global economy meant increased risks to Japan's recovery, a warning echoed by BOJ board member Ryuzo Miyao.\par \par \uc2  Miyao, who joined the board in March, told business leaders in western Japan that a possible extended spell of sluggish U.S. economic growth could force the BOJ to alter its forecast of a sustained moderate recovery in Japan's economy.\par \par \uc2  He also said the BOJ was not ruling out any policy option, including increasing its government bond buying from the current 21.6 trillion yen ($254.5 billion) per year.\par \par \uc2  With its hands tied by public debt double the size of Japan's $5 trillion economy, the government has mainly counted on the BOJ to come up with ways of revving up the sagging economy.\par \par \uc2  But Kan said Tokyo planned a comprehensive package of measures that would stimulate domestic demand and help to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  While he did not elaborate on measures to boost domestic demand, he said one option was to use the yen's strength to invest in natural resources overseas.\par \par \uc2  "I think it is necessary to combine economic policy and monetary policies that will be conducive to ... (a yen exchange rate) slightly lower than the current level," he added.\par \par \uc2  Kan has instructed his cabinet to compile an extra budget for the current fiscal year to March, although the size of spending will likely be too small to significantly boost the economy.\par \par \uc2  Unsterilised \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are a departure from the usual \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  practice of absorbing the extra funds through issuance of government bills, effectively making \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  a part of a monetary loosening mix.  ($1=84.88 Yen)  (Additional reporting by Masayuki Kitano, Yoko Nishikawa, Kaori Kaneko and Rie Ishiguro in Tokyo, Karolina Tagaris in London; Editing by Tomasz Janowski and Edmund Klamann)     \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/ (WRAPUP 2)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument 