\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Nerves Build As Yen Rises Again\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Martin  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 511 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2010\par 13:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Traders are bracing for a second burst of yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week, as the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves higher and the risk of a downbeat Federal Reserve statement overnight threatens to push the dollar markedly lower.\par \par \uc2 As traders seek to test the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's nerve, the yen has started to creep back up for the first time since the Bank of Japan blasted it lower last week.\par \par \uc2 Many market watchers believe that if this continues, the BOJ is likely to jump back in, in order to convince traders and investors that its efforts to hold the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down are serious.\par \par \uc2 "You can't rule it out. The BOJ has to be very aggressive," said Paul Mackel, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 HSBC\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ, acting on the behalf of Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , launched a super-sized zap on the sky-high yen for the first time in six years on Sept. 15, selling Y2 trillion ($24 billion) to push the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  down. The effects of that move lasted for almost a week, keeping the dollar close to Y86, from just under Y83 immediately before the BOJ took action.\par \par \uc2 That offers some support to exporters and plays a role in limiting deflation--a spiral of falling prices that is often exacerbated when a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  makes imports cheaper. The Japanese authorities have made it clear that they are ready to repeat the move if they see new strength in the yen.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday, the yen popped higher for the first time since that BOJ sting, in a small but psychologically significant move. The dollar dropped from the day's high of Y85.77 to a low of Y85.26 before regaining some ground, and analysts said the market was testing the BOJ's willingness to act.\par \par \uc2 If the yen moves much higher--a sure bet if the Federal Reserve dents the dollar with a surprisingly downbeat message on the U.S. economy later Tuesday--then analysts believe a renewed run of official yen selling could ensue.\par \par \uc2 To be sure, the consensus among economists is that the Fed will stand pat, rather than pushing ahead with fresh bond purchases to support the economy--a step that would almost certainly whack the buck. Still, given the scale of the BOJ's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week, few are willing to ignore the chance of a repeat move.\par \par \uc2 "I think if the dollar falls under Y85, there's a real risk," said Adam Cole, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at the \uc2 Royal Bank of Canada\uc2  in London. "The authorities made a huge effort to get the dollar above Y85, so politically, they can't be seen to fail to hold it above there immediately."\par \par \uc2 At 1045 GMT, the dollar was at Y85.50. The Federal Reserve is due to make its regular statement at 1815 GMT.\par \par \uc2 -By Katie Martin, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9346; katie.martin@dowjones.com [ 21-09-10 1104GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100921e69l000vo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
