\par\par \b \uc2 WSJA(9/22) Japan Real Time Report\b0\par\par\uc2 630 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2010\par 19:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par 4\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (From THE WALL STREET JOURNAL ASIA) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Can't stop the music?\f28 \par \par \uc2 China spat catches SMAP\par \par \uc2 China has been making clear its displeasure with Japan ever since a certain boat collision two weeks ago. But its latest measure takes the tiff to a new level: Next month's Shanghai debut of Japanese boy-turned-man band SMAP has been postponed. As the saying goes, Hell hath no fury like a female pop fan scorned.\par \par \uc2 This follows China's abrupt cancellation Monday of a planned visit to the Shanghai World Expo by 1,000 Japanese youth, as the row over the run-in between a Chinese fishing boat and two Japanese coast-guard vessels in disputed waters intensifies.\par \par \uc2 The website of the Chinese ticketing agency had stopped offering tickets as of early Tuesday, but only later did the news turn truly dire: SMAP's management company announced that the concerts will be rescheduled. Fans have been denied once this year already: The band was set to play the World Expo in June when organizers canceled, citing security concerns.\par \par \uc2 SMAP's dominance in the Japanese entertainment industry is unmatched -- the five men are everywhere, though not usually singing. They host a weekly cooking show, and at any given moment it seems at least one can be seen starring in a TV drama or variety program.\par \par \uc2 Or, failing that, a TV ad; until recently, SMAP heartthrob Takuya Kimura held the celebrity record for appearances in commercials.\par \par \uc2 -- Yoree Koh\par \par \uc2 Japan's workers: There's\par \par \uc2 no place like home\par \par \uc2 According to a survey released Thursday, two-thirds of Japan's white-collar workers don't want to work abroad . . . ever.\par \par \uc2 The younger and more junior the employee, the greater the resistance: Among managing directors -- usually in their 40s and 50s -- the no-go contingent is in the (large) minority, at 43%, but among nonmanagers it's a 71% supermajority. The survey of 400 people was conducted by the Sanno Institute of Management, a graduate school in Tokyo.\par \par \uc2 An official at one of Japan's leading banks recently confided that it's impossible to get young employees to go abroad, even for bank-paid study.\par \par \uc2 "They feel like they'll fall behind their peers if they go overseas. It's so stupid," grumbled the official, who came from a generation whose best and brightest were dispatched to top U.S. universities to learn English and broaden their horizons. (The top reasons given by survey respondents were less competition-driven: a lack of English and fears the overseas environment may not be safe.)\par \par \uc2 Companies like Rakuten and \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  are mandating that English be spoken at meetings. But their push to globalize may fall short if employees refuse to set foot outside \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 -- Mariko Sanchanta\par \par \uc2 Mr. Shirakawa, will yen\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  work?\par \par \uc2 After the dramatic announcement a week ago that yen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had begun, Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governor Masaaki Shirakawa issued a robust statement of confidence: "The Bank of Japan strongly expects that the action taken by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market will contribute to a stable \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate formation."\par \par \uc2 Analysts and economists are more skeptical. Japanese officials said the country was acting on its own, and expert opinion is dubious about unilateral efforts. As one respected Kyoto University economist put it in his 2008 book, "Modern Monetary Policy in Theory and Practice": "When the countries concerned make a coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  . . . there are cases in which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is effective." Otherwise, expectations should be low -- and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's case is conspicuously otherwise.\par \par \uc2 The economist's name? Masaaki Shirakawa. He became governor of the Bank of Japan in April 2008.\par \par \uc2 -- Jacob M. Schlesinger and Yuka Hayashi\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 September 21, 2010 13:52 ET (17:52 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100921e69l0047u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
