months and will be modest near term, adding that underlying inflation is below levels consistent with maximum employment and price stability. FOMC reiterated conditions warrant exceptionally low rates for "extended period" and that it's "prepared to provide additional accommodation if needed". Intel, \uc2 Hewlett Packard\uc2  and Caterpillar supported the DJIA, with gains of 1.1%-2.2%. But S&P 500 Financials fell 1.0%, with KBW Bank index down 1.4%. S&P 500 Materials fell 0.4%, with \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  down 1.9%, Vale up 1.6%, Freeport down 0.4%, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  down 1.0%, Barrick Gold up 0.8%. BHP ADRs at A$38.90 (based on AUD/USD at 0.9448) vs local close at A$38.72. London listed BHP down 1.1%, Rio (RIO.AU) down 0.9%. Spot gold at US$1,286.70 (set record high of US$1,290.54) vs US$1,278.40. Nymex crude oil fell 1.8% to US$73.52. LME copper down 0.4%, zinc down 1.8%, aluminum down 1.0%, nickel down 3.0%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR/USD at 1.3068 vs 1.3062. AUD/USD at 0.9448 vs 0.9459. S&P/ASX 200 last 4617.5. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2303 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei likely to fall as firmer yen (USD/JPY now 85.10) impacts select exporters after U.S. Fed hinted it could take additional measures to stimulate economic growth. "The focus remains on how long the impact of last week's (\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  government yen \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will last," says Hisatsune Kobayashi, senior market analyst at Nikko Cordial Securities. "It's hard for the market to be positive when (USD/JPY) 84 is in sight so profit-taking may set in." Longer-term, index says may still fall below 9000 if USD/JPY drops below 83. Puts today's Nikkei range at 9500-9600 vs 9602.11 (down 0.3%) yesterday close. Adds heavy selling unlikely for now as investors remain optimistic after Fed confirmed it will take steps to prop up U.S. economy if conditions deteriorate. Nikkei 225 December futures ended Chicago trading at 9525 vs Osaka close at 9530. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Malaysia August CPI likely +2.0% on year vs +1.9% in July, according to median forecast in Dow Jones poll of 11 economists. Standard Chartered economist Alvin Liew says inflation likely edged up, driven by higher food, transport costs; "inflation remains positive but very benign." Affin Securities economist Alan Tan says inflation likely to continue to slope higher over next few months but "very low" CPI in 1H to keep full year CPI at about +2%. While \uc2 David Cohen\uc2  of Action Economics says "we have reduced our full year CPI forecast to +1.8%." CPI was down 2.4% on year in August 2009. Data due at 0900 GMT. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2259 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ 2Q current account deficit widened to NZ$880 million vs NZ$159 million surplus in 1Q and consensus forecast of NZ$600 million deficit. Stats NZ says deficit due to rise in investment income deficit combined with lower taxes received from foreign investors. "Foreign-owned New Zealand companies earned higher profits this quarter, driving the rise in the income deficit," says Stats NZ International Accounts manager John Morris. Current account deficit now 3.0% of GDP vs 2.4% in 1Q and likely to widen further as foreign owned company profits pick up further. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com) [ 09-21-10 1938ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100921e69l000fd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
