\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:Lead JGB Futures May Rise To 142.80 On FOMC Remark\b0\par\par\uc2 1,559 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2010\par 01:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2337 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead JGB futures may rise to 142.80 vs Tuesday's close at 142.38, after U.S. Treasurys rally following FOMC saying overnight it's ready to ease more if needed, says Katsutoshi Inadome, fixed income strategist at Mitsubishi UFJ Morgan Stanley Securities. Says benchmark 10-year JGB yield may fall to 1.000% vs 1.045% Tuesday. "The dovish contents of the FOMC statement appear not to have been fully factored in, and that alone means there is room for (the yield) to test the psychological threshold at 1.00%."(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2334 GMT [Dow Jones] BOJ will struggle to strengthen USD/JPY if it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  again, says NAB Chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist John Kyriakopolous. "We have argued that should the U.S. restart quantitative easing which will be accompanies by falling short-term term bond yields, the Bank of Japan will face a tough task to prevent a re-weakening in dollar/yen." Pair last 85.05. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2331 GMT [Dow Jones] Gold $1,287.75, up $2.80 early in Asia, after trading up to fresh record high of $1,290.55/oz, following issue of U.S. Federal Reserve's statement. \uc2 Commonwealth Bank of Australia\uc2  says this suggests U.S. interest rates would stay low for an extended period, and Fed ready to resume quantitative easing if necessary. "The relatively weak prognosis of the U.S. economy by the Fed saw the U.S. dollar sold off and good support for gold," CBA says. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2331 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net buy orders for 8.7 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 8th consecutive day of premarket buying may be positive for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Buy orders total 24.1 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 15.4 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2329 GMT [Dow Jones] Parity fever building in Australia, AUD/USD can vault next hurdle at 0.9650, says NAB Chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist John Kyriakopolous. Notes Australia-U.S. two year swap yield widened to 460 bps, highest in 27 months and USD index points to further weakness. "Should the RBA hike rates over coming months and Chinese GDP growth re-accelerate, then a rise to above 0.9650 is on the cards (this is the next resistance level). Add U.S. quantitative easing and a fresh high above 0.9850 is likely, with parity just around the corner." AUD/USD last 0.9545. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2326 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall to 84.80 as FOMC outcome Tuesday sends U.S. Treasury yields down, says senior dealer at major European bank. Says, "There is no reason to buy the dollar if you look at the U.S. Treasury yields"; but adds, further drops in USD/JPY unlikely as market cautious about Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Pair may trade in 84.80-85.50 range for rest of global day vs 85.09 last. EUR/USD biased up; may target 1.3334, high in early August, on broad USD weakness. Pair last 1.3256. EUR/JPY may rise to 113.00 vs last 112.80. But upside likely capped by Japan exporters' sales, trader says.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2318 GMT [Dow Jones] FOMC statement overnight saying Fed "prepared to provide additional accommodation if needed" likely to perpetuate broad-based USD weakness in near-term as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stance likely to keep downward pressure on