\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Foreign Net Buy Orders On 8.7M Japan Stocks\b0\par\par\uc2 1,725 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2010\par 01:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2331 GMT [Dow Jones] Offshore accounts at 10 foreign brokerages place net buy orders for 8.7 million Japanese shares overnight, according to traders; 8th consecutive day of premarket buying may be positive for market at open, although yen value basis of figures unknown. Buy orders total 24.1 million shares, with sell orders amounting to 15.4 million shares. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2329 GMT [Dow Jones] Parity fever building in Australia, AUD/USD can vault next hurdle at 0.9650, says NAB Chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist John Kyriakopolous. Notes Australia-U.S. two year swap yield widened to 460 bps, highest in 27 months and USD index points to further weakness. "Should the RBA hike rates over coming months and Chinese GDP growth re-accelerate, then a rise to above 0.9650 is on the cards (this is the next resistance level). Add U.S. quantitative easing and a fresh high above 0.9850 is likely, with parity just around the corner." AUD/USD last 0.9545. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2326 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may fall to 84.80 as FOMC outcome Tuesday sends U.S. Treasury yields down, says senior dealer at major European bank. Says, "There is no reason to buy the dollar if you look at the U.S. Treasury yields"; but adds, further drops in USD/JPY unlikely as market cautious about Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Pair may trade in 84.80-85.50 range for rest of global day vs 85.09 last. EUR/USD biased up; may target 1.3334, high in early August, on broad USD weakness. Pair last 1.3256. EUR/JPY may rise to 113.00 vs last 112.80. But upside likely capped by Japan exporters' sales, trader says.(kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2318 GMT [Dow Jones] FOMC statement overnight saying Fed "prepared to provide additional accommodation if needed" likely to perpetuate broad-based USD weakness in near-term as \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's stance likely to keep downward pressure on USD-interest rates. But despite renewed USD weakness, USD/JPY falls should be limited, due to concern Tokyo may soon again conduct USD-buying, JPY-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Hiroshi Maeba, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at Nomura Securities. "After the FOMC, the general trend of dollar weakness will persist, but any falls against the yen will be constrained by caution over \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Maeba says. "The Japanese government's stance that it will continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  helped buoy the dollar up above Y85.00 after it briefly broke that level last night after the FOMC, and should continue to curb any sharp falls." USD/JPY last 85.13.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2313 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 offshore leads slightly bearish, with overnight SPI 200 futures down 0.2% at 4640 as Wall Street gave up intraday gains. DJIA up 0.1% at 10761.03, S&P 500 down 0.3% at 1139.78, Nasdaq down 0.3% at 2349.35. Ireland sold the maximum intended amount of EUR1.5 billion worth of government bonds, although yields rose sharply, reflecting political and economic uncertainty. U.S. August housing starts rose 10.5% to 598,000 vs expected 0.2% fall to 545,000. FOMC said economic recovery slowed in recent months and will be modest near term, adding that underlying inflation is below levels consistent with maximum employment and price stability. FOMC reiterated conditions warrant exceptionally low rates for "extended period" and that it's "prepared to provide additional accommodation if needed". Intel, \uc2 Hewlett Packard\uc2  and Caterpillar supported the DJIA, with gains of 1.1%-2.2%. But S&P 500 Financials fell 1.0%, with KBW Bank index down 1.4%. S&P 500 Materials fell 0.4%, with \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  down 1.9%, Vale up 1.6%, Freeport down 0.4%, \uc2 U.S. Steel\uc2  down 1.0%, Barrick Gold up 0.8%. BHP ADRs at A$38.90 (based on AUD/USD at 0.9448) vs local close at A$38.72. London listed BHP down 1.1%, Rio (RIO.AU) down 0.9%. Spot gold at US$1,286.70 (set record high of US$1,290.54) vs US$1,278.40. Nymex crude oil fell 1.8% to US$73.52. LME copper down 0.4%, zinc down 1.8%, aluminum down 1.0%, nickel down 3.0%. In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, EUR/USD at 1.3068 vs 1.3062. AUD/USD at 0.9448 vs 0.9459. S&P/ASX 200 last 4617.5. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2303 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei likely to fall as firmer yen (USD/JPY now 85.10) impacts select exporters after U.S. Fed hinted it could take additional measures to stimulate economic growth. "The focus remains on how long the impact of last week's (\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  government yen \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market) \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will last," says Hisatsune Kobayashi, senior market analyst at Nikko Cordial Securities. "It's hard for the market to be positive when (USD/JPY) 84 is in sight so profit-taking may set in." Longer-term, index says may still fall below 9000 if USD/JPY drops below 83. Puts today's Nikkei range at 9500-9600 vs 9602.11 (down 0.3%) yesterday close. Adds heavy selling unlikely for now as investors remain optimistic after Fed confirmed it will take steps to prop up U.S. economy if conditions deteriorate. Nikkei 225 December futures ended Chicago trading at 9525 vs Osaka close at 9530. (kana.inagaki@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2300 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Malaysia August CPI likely +2.0% on year vs +1.9% in July, according to median forecast in Dow Jones poll of 11 economists. Standard Chartered economist Alvin Liew says inflation likely edged up, driven by higher food, transport costs; "inflation remains positive but very benign." Affin Securities economist Alan Tan says inflation likely to continue to slope higher over next few months but "very low" CPI in 1H to keep full year CPI at about +2%. While David Cohen of Action Economics says "we have reduced our full year CPI forecast to +1.8%." CPI was down 2.4% on year in August 2009. Data due at 0900 GMT. (ankur.relia@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2259 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ 2Q current account deficit widened to NZ$880 million vs NZ$159 million surplus in 1Q and consensus forecast of NZ$600 million deficit. Stats NZ says deficit due to rise in investment income deficit combined with lower taxes received from foreign investors. "Foreign-owned New Zealand companies earned higher profits this quarter, driving the rise in the income deficit," says Stats NZ International Accounts manager John Morris. Current account deficit now 3.0% of GDP vs 2.4% in 1Q and likely to widen further as foreign owned company profits pick up further. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2242 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD can push higher given wider bullish sentiment, could break July 2008 high of 0.9850 during 4Q, says \uc2 Westpac\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Sean Callow. Notes however volumes to date have been quite thin, suggests lot of money waiting on sidelines to bet on pair. Hawkish RBA, dovish Fed, robust domestic growth all suggest higher AUD. "We do see it higher on a multi-week basis." AUD/USD last 0.9533. Risks to this outlook stem from Europe's sovereign debt crisis. "We're due for some bad news, it would produce a pretty quick move because the volumes haven't been large."(enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2240 GMT [Dow Jones] Australian wheat prices face downward pressure today from lead from U.S., where wheat prices fell overnight, with soft red winter wheat for December delivery on CBOT falling 13.75 cents or 1.9% at US$7.18/bushel. U.S. wheat prices fell for a second consecutive day overnight as threats to world production appear to be already priced into markets, analysts say. Meanwhile, AUD strengthens further, last 0.9531, adding to downward pressure on Australian wheat. Yesterday, ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in New South Wales fell A$8.50 to settle at A$279.00/metric ton; ASX milling wheat futures deliverable January in Western Australia lost A$10 at A$307.00. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2237 GMT [Dow Jones] FOMC statement "very interesting," says Ian Shepherdson, chief U.S. economist at \uc2 High Frequency Economics\uc2  in a note. Says Fed now explicitly seeking higher inflation rate, notes Fed comment that it's "prepared to provide additional accommodation if needed... to return inflation, over time, to levels consistent with its mandate." Shepherdson says: "Wow. This is a big change, and we are inclined to interpret this shift as setting the groundwork for QE (quantitative easing) part 2. Mr. Bernanke he still faces stiff opposition from Kansas city president Hoenig, who today opposed reinvesting maturing principal, never mind buying net new assets, but it looks to us like the Chairman is preparing to take on the hawks. Good." (colin.ng@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2233 GMT [Dow Jones] Though August election may have pushed back time table for market watchdog ASIC to unveil new market rules, re-elected Labor government remains committed to opening up exchange trading to competition, ending ASX Ltd.'s (ASX.AU) near monopoly, once rules are in place. A spokesman for Treasurer Wayne Swan--who is now set to make final decision after former corporate law minister Chris Bowen's portfolio switch to immigration--confirms government is still committed to granting rival Chi-X a license; "the government has stated its support for competition between markets for trading in listed shares in Australia, announcing in-principle approval of Chi-X Australia Pty Ltd.'s Australian market licence application in March 2010," the spokesman says. "This is a vital step in the development of Australia as a financial services hub." Further steps to be taken before final decision include finalizing regulatory framework, including amending Corporations Regulations 2001 and settling of ASIC-set market integrity rules. Chi-X now targeting a March 2011 launch date, was October 2010. (rachel.pannett@dowjones.com) [ 09-21-10 1930ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100921e69l000f9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
