\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Summary Of US Stocks\b0\par\par\uc2 1,501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 September 2010\par 00:13\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 2213 GMT [Dow Jones] WALL STREET: Blue-chip stocks finished mixed after Fed hinted it could take additional measures to stimulate economic growth. Caterpillar +2.2%, \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  +1.4%, Intel +1.1%. Fed concluded policy meeting without announcing additional steps to boost recovery but officials signaled uncomfortable with recent low levels of inflation, said prepared to take additional action to boost economic recovery. "It looks like the Fed is keeping its foot on the economic gas pedal but hasn't yet pushed the turbo button," says Burt White, chief investment officer at LPL Financial. "They definitely showed a lot of restraint and frankly confidence that the economy is sort of plowing through a soft spot and not headed toward a double dip." \uc2 ConAgra Foods\uc2  fell 3.6% after packaged-food company said fiscal 1Q earnings fell 12%, hurt by intense price competition, rising costs. \uc2 Nvidia\uc2  +5.4% as chip maker unveiled plans for advancements of graphics chips over next 3 years as faces renewed competition. \uc2 Edwards Lifesciences\uc2  jumped 9% as investors got more hopeful for medical-company's release of data from U.S. study on highly anticipated replacement heart valve that doesn't require open-heart surgery. DJIA +0.1%, Nasdaq fell 0.3%, Philly semicons down 0.3%. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2209 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. SUMMARY: USD stumbled after Fed said it stood ready to kick-start slowing U.S. economy. EUR rose sharply, racking up gains of more than 1.5% vs USD, while USD/JPY briefly dips below 85; "What we see is the door being kept open to further quantitative easing," says Michael Woolfolk, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNY Mellon in New York. Fed statement also seemed to confirm--at least for now--that double-dip recession is "off the table," Woolfolk says, allowing investors to leave behind worst-case scenario in favor of taking risks on higher-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as AUD/USD, which soared to 2-year high at 0.9566 from 0.9473 late Monday. EUR/USD at 1.3247 late Tuesday from 1.3061 late Monday, USD/JPY at 85.12 vs 85.75, EUR/JPY at 112.75 vs 112.02, GBP/USD at 1.5627 vs 1.5552, USD/CHF at 0.9976 vs 1.0052. USD Index at 80.442 from 81.324. Stocks mixed after Fed hinted could take additional measures to stimulate economic growth; DJIA +0.1%, Nasdaq fell 0.3%, Philly semicons down 0.3%. Treasurys rallied as expectations grew Fed will eventually launch another large-scale bond buying program to help economy; 2-year yield down 3.2 bps at 0.432%, 10-year yield down 10.8 bps at 2.596%. Crude futures settled lower, weighed by concerns about sluggish economic recovery; October Nymex crude settled $1.34 lower at $73.52. December Comex gold rose $6.90 to $1,287.70. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2205 GMT [Dow Jones] Risk seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  buyers will soon fade away as market will need U.S. Fed to actually implement QE if gains in AUD/USD, EUR/USD are to be sustainable, say \uc2 UBS\uc2  strategists. "Risk seeking sentiment in \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  seen post the FOMC statement may prove temporary as market participants will likely need to have the Fed actually announce further quantitative easing in order for risk seeking to be more sustainable." Say \uc2 UBS\uc2  core view is inflation has bottomed, QE won't be necessary. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2200 GMT [Dow Jones] NZ shares likely to open flat given mixed leads offshore; "we are expecting a relatively quiet opening after a strong day yesterday," says First NZ Capital's Philip Hunter. NZX-50 ended up 0.6% at 3236.76 yesterday. Investors to digest FOMC statement, also watching for Pumpkin Patch (PPL.NZ); "the market is focused on trying to get a feel for how the consumer is faring so every retail announcement is watched carefully." (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2152 GMT [Dow Jones] AUD/USD set to drift in what's expected to be very quiet session ahead, likely pair will give up some gains along with EUR/USD, says Commonwealth Bank Strategist Joseph Capurso. Says main focus will be whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on JPY. EUR/USD last 1.3255. AUD/USD last 0.9537. Says little to guide either near term after big moves post-Fed. AUD/USD now eyeing July 2008 high of 0.9850. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2150 GMT [Dow Jones] Big watch for session ahead will be whether BOJ/MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken JPY but it's likely they won't move unless USD/JPY moves sharply lower and well below 85.00 region, says CBA \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Joseph Capurso. "I suspect they won't unless it starts falling very sharply." Pair last 85.14. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2127 GMT [Dow Jones]NZ government bonds opened slightly higher, while interest rate swaps lower with prevailing bid tone, says local bond trader. Adds U.S. Treasurys well supported after FOMC announcement, with curve lower in yield, as Fed says prepared to lend more support to economy if necessary; 2-year swap rates down 2 bps at 3.79%, 10-year swap rates down 2 bps at 4.97%, November 2011 bond yields down 2.5 bps at 3.61%, May 2021 bond yields down 4.5 bps at 5.245%. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2011 GMT [Dow Jones] New Basel 3 bank capital requirements, once fully implemented, could increase a bank's cost of providing a liquidity facility to a municipal issuer by about 1 percentage point, says Chris Mauro, munibond research director, at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 . This probably would increase public sector issuers' cost of capital and could lead to more banks pulling away from the muni credit backstop market, he said. This month, the Basel Committee on Banking Supervision said a 4.5% capital level must be in place by 2015 with an additional 2.5% by 2019. (stan.rosenberg@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2010 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD easing back after gaining sharply on Fed statement, pair last 0.7323 after flirting with 0.7350, says BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Mike Jones; "I think, despite all the initial excitement, the market has come round to the idea that nothing has really changed for the Fed and there wasn't really anything new in that statement that we didn't already know." On day, says investors watching for balance of payments data, won't have much impact unless significantly different than consensus; median in Dow Jones Newswires poll of 13 economists tipping NZ$600 million current account deficit in 2Q. Says unless there is a real surge in Asia equities pair unlikely to break through 0.7350, tips support at around 0.7245 initially. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2010 GMT [Dow Jones] "Hopes that the FOMC would today announce more asset purchases always looked fanciful," says Paul Dales at Capital Economics. "Those officials who expressed their reservations last month were always unlikely to support an actual expansion of that balance sheet this month, especially when the economic data has improved," he notes. Even so, "we doubt more asset purchases would boost activity by much," Dales says. They might lower longer-term rates, but rates aren't the problem; it's "a need to deleverage and a chronic lack of confidence" keeping households and businesses from borrowing and spending more, he adds, something the Fed can't alter. (john.shipman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2009 GMT [Dow Jones] US stocks little changed, after the Fed again dances around the question of whether it will throw yet another net under the economy. DJIA adds 8 to 10761, but S&P 500 slips 3 to 1140, Nasdaq Comp eases 6 to 2349. Stocks did rally, but gave it back by the close. Treasurys and gold stick their rallies, while the dollar sells off. The Fed says it's prepared to "provide additional accommodation," although it didn't signal the start of any new bond-buying programs. But it did say, in it usual circuitous way, that inflation's too low, and if it goes much lower, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will step in. (paul.vigna@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2002 GMT [Dow Jones] Canadian bonds ended higher, advancing along with Treasurys after the Federal Reserve's policy statement pointed clearly to the possibility of further monetary easing. The front end of the market was also supported by news in earlier trading that inflation in Canada was well contained in Aug. Canada's two-year bond yield was at 1.444% vs 1.495% late Mon. The 10-year bond yielded 2.907% vs 2.935%. On Wed, Canadian retail sales data for July will be released. (don.curren@dowjones.com) [ 09-21-10 1813ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100921e69l000eo\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
