\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: MOF Will Only  \highlight22\uc2 Intervene \highlight\uc2  On Sharp Fall In USD/JPY -CBA\b0\par\par\uc2 1,267 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2010\par 23:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 2150 GMT [Dow Jones] Big watch for session ahead will be whether BOJ/MOF \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervenes\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to weaken JPY but it's likely they won't move unless USD/JPY moves sharply lower and well below 85.00 region, says CBA \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Strategist Joseph Capurso. "I suspect they won't unless it starts falling very sharply." Pair last 85.14. (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 2127 GMT [Dow Jones]NZ government bonds opened slightly higher, while interest rate swaps lower with prevailing bid tone, says local bond trader. Adds U.S. Treasurys well supported after FOMC announcement, with curve lower in yield, as Fed says prepared to lend more support to economy if necessary; 2-year swap rates down 2 bps at 3.79%, 10-year swap rates down 2 bps at 4.97%, November 2011 bond yields down 2.5 bps at 3.61%, May 2021 bond yields down 4.5 bps at 5.245%. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2011 GMT [Dow Jones] New Basel 3 bank capital requirements, once fully implemented, could increase a bank's cost of providing a liquidity facility to a municipal issuer by about 1 percentage point, says Chris Mauro, munibond research director, at \uc2 RBC Capital Markets\uc2 . This probably would increase public sector issuers' cost of capital and could lead to more banks pulling away from the muni credit backstop market, he said. This month, the Basel Committee on Banking Supervision said a 4.5% capital level must be in place by 2015 with an additional 2.5% by 2019. (stan.rosenberg@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2010 GMT [Dow Jones] NZD/USD easing back after gaining sharply on Fed statement, pair last 0.7323 after flirting with 0.7350, says BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Strategist Mike Jones; "I think, despite all the initial excitement, the market has come round to the idea that nothing has really changed for the Fed and there wasn't really anything new in that statement that we didn't already know." On day, says investors watching for balance of payments data, won't have much impact unless significantly different than consensus; median in Dow Jones Newswires poll of 13 economists tipping NZ$600 million current account deficit in 2Q. Says unless there is a real surge in Asia equities pair unlikely to break through 0.7350, tips support at around 0.7245 initially. (rebecca.howard@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2010 GMT [Dow Jones] "Hopes that the FOMC would today announce more asset purchases always looked fanciful," says Paul Dales at Capital Economics. "Those officials who expressed their reservations last month were always unlikely to support an actual expansion of that balance sheet this month, especially when the economic data has improved," he notes. Even so, "we doubt more asset purchases would boost activity by much," Dales says. They might lower longer-term rates, but rates aren't the problem; it's "a need to deleverage and a chronic lack of confidence" keeping households and businesses from borrowing and spending more, he adds, something the Fed can't alter. (john.shipman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2009 GMT [Dow Jones] US stocks little changed, after the Fed again dances around the question of whether it will throw yet another net under the economy. DJIA adds 8 to 10761, but S&P 500 slips 3 to 1140, Nasdaq Comp eases 6 to 2349. Stocks did rally, but gave it back by the close. Treasurys and gold stick their rallies, while the dollar sells off. The Fed says it's prepared to "provide additional accommodation," although it didn't signal the start of any new bond-buying programs. But it did say, in it usual circuitous way, that inflation's too low, and if it goes much lower, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will step in. (paul.vigna@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 2002 GMT [Dow Jones] Canadian bonds ended higher, advancing along with Treasurys after the Federal Reserve's policy statement pointed clearly to the possibility of further monetary easing. The front end of the market was also supported by news in earlier trading that inflation in Canada was well contained in Aug. Canada's two-year bond yield was at 1.444% vs 1.495% late Mon. The 10-year bond yielded 2.907% vs 2.935%. On Wed, Canadian retail sales data for July will be released. (don.curren@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1953 GMT [Dow Jones] Just because no new Bank of Japan hand hasn't been seen to prop up the USD/JPY, doesn't mean it's not coming soon, says Hidetoshi Yanagihara of \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2 . "I haven't seen any evidence of it so far," he says. But it definitely could be coming with so much strong sentiment being unleashed against US dollar after the dovish Fed statement, including with USD/JPY pair. Pair now at 85.10 after briefly breaching 85, according to EBS via CQG. "Probably in Tokyo time you could see a move by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but nobody knows for sure," he says, about further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 (andrewj.johnson@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1951 GMT [Dow Jones] The CDX North America High Yield index, tracking credit derivatives on junk bonds, strengthened after the Fed statement this afternoon. "Reaction in the U.S. high yield market was cautiously firm as investors seemed satisfied that the Fed was sufficiently cognizant of the slowdown in the economic recovery," said Otis Casey, a credit analyst at index administrator Markit. "The rally in U.S. Treasury prices was due to indications that the Fed would continue to be a buyer in the market rather than a flight to quality investments." As of 3.40pm EDT the CDX HY was trading at 98.37, up from 98.28 before the Fed statement. Monday's closing level was 98.63.(katy.burne@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 1940 GMT [Dow Jones] Indications of interest in an $817M Build America Bond issue of the Texas Transportation Commission are being taken at various spreads to Tsys, with the longest 2040 maturity reaching 100 bps over the 4 3/8s of May 15, 2040, lead underwriter JPM said. The same group set retail prices for $160M of the highway agency's tax-exempt GOs at levels reaching 2.23% for 2018 bonds carrying 4% and 5% coupons.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par (stan.rosenberg@dowjones.com) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1939 GMT [Dow Jones] Fed funds futures traders less and less certain that Fed will move to tighten rates in the next year, after FOMC again outlines a slowing pace of recovery in output and employment over recent months. October 2011-dated fed funds futures price 22% likelihood that FOMC will move to tighten fed funds rate at late September meeting next year, down from a 36% chance seen by contract at Monday's close, and 42% at the end of last week. (jacob.bunge@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 1939 GMT [Dow Jones] Eurodollar futures close with price gains Tuesday on outlook from FOMC, seen by market participants as opening the door to further quantitative easing efforts to keep credit flowing and hold rates low. Front-month Dec Eurodollar contracts settle 3 BPs higher at 99.635, reflecting market expectation for Libor to rise to 0.365%, down from an anticipated 0.395% priced in by the contract at Monday's close. Quarterly 2011 contracts gain 5 to 8.5 basis points, indicating that traders see a slower rise in inter-bank lending rates over the coming year. (jacob.bunge@dowjones.com) [ 09-21-10 1750ET ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100921e69l000ef\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict