\par\par \b \uc2 WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Euro Gains On Well-Received Auctions; Fed Awaited\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bradley Davis   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 755 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 September 2010\par 18:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The euro gained against the dollar Tuesday after several successful auctions of euro-zone government debt helped allay concerns over the region's festering fiscal issues.\par \par \uc2 After posting its gains on the heels of the well-received auctions, the euro was unable to break to a new one-month high, with most major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  treading water in North American trading ahead of the afternoon meeting of the Federal Reserve.\par \par \uc2 The Fed's rate-setting committee is not expected to change key rates, but speculation has swirled that the Federal Open Market Committee could hint at a new round of economy-stimulating asset-purchase programs, known as quantitative easing, in the wake of a slowing U.S. economy.\par \par \uc2 Better-than-expected data for August housing starts and building permits points to an economy that might not be slowing drastically enough to warrant immediate Fed action, said Joe Manimbo, market analyst at Travelex Global Business Payments in Washington.\par \par \uc2 "Perhaps if the Fed is looking for one more reason not to provide further stimulus measures over the balance of the year, this would be that type of data," that would lead to a rosier assessment of the economy, boding well for the dollar, which has been weighed down by talk of further stimulus, Manimbo said.\par \par \uc2 Tuesday afternoon, the euro was at $1.3135 from $1.3061 late Monday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y85.40 from Y85.75, while the euro was at Y112.17 from Y112.02. The U.K. pound was at $1.5551 from $1.5552. The dollar was at CHF1.0039 from CHF1.0052.\par \par \uc2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the dollar against a trade-weighted basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was at 81.018 from 81.324.\par \par \uc2 Successful auctions of government debt in Ireland, Spain and Greece helped the euro climb higher against the dollar, but its upside potential has been capped, analysts said, as investors are squarely zeroed in on the Fed's 2:15 p.m. EDT meeting.\par \par \uc2 If the FOMC hints in its statement--or actually announces--a fresh round of stimulus, the dollar is likely to weaken, analysts said, as investors would be more likely to believe key rates would remain ultralow for an even longer period. Investors use cheap dollars to fund bets on higher-yielding assets.\par \par \uc2 "If no further quantitative easing is announced, the U.S. dollar should be supported as it has been dampened by expectations of further quantitative easing in recent weeks," said Geoffrey Yu, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 UBS\uc2  in London.\par \par \uc2 Contrasting with worries over slowing U.S. growth, the Australian dollar hit a fresh two-year high against the U.S. dollar Tuesday, as the robust performance of the Australian economy pointed to future increases in key interest rates.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar ticked above $0.9500, to a high of $0.9503, for the first time since late July 2008, according to CQG. In afternoon trading, the Australian dollar had pulled back to $0.9478 from $0.9473 late Monday.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  is on track to raise interest rates soon, saying Tuesday if its current central scenario for the economy eventuates, the monetary policy brakes will need to be tapped again.\par \par \uc2 After the Fed's Tuesday meeting, investors will also be on guard for signs of Japan in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets after the country's massive Y2 trillion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  last week to temper recent yen strength. If the dollar falls below Y85, the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  again to prop the greenback, said Hidetoshi Yanagihara, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\uc2  in New York.\par \par \uc2 Separately, China's yuan rose for the ninth straight session Tuesday to another record high against the U.S. dollar under the current system, driven by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  setting the dollar-yuan central parity rate below 6.7000 for the first time.\par \par \uc2 The yuan's recent strength comes as Chinese Premier Wen Jiabao visits the U.S. this week to attend a United Nations and meet with U.S. President Barack Obama. Obama on Monday said China hadn't done enough to let the yuan rise, echoing comments made last week by Treasury Secretary Timothy Geithner.\par \par \uc2 -By Bradley Davis, Dow Jones Newswires; 212-416-2654; bradley.davis@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (Joy C. Shaw in Shanghai contributed to this article.) [ 09-21-10 1209ET ]\par \par \uc2 1033 1074 1158 1863 1865 20714 28038 4164   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100921e69l000b7\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
